      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The Ex-Queen Isabella of Spain.—There was much that wai romantic and tragic in the life of the Ex-Queen Isabella of Spain, whose death has occurred at Paris, where she had mostly resided since her abdica- tion in 1868. Born in 1830, she was eleven years younger than our own Queen Victoria,but from the first ' her lot in life was cast in stormy. places. Her birth was the cause of the Carlist uprising, which desolated ' Spain with two wars, and her marriage, when she was I sixteen, almost embroiled Europe in a general war. Her father Ferdinand VII., despairing of a son, had set aside the Salic Law, thus securing the succession to his daughter. But the King's brother Don Carlos did ' not acquiesce in thisarraneement, and upon Ferdinand's death in 1833, at once raised the standard of rebellion. Thus strife and violence reigned from the early child- 1 hood of the ex-Queen, nor was her marriage a happy one. Finally, when the revolution broke out in 1868, t she ffed to Paris, and shortly afterwards abdicated in ! favour of her son Alfonso XIT, the father of the present King of Spain. Miss Cob be's First Book.—Many a disappointed author must hate envied the success of the late Miss Frances Power Cobbe, who wrote her first book on the subject of "The Theory of Intuitive Morals," and although the writer was quite unknown to fame, and the title scarcely a "popular" one, yet found in Mr. Longman a publisher who was willing to produce the work. We are not told whether the venture resulted in a profit, but at any rate Miss Cobbe's subsequent work amply justified the favourable opinion which Mr. Longman formed with regard to her abilities. It was, by the way, this lady who made the interesting discovery that Byron's famous verses "Fare thee well! and if for ever," were -written on the back of a butcher's bill—unpaid, like most of the rest. Bags or plates for the Collection.—The remarks of the Dean of Norwich on "Shabby Churchmen" have led to a revival of the question of the merits of bags and plates for the collection of the offertory. Of course one can understand and admire the high motive of thosel incumbents and wardens who prefer bags, believing that plates induce people to give "grudgingly and of necessity," but there is something to be said on the other side. A well known vicar thus referred to his experience. "I confess I don't like plates, and we have tried both ; but we found that when bags were used the offertory was one third less, and, seeing that the church is without endowment, we cannot afford to give up so large a portion of the "income." It is said that when a certain judge goes to church he provides half a sovereign, and a sixpence, one of which is to be placed in a plate or a bag, whichever is presented,and it would not be difficult to name some churches with "fashion- able "congregations where the bag is found to contain alarge proportion of coppers. The Wedding Present Tax.—The Society woman who was heard to remark that every moment of her avail- able time this month would be spent in selecting wedding presents, does not stand alone in this particu- lar grievance. The wedding present tax has probably never been more severely felt than during these four weeks of April, when one wedding is following quickly upon the heels of another at those churches most favoured by Society folk. In some cases so little time has been allowed that one wedding party has not left by the time the next on the list has arrived, when the confusion has been considerable, both parties getting hopelessly mixed up. But to return to the question of presents. The man,who,upon a recent occasion selected a curious, antique door knocker, is to be envied, for he was at least original in his choice, but though we can- not all hope to alight upon a gift that has not a dupli- cate in the whole collection, we can at least give useful presents. Furniture is always acceptable, and this Spires largely in the fashionable wedding present list now-a-days, while the idea of several people combining and presenting some really handsome item is happily fast raining ground. Jewels to Match the Dress.—Only that jewellery which matches the color of the toilette is now worn by the well-dressed woman, but if one has not sufficient assortment of turquoises sapphires, emeralds and rubies from which to choose for this purpose. it is always possible to avoid glaring contrasts. Earrings both small and large are being worn, and some fashionable women have been seen wearing odd earrings,such as a black and white pearl. A new and pretty design for earrings is a cluster of various coloured gems, skilfully arranged to represent a basket of flowers. Parasols and En-Taut-Cas.—En-tout-cas and parasols make a brave show in the shops, and though at present it seems safer to sally forth with the useful unorna- mental umbrella, a purchase for these accessories— optimistic enough to look forward to a possible use for them—is occasionally found. With regard to handles, beautifully carved ivory, crystal and enamel specimens, set with gems, are in greatest favour, and little in the way of freaks" is seen. When knobs are indulged in these are usually found to be useful as well as orna-</p>
        <p>mental. Some contain a powder puff, others are fitted as a purse, while others again hold matches, and tiny cigarettes. The covering of the en-tout-cas should match the toilette with which it appears, but compara- tively few women can afford to carry out this costly idea when elaborate parasols ai.e concerned. From an economical point of view the black parasol—all chiffon and lace—is the best investment—for it will go with any toilette, and always look well, provided a touch of black appears about the neck and waist, to blend the two into "one harmonious whole." „ . .</p>
        <p>A Delightful Novelty.—The unmade " robe " in Viyella costume cloth, is a delightful innovation, and one like:y to be widely patronised this summer. Like the finer, thinner variety of Viyella, we have so much appreciated for nightdresses, morning wrappers, dress- ing gowns, and dressing jackets, etc., this costume cloth is absolutely unshrinkable, it will wear splendidly, the colourings are exquisite, and last but by no means least the price is moderate indeed. The skirts are all of the most approved shapes, beautifully tucked, corded, strapped, frilled, embroidered, or otherwise adorned, , and the merest amateur will be able to fix them into the waistband, and finish them off.</p>
        <p>The Siring "Earthquake."—Happy are the people who can get away from the annual " earthquake " especially if the painters and decorators are also making chaos of the home. For those who have to "Jive through it" it is the most dreadful time of the wear, and in addition to the worry and wretchedness, many people suffer in health from newly painted surroundings. Those who are susceptible to the action of poison should on no account sleep in bedrooms in which tke painters have only recently been at work. Some are not affected by the odour, but many suffer from headaches and sickness and these people should make every effort to get away whilst house painting is about. There is a common practice of exposing basins of water, or pails of wet straw, or hay, to reduce the smell of the paint, but the effect is very trifling. Milk exposed in shallow vessels for this purpose, has much greater absorbent powers, and for this reason should never be left in newly painted larders or kitchens, when required for domestic use. MADGE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>DAINTY TEA-TABLE FARE.</head>
        <p>DAINTY TEA-TABLE FARE.</p>
        <p>Mothers and daughters could add to the delights of I the tea-table, if they only knew, as thousands of them I do, how easily and quickly the most dainty and tempt- ing little tea scones and light cakes can be made with the help of Brown &amp; Poison's new raising powder called "Paisley Flour." "Paisley Flour" is mixed one part with six to eight parts of ordinary flour, and delicious pan-bread can be baked with it, without the tedious delay :caused by the use of yeast, or the uncertainty of baking powder. "Paisley Flour" is worth atrial by all who bake at home, indeed, it has made many converts to home-baking. Get a 7d. packet from your grocer, and see for yourself. If you are not pleased withit you I can get your money back at Brown &amp; Poison's expense.</p>
        <p>Journalists' Meeting at Warrington. The spring quarterly meeting of the Liverpool Dis- trict Institute of Journalists was held on Saturday at • the Town Hall, Warrington, Mr. G. E. Wigley, of Southport, presiding. There was a representativn attendance. The business embraced questions of organization and the professional examination scheme recently introduced for pupil associates and full mem- bers. The district committee reported the election of one member from Formby, two associates from South- I port, and one pupil associate from Widnes. The visitors were afterwards entertained at tea by the , Mayor of Warrington (Alderman Bolton) in the cosy I tea room of: the Town Hall, and votes of thanks were ! passed for his worship's hospitality, and for. the ' arrangements made by the local members, acknow- ledgments being made by Alderman Bennett on behalf of the Mayor, and by Mr. J. Hawthorn, chairman of the Warrington Sub District. Subsequently, the visit- ' ing delegates viewed with interest the municipal trout hatchery, the Crosfield Conservatory, the public baths and the Warrington Academy.. It is proposed to hold the summer quart€Ay mee ting at Llandudno in Jul ! or August:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>POOR RATES AT _RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>POOR RATES AT _RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Lady writes—</p>
        <p>"Humanly speaking, Benger's Food' entirely saved baby's life. I had tried four we-known foods, but he could digest nothing, until we began the `Beng-,er.' He is now rosy and fattening rapidly.</p>
        <p>Benger's Fsod in Tif7S, of Chemists, Zic., everyW:JOre.</p>
