      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <p>ancYEVPORI McDOUGALL'S Patertt sELF-RAISING FLOUR The Examiner Saturday, April 16 1904.</p>
        <p>(Copyright.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>FASHIONS OF TO-DAL</head>
        <p>FASHIONS OF TO-DAL</p>
        <p>Eastertide falling rather early this year the fashions are more than usually advanced and they are undoubtedly very charming. In- deed there is danger in this profusion of beautiful articles of dress to those of us who are at all impulsive, as one is apt to fall a ready victim to tile fascinations of the all too lovely gowns, hats, and blouses, not to men- tion the hundred and one dainty additions to the toilette, and so be led into undreamt-of extravagances exceeding what we can well spare from our dress allowance. This is in fact a very real difficulty to many women, for the first rush of the spring fashions seem par- ticularly fascinating for the modes are in all their freshness, without losing a fraction of their novelty while they strike one favour-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>;AT COSTUMEJ</head>
        <p>;AT COSTUMEJ</p>
        <p>ably at first, at all events, coming after thei dreariness of winter. It is this contrast oil.; all that is bright and gay with the sombre- ness of the preceding modes that is par- ticularly alluring and prove too seductive) for many lovers of dress. The dainty items Df dress which go to make up the whole • charming effect, though the cost of each is comparatively trifling, there are so many that 'A: the. sum total is apt to prove unpleasantlyl startling when one comes to reckon up the outlay, and an -.p.larmingly large hole is apt to be made in 4116 sum set apart from our quarter's dress allowance. There are so very many dainty collars of lace and linen with silk or chiffon ties to match that seem at the time absolutely necessary that it is easy to spend pounds instead of shillings. Most of the leading firms are displaying early summer costumes and coats, and a par- ticularly neat toilette is given in my first sketch expressed in navy blue cloth of a light canvas-like texture much stitched and trimmed with ornamental buttons. The style is a simple one but very smart, espec- ially when worn together with a modish toque of straw trimmed with twists and bows of fancifully pleated straw with ,a quill</p>
        <p>thrust in an impertinent fashion through the straw a little to one side of the front. The quill should be of an ornamental character such as never sprang from the plumage of any known bird. The ready-made costume business has of late years attained to mar- vellous 'proportions, and this is hardly to be wondered at when one sees really smart and well-made garments turned out in innumera- ble styles and made to suit the requirements of all,</p>
        <p>The pretty blouse depicted is of soft white silk and the deep yoke is made of tucked silk and lace inserted alternately. The yoke, which extends down over the shoulders and forms part of the upper portion of the sleeves, is one of the features of the newest and prettiest blouses. These are always made with high transparent lace collars, boned to keep them in place round the throat. Others, again, are made with a series of flounces or frills falling downwards from the yoke over the figtire, and in some instances being so graduated as to form a point at the waist back and front. I saw a very pretty blouse made in this style of thin pink-flowered muslin over a silk slip. The yoke was of lace and transparent, and the pointed frills fell over a deep waist-band</p>
