      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>SMART TOQUE.</head>
        <p>A SMART TOQUE.</p>
        <p>of swathed silk. The effect was very charm- ing. Another for morning or early after- noon wear was of cream cashmere with a double yoke piped with green, which formed a sort of collar and was apparently held down at intervals by strappings fastened with small gold buttons. Blouses to-day are no longer the ill-fitting garments from which they originated, but require considerable skill and neatness to ensure success.</p>
        <p>The latest shape in toques is shown in the sample sketched. It is made entirely of Roman hyacinths and is very becoming, although the shape is not one which may be exploited by any style of face. Floral toques are always in vogue at this time of year as they seem suited to the freshness of the budding trees and early flowers. This year they are more than ever worn and in many sorts of flowers. Small flowers are, perhaps, the prettiest, such as violets, hyacinths, and buttercups, but I have :-een also some very charming models of shaded roses merging from the palest pink to crim- son, while nasturtiunas are also very natural- looking and most becoming to those people who are dark-haired, as they are best suited to a gypsy style of looks. Yellow and brown in different tones are very fashionable at present, but will soon give place to paler colours. Geraniums are also likely to be much worn, but are apt to be stiff looking if not carefully arranged,</p>
        <p>COQUETTE,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>GHOST HUNT.</head>
        <p>A GHOST HUNT.</p>
        <p>BY C. J. CUTCLIFFE HYNE, Author of "Honour of Thieves," &amp;c., Ste.</p>
        <p>' There were a pair of us apportioned off to the job, and we started with feelings of pleasurable excitement Ghosts are always fascinating to hear about, and the nearest that either of us had got to a ghost up to then had been third-hand, where you only taste a faint outline of the original shivers. So we foregathered from the uttermost ends of these islands, and ‘lunched at a railway station inn, we two bipeds, and Jack, who by persuasion is a wire-haired fox-terrier. We parted for an hour or so to pick up infor- mation and permits, and then met again beside a dog-cart, and started off for a long drive deep into the country. A sense of depression—journalistic depression—weighed upon us heavily. We were to suppress names in any account we might write of this haunted house; we were to keep back all geographical hints whatever which might lead to the identification of the place; and we had to promise this or remain out of the business alto- gether- It seemed there were many people now living down there who were implicated in this matter, and no doubt from their point of view the proviso was understandable enough. • A first sight of the house itself did not tend to cheer us. We had conjured up visions of old monastic architecture, with fish-ponds and under- ground passages and secret rooms. Instead, we found a huge place of yellow brick, which had been added to by various proprietors between the years 1820 and 1885, and would have been glaringly ugly but for a curtain of ivy along a third of one side. Worst of all, it was fitted throughout with electric light! Ye gods! in these days of Röntgen rays had ghosts condescended to "walk" in the neighbourhood of an incandescent lamp? One of us swore. He said he'd been dragged over many a score miles of railway by some stupid practical joker. He said one might as well look for pearls in periwinkles as spooks in a barrack like that. But the other of us pointed out some indisputable facts. Tenant after tenant had refused to stay in the house. Then for a while it remained empty. Recently a returned colonial had rented it on a five-year lease at two hundred pounds per annum. He was a man of iron nerves and modern tastes. He swept and garnished the house from cellar to garret. He it was who had fitted up the electric light plant at a cost of five hundred pounds. Had he been alore he might have stayed, though he confessed that the noises worried him, and he habitually carried a revolver. But no servant would remain in his employ. They came, they saw, they shrieked, they went; some in tears, some in hysterics.; and one, a cook, was carried off to the county asylum, vowing she was hag-ridden. The empty house, as we saw it, carried dumb evidence of that visitation; every door-had three bolts on the inside; but nothing they could do would keep the noises and the ghostly visitants away. And at last, in despair of a cure, the colonial sold his furniture and left. His lease had four years yet to run; he knew for a certainty that no other tenant would take the place off his hands; but he preferred to forfeit the rent rather than remain.</p>
        <p>There certainly was an argument in all this. No man deliberately shifts his abode which he has chosen, and throws away two hundred pounds a year, without a reason which he, at any rate, con- siders valid. _ _ _ .</p>
        <p>We locked the front door from the inside, and set ourselves to make a thorough survey of the place from roof to foundations. The ground floor was uninteresting. There were some twenty empty rooms of various sizes, with a corridor running down the middle that was broken by steps and stairways. On the floor above, the rooms were smaller, and had most of them doors leading from one into the other. These, however, were, in all save four instances, boarded up and covered with papered canvas. The corridor on the tipper floor was visible from end to end, but was barred in the middle by a glazed window, which gave a very curious effect. Thcira was, however, a passage round this. The cellarage promised more *interest. There were regular catacombs of cellars, and we wandered through them with a lantern. We said "rats" repeatedly, and Jack went to look. He hunted about diligently for quite an hour, and then he returned and looked unenthusiastic. He let us know plainly that no rats existed in the ,teighbourhood. There were only frogs about the cellars, and dust and mildew, none of which were at all in Jack's line. So we examined all the outlets to make sure they could not be tampered with, and came up again to the musty daylight of the ground floor, and locked the cellar door behind us and carried off the key. There remained the attics and the leads. The attics were directly under a slate roof and a blazing sun, and the heat in them was stifling. They rambled into all sorts of unexpected corners and shapes; they, many of them, had to be explored on all fours ; and we emerged from them dusty, tired and dispirited. But we could not help seeing that they were the most likely district for a ghost- haunt of any part of the house. And this was unfortunate, because the chambers of the noises and the spectres lay on the floor beneath. We climbed through a skylight on to the leads and made a diligent inspection there also, and discussed the matter at length. The only decision we could arrive at was that if any phantom chose to visit this house, he was upsetting all the laws of environment that have ever been laid down. So with very contemptuous feelings we settled ourselves down in ono of the dusty rooms to spend the night in wakefulness, and were prepared to depart in the mornin* and report that the whole thing was the most abject of fizzles. We ate and smoked and yawned, and between-whiles we tried to extract interest cut of a mild game of poker with a euchred pack. Jack sat and watched us for a while, looking bored to death, and after a while annexed an overcoat and went to sleep. But a little after twelve he sat up and cocked one ear round to listen. One of us said he'd take two aces to fill his hand, but the other of us stoned on the deal. He was listening also, and looking through the open doorway towards the large empty room which lay at the other side of the corridor.</p>
        <p>Presently there was no doubt about it. Some- body over the way put his shoulder to a piece of furniture and began to move it across the floor, sometimes grating it along in jerks, sometimes waggling it from corner to corner. The piece of furniture was evidently weighty from the noise it made, and the wielder of it was weak but energetic. We could distinguish all these matters with absolute certainty, the only awkward part of it being that there was no furniture in the house to move. The empty house, moreover, filled itself with echoes; the nestlings rustled in the ivy out- side; the ivy tapped the windows; and the night- ingales in the shrubbery beyond, struck up louder songs. We suggested that Jack should go and prospect. Jack said he would wait for us. So we took the lamp and went all three together. One of us bipeds brought with him a blackthorn ; the other carried shoulder-high a half-brick which he had selected as his armament; and we. walked across the corridor with cautious steps. We knew for an absolute certainty that the room was empty, and we could hear with distinctness that piece of furniture being dragged across the floor by someone in a flaring bad temper. For myself, I believe it was a wardrobe, well ballasted with clothes, and without castors. Of es urse, it was foolish to burden ourselves with the blackthorn and the brick, and when wø got into the other room we said so. The ghost obligingly stopped shifting the wardrobe when we came in, and apparently swellowed up all the dust he had raised, since none was in the air. We wondered if he had made himself hot with his exertions. We noticed a distinct sense of chill ourselves, and so did Jack. The starch went out of Jack's tail, and he said that as a residence ho considered the room we had just left the nicest in the house. So we peppered the floor with flour to mark any subsequent footsteps, and returned across the corridor. The ghost waited a minute or so to get back his wind, and then recom- menced operations with the wardrobe. We beat up the ghost's quarters two or three more times that night, when lie got extra noisy, but he was never en view, incl• never did lie leave any trail on the carpet of flour ;.and between-whiles we played poker with the euchred pack and said we didn't mind. We did not feel in the least inclined to sleep, and told one another that only duty and the pleasure of hearing the nightingales was keeping us awake. Whan (lawn ramp. the ehnst kpocked off business THE GREAT SKIN CURE.---" Budden's S.R. Skin Ointment " will cure Old Wounds, Ulcerated Legs. Eczema, Ringworm, Scrofula, Piles and Scurvy, and all Skin Diseases. Boxes, 74d. and 1/11d. Agent for St Helens 4. W. Jackson. Warrington : H. M.Ashton Runcorn: Weston and Co. Ferlesteum : A. Peake widnes : Lewis and Calvert, Ltd.l[fipAr</p>
        <p>Wa gave the house another and most thorough 153SpeCt1011 that afternoon, though without discover- ing anything of interest, and in the evening went down to the village inn and purchased local fables with a deluge of beer.</p>
        <p>The celebrity ot the ghost was a thing to he marvelled at; everybody had got personal reminiscences to give away; it was the sensation of the county. The tales in many cases did not tally with one another; even the sex of the spectre varied; but, setting aside the vagaries of the bucolic imagination, there was no getting over the fact that there was something about the big yellow- brick house that was remArkably unexplainable. The noises were ascribed to various things; but there was one ghost, that of a tall, red-haired woman, which constantly reappeared in the villagers' tales; and they were not chary of saying who she was and why she was there. But as that is one of the things which we are under contract to suppress, no more may be said on that point here. That red-haired lady usually confined herself to scaring the women-servants out of the house; but we two investigators discovered that on one occa- sion at least she had appeared to a man. A farm labourer in the public-house that evening, inspired either by our eloquence or our beer, made con- fession. The matter had happened two years ago, he said; he had never spoken of it before that moment, being fearful of the consequences, seeing that he was a law-breaker at the time of meeting. He was going off a-mushrooming in a paddock belonging to the great house, and to do so intended to pass down the carriage drive. At the entrance, the woman with the red hair came and drove him back. He fled, "all of a clam' swee-at." The time was just before the dawn. He was near enough to her to hear the swish of her silken skirts. The memory of it was still strong in him. The beer mug chattered against his teeth when he finished telling about it. We went back to the house that night more impressed with the reality of the business before us. We shifted our quarters across to the room where the ghost had been the night before, and then we went round the house, searching it syste- matically, fastening the bolts of every window so that they could not be opened from the outside, and stretching wires across all the doorways at nine inches above the ground. The ghost most courteously waited till we had finished and were settled down, and then commenced business—in the room we had occupied on the previous night. He was handling the wardrobe as before, and kicked about a rocking-chair, that squeaked, between-whiles.</p>
        <p>It was annoying, and we beat up his quarters several times without meeting him. He always coyly stopped work and disappeared when we went into the room with the lantern, and made up for it by lumbering the furniture about with redoubled noise as soon as our backs were turned. At last we said we would give it up as a had job, and as there was nothing to be frightened of, we would go to sleep. But Ido not think we either of us dozed much; and morning found us broad awake, and each confessing to a feeling in his mouth as though he had been making a hearty meal off bedclothes.</p>
        <p>Now, to live in an atmosphere of mild creepi- ness may be a luxury for once in a way: but a surfeit of it is unhealthy. We began to sympathise with the colonial gentleman who was letting his two hundred pounds a year run to waste. But none the less we were persuaded that by ingenuity the noises might be stopped, and the apparitions chased away. We told one another sternly—when we were out in the garden—that of course ghosts were all skittles. We rested as before on the daisies and beneath the sun, and that afternoon we made careful inspection of the house from the outside. We worked with thoughts at a goodish strain, and just before sunset one of us made a discovery. He noticed that an iron waterpipe running up to the leads above was unduly polished, and on looking further he saw the faint yellow scratch of brass boot-nails ::bout ten feet above the ground.' Being of an athletic mind, he put his hands and knees to the pipe and climbed upwards. As he climbed, he felt, the paint past a certain mark grow rough beneath his fingers. So the water-pipe had not been used as a stair- way to the leads. It had not even been used as an entrance to one of the upstairs windows, because the smoothed paint ended three feet below the sill. Yet up .to that mark it was polished to an almost ivory smoothness.</p>
        <p>The one of us who had climbed, came to grass again, and called his friend; and the pair of them. smoked pipes of consideration. For what earthly purpose could any person habitually swarm a tail- pipe to within three feet of a window-sill and no further, but always so far? We theorised over the matter till long after the sun had sunk behind the pollarded willows on the other side of the river. The tobacco glowed warmly in the dark, and the talk died. Then one of us got up and went round to the ,other side of the house, and presently returned. "There's a fall-pipe over there just the same, he said. "It runs up outside the room where we stayed last night. This pipe here is outside the room where the row was the night before. There's ivy round each. I'm going up to prospect." He climbed the fall-pipe in the dark, and when he got to the place where the paint ceased to be pnlished, he gripped with his knees and one hand, and groped with his free fingers amongst the ivy. He found nothing to interest him there, and So changed hands. Then he said "By Jove ! " fumbled for a while longer, dropped a garden hoe to the ground, and followed after it himself. He said he had found an iron ventilator in the wall beneath the ivy. It was loose, and he pulled it out. To his surprise, it was tethered with :a yard of string, so that it could be taken from its place and left to dangle without falling to the ground. Inside, between floor boards and ceiling, was the garden hoe; his theory was that the hoe, if scraped upwards against the wood, would simulate the noises which had so disturbed us. We put this notion to the test. One of us went inside. The Bound of heavy furniture being dragged about, if not quite so perfect as it was the night before, was at any rate realistic enough for all practical purposes, and highly creditable to an amateur ghost who was then 'walking " for the first time, and who was unskilled in clinging on to a fall-pipe like a fly and manipulating a clumsy garden hoe at one and the_same_time.</p>
        <p>We laughed, and laid our plans. A lighted lamp was placed in the room we had occupied on the first night, but we ourselves stayed outside and went into cover in a shrubbery at the opposite side of the house. We dared not smoke, and conse- quently the wait was not only cold but wearisome, and even the nightingales failed to amuse us. The ghost um unpunctual, too, which was inconsiderate of him. It was one o'clock before he crept in over some pastures at the back, and went and found out whinh room had the light, and then came round and climbed the fall-pipe on the opposite side of the house. He was wearing a mackintosh inverness, which rustled like silk skirts as he moved. Practice had made him very nimble. He pulled out the ventilator, reached for the hoe, and commenced business. It really was wonder- fully clever. I could have sworn there was a heavy wardrobe being lugged about over the dusty floor —a wardrobe without castors, and packed just cram-full of clothes.</p>
        <p>- Then one of us laughed, did the ghost dropped the hoe and slid to the ground. But we were rather too quick for him ; there was a scuffle and a surrender; and a couple of minutes later we were examining him by lamplight in one of the very rooms which he had for so long successfully haunted. He was a smallish man with bright red hair, and a look in the eyes (poor fellow!) which told us what was wrong. Jack (perhaps in memory of past discomforts) growled at him suggestively. The ghost wanted to know what we were going to do with him, and now that the excitement of capture was over we Were beginning to ask our- selves the same question.me w But, under the mellow- ing influence of sohiskey-and-water in the cup of a flask, he took uponhanself to release us from a good deal of responsibility. He told us his • tale, and from the first half-dozen sentences it was easy to see that he had a tile pretty loose somewhere. According to his own yarn, he had been vilely treated by a lady who had a good deal to do with the hous, and out of revenge had sworn to himself to drive out anyone who should occupy the place ; and up to that date had very thoroughly carried out his designs. He had a good deal to svay about the lady in question, but she is alive now, and our mouths are closed. Besides, what he told us way not be true; he had a very obvious bee in his :,onnet, poor man! He would have talked about his grievance till the dawn came, if we bad been willing to listen; but tiredness had got us as its own. We con- gratulated him on the realism of his "manifesta- nluldlyd.lictllted. .and tions." whereat he Wsl4</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>EDUZATION EXPENDITURE.</head>
        <p>EDUZATION EXPENDITURE.</p>
        <p>ESTIMATES BEFORE THE TOWN COONtIL.</p>
        <p>Scale of Teachers' Salaries not Approved.</p>
        <p>There was a large attendance of the general public at the meeting of the Town Council on Tuesday evening, when the estimates of expenditure for the Education Committee, as well as the Corporation Committees, were discussed.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT proposed the confirmation of the pro- ceedings of the Education Committee, and alluded to the salary of the treasurer and accounting officer, which had been fixed at £lOO. The aldermaryspoke highly of Mr. Fairhurst's valuable services. He said the matter . had been very carefully considered by the committee, who had come to the conclusion that Mr. Fairhurst was fully entitled to this recognition of his services. The accounts of the Education Committee were extremely numerous and complicated, and the treasurer had pass- ing through his hands yearly no less a sum than £40,000. From the experience they all had had of his work for a, long time they knew that that work was always per- formed with thoroughness and assiduity. (Hear, hear.) Councillor ELLISON seconded.</p>
        <p>Councillor JAMES EVANS moved an amendment to the effect that the resolution relating to the increase of the salaries of the teachers be not confirmed. The School Management Committee were, perhaps, rushing educa- tional matters faster than was necessary. Moreover this was not an opportune time to raise salaries, and they were laying on the ratepayers a heavier burden than they would be able to bear. (Hear, hear). He had been told that the salaries had been increased according to a scale, but if that were so it was a very heavy one. He did not think school teachers were suffering any loss looking at the supply and demand, as they knew the labour market to-day. He moved that the resolution be deferred. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Councillor WILKINSON had great pleasure in second- ing the amendment. Education was, going to le a gery expensive matter for Warrington. He (the speaker)had suffered long enough with Bd. and 10d. on the rates, but the addition of the last Gd. was making him kick. (Laughter). On calculating the whole of the salaries, he found that the advances to the teacheis would be responsible for a 3d. rate. He added that he would like to see the School Management Committee altered. There were only three members of the Council on that Committee; and he thought there ought to be more of them responsible to the ratepayers. Alderman BENNETT replied that the scale had not been adopted hastily, but, on the other hand, that great consideration had been given to it. The increase of the salaries was in accordance with the scale which had been under the consideration of a responsible committee, and had been considered in conjunction with similar salaries in other towns. Their own staff did not com- pare too liberally with other towns, and in some depart- ments they were underpaying rather than overpaying their teachers. In spite of the alleged liberal scale, it was difficult to get the requisite number of certificated teachers. A little time ago they were 25 short. There would be no economy in starving the schools, and he hoped the Council would not undo the work which had occupied so much time and labour on the School Management Committee. Alderman SMETHURST alluded to the increases in the salaries of the teachers' and instanced the fact that one salary had been raised from £125 to £220, and another from £llB to £2OO. If the Corporation raised the salaries of their officials at that rate, the members of the Council would soon get into trouble. (Hear, hear, and laughter.) The Education Committee should care- fully consider these matters. If care was not exercised they would be adding not only 11d, in the £ on the rates' but ls. Gd. before they had finished. Their scholars had passed equal to any town in Lancashire almost, and he did not see that this big rise of salaries would increase the efficiency of the scholars to any great extent. The MAYOR asked what was the reason for the increase, as he had never heard any complaints from the teachers who appeared to be satisfied with the wages which IL-1 been previously paid? They would have been satisfied with any reasonable increase. It appeared the head teachers had sent the minutes back with regard to themselves. He could quite understand the feeling of the ratepayers with regard to there being more extravagance than there ought to be. (Hear, hear). A vote was then taken on the amendment. For it I there voted Alderman Smethurst, Councillors John Roberts, Wilkinson. Thoruton, Forshaw,Burton,Duncan, Iliorrocks, P. Peacock, A. Peacock, Wilson, Barton, IJames Evans, Lord, Pemberton and Ashcroft (16). Against, Aldermen Fairclough, Bennett, and Pierpoint, Councillors John Evans, Tinnion, Dodds, and Stott (7). The MAYOR: The amendment moved by Councillor James Evans is carried and the minute in question will be sent back. Councillor WILKINSON observed that there were people in the borough who had not yet thoroughly grasped the question of education. They were only beginning to do so when it 'Affected their pockets. (Laughter.) A little restriction ought to be put on the spending power. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>THE BOROUGH TREASURER'S REMUNERATION. ANOTHER AMENDMENT PROPOSED. THE DISMISSAL OF TRAMWAY MEN.</p>
        <p>Councillor LORD then proposed an amendment to the proposal to pay the Borough Treasurer an extra £lOO a •year. He had no animus whatever to Mr. Fairhurst,but he objected to the increase. He believed Mr. Fairhurst was a good offi6ial, but had a good salary, and the pre- sent was not tketime to increase a highly paid official's salary by £lOO. They were trying to economise in other departments—especially in the Tramways—where they were dismissing Thor 20 men in order to curtail expenses. ("Shame.") These men were working men, probably with wives and families, and he thought it was a shame to increase these highly paid officials' salaries in the way they were doing, while trying to curtail expenses in other ways. The MAYOR: Does anyone second? No answer being given the MANOR said the amend- ment therefore fell to the ground. The other minutes were then agreed to.</p>
