      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>di6</head>
        <p>di6</p>
        <p>GREAT BRITAIN AND PRANCE.</p>
        <p>By the Signing of the agreement between Great Britain and France, an object has been achieved which must indeed be a source of congratulation to the British and French Foreign Ministers, Lord Lansdowne and M. Delcasse, respectively. The agreement, it should be stated, does not in any way interfere with the arbitration treaty already in existence, nor does it include a new alliance, but it sweeps away those dangerous zones which menaced the peace of the two nations, in Newfoundland, West Africa, Morocco, Siam, Egypt, and the New He- Wides. Over these points of contention the French and British diplomats have wrangled for many years, and there has been a danger that while the problems they pre- sented were left unsolved, some unfortunate blunder might plunge us into a war with our neighbours across the Channel—a war which, however decided, would be a calamity to civilisation and progress of the world. It was cognisant to everyone that our relations with France towards the end of the last century were as had as they had been since the Napoleonic wars, and for the happy conclusions now made permanent by the signing of the agreement by Lord Lans- downe and the French Ambassador, M. Cambon, King Edward is largely responsible. His Majesty from the time of his accession, has striven persistently for a better under- standing between the two nations, and thanks to the admirable tact and wide knowledge possessed by our Sovereign, his efforts have met with complete success. The King's visit to Paris, and President Loubet's to London; international visits of chambers of commerce, and members of Parliament, did much towards hastening the negotiations. Now that the bonds of friendship have been cemented it behoves all to foster the entente cordiale which should be the natural outcome of this agreement. - •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>YOUNG SPANISH KING.</head>
        <p>THE YOUNG SPANISH KING.</p>
        <p>King Alfonso, now that he has come of age, is beginning to exert himself actively and to show that he possesses no small amount of personality. He is still known amongst his subjects as "El Pegueno " (the little one), and he is building up great popularity for hiraself by his lack of affecta- tion and genial- ity of manner. He is at present taking for the first timehis pos- ition amongst the ruling sover- eigns in Europe, and a few days ago he met and received the Kaiser upon his visit to Spain, and made a great impres- sion by his dignified and kingly bearing. Now he is engaged upon a tour of his country, pr e - para tor y to formal visits to Paris, Berlin, and Vienna and receiving King Edward in Spain,which our King proposes</p>
        <p>KING ALFONSO.</p>
        <p>to viiit-shOrtiv. One incident only has marred the harmony of his reception and that occurred in Bar- celona, which has always been such a hot- bed of anarchy that it was thought that the young King would have omitted it from his programme. However, with characteristic pluck, he preferred to run the gauntlet of any possible disloyalty, with the result that he was greeted most warmly. But it is a question whether, nevertheless, he was not the object of an anarchist outrage, which, if intended, failed utterly. The facts are being kept comparatively dark and the telegrams are obviously well censored, and all that is certain is, that when the King was leaving the Labour Exhibition an explosion occurred. At first a bomb outrage was reported, and later on a gas escape, but which it was is uncertain. However, the satisfactory side of the question is that King Alfonso was never for an instant in danger, and the affair, during which he displayed the greatest toolness and bravery, has had the effect of Altogether, his reign has opened in a very aus- picious and promising manner, and great things are expected of his capacity to rule and to bring Spain once more to the front amongst the European Powers.</p>
