      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>TOLSTOY AND THE WAR.</head>
        <p>TOLSTOY AND THE WAR.</p>
        <p>There is not much definite to record in regard to the war in the Far East, though unconfirmed reports—in the main of Japanese victories—are plentiful. Meanwhile that in- teresting character, Count Tolstoy, has given to the world through the medium of a French journal- ist,his views upon war and civilisation. Dealing with two races--Rus- sian and Japan- ese—he derii e d there was any inherent inferi- ority of yellow man to white man, and he sees nothing in Japanese civili- sation which stamps it as in any way infe- rior to Euro- pean. He em- COUNT TOLSTOY. phatically re- pudiated the suggestion that th e vie tory of the Japs would be detrimental to civilisation. He re- fused to pass judgment on the war, or the case which Russia and japan have put for- ward : he hates all war, and does not admit that any grievance will justify the killing of man by man. It was suggested to Tolstoy that the fact that the destiny , of Russia was at stake in the war might i have caused him, as a Russian to modify Ithe ideas he had preached ;.11 his life, whereupon the Count replied, "I ask myself what is my real duty? I feel that the cause of humanity is the supreme cause, and my conscience convinces me that murder, however effected, however disguised, is execrable; that war is a monstrous scourge? The aged Count, it is stated, was labouring under intense emotion whilst being inter- viewed, and a' ove moment there were tears in his eves. .</p>
