      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <p>"rff -Pr f.! " I'o 17W, 5, al t The Examiner, Saturday, April 16 1904. WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>! was a fresh charge on the rates of £175. Although Dr. Gornall had been paid £5O a year as borough analyst they had also paid certain fees for testing the gas, and to Mr. Ruddock for testing the products of the Long- ford Depot and for analyses of the water. Mr. Ruddock would do his analytical work as before and also the analysis of the 200 samples under the terms of his appointment at a salary of £175. He would also find his own materials and apparatus, and this he reckoned would cost Mr. Ruddock £6O or £7O. He pointed out that in their previous experience of Mr. Ruddock they had found his c barges very reasonable, and he confessed that he never dared to trust important assays to young men. They did not want young men improving their exp erience in the difficult and important work of analysis at the expense of the stomachs of the people of Warrington. Councillors JOHN EVANS and WILKINSON spoke in support of the committee's recommendation. The former remarked that they had thought of appointing a chemist at Longford but would not need to do so now. Alderman PIER POINT said he was more satisfied as to the necessity for the appointment of a borough analyst, although he still felt that all wisdom was not found in the heads of the old. Some men who had the finest qualifications as chemists were not yet 25. Councillor Tr NNION : It's only experience we think anything of. (El ear, hear). Alderman SUTTON, in his reply, pointed out that the Medical Odicer had had to work almost night and day during the past twelve months, to the neglect of his own particular work in connection with the infectious disease in the town. In answer to a question by Councillor Dodds, the MA.YOR mad an agreement to be entered into with Mr. Ruddock by which for the £175 he agreed to do the work he had previously been called upon to do for which fees had been charged and the 200 samples in addition. The Mayor pointed out that extra work beyond this would have to be paid for as before. The amendment was then put and lost, only three voting for it. • The minutes were adopted. costaAcroßs AND THE STANDARD WAGE. COUNCILLOR STOTT MAKES PRIVATE INQUIRIES. A CONTRACT AGAIN HELD OVER. At a meeting of the Sanitary Works Committee, a letter was read from Messrs. Thomas Rhodes and Sons stating that they paid the rate of wages recognised in the district, and observed such hours of labour as are generally accepted as fair in their trade.—Resolved, that the resolution of the 16th February, 1904, accept- ing the tender of Messrs. Parkinson and Sweaney to supply 500 galvanised steel pails at 4s. 6d. each and 500 ashbins at ss. 2cl. each be rescinded; and that the tender' of Messrs. Thomas Rhodes and Son, of Stourbridge, to supply 500 galvanised .teel pails at 4s. 41d. each, and I , 500 ashbins at 4s. 11d, each, be accepted on condition that evidence is produced as to the rate of wages and I ' hours of employment.—Resolved that the wages of all I ash carriers who have been in the employ of the Cor- poration for more than 12 months be increased to 235. per week, and that all other men commence at 225. and continue to receive that wage until the completion of 12 months' satisfactory service.—Resolved, that the , tender of Messrs. W. and J. Richardson for six months' supply of slack delivered at,Longford Depot be accep- ted.</p>
        <p>Councillor John EVANS proposed the adoption of the minutes, and Alderman Smethurst seconded. Councillor SToTr said he wished to move an amend- ment that the paragraph relating to Messrs. Rhodes and Sons be referred back to the committee. It might se?m a trivial matter to discuss, but this firm was not conforming with the Standing Orders. He also gave an emphatic denial -to the statements in the letter received by the Town Clerk from the firm and brought before the last meeting; of the committee. He had also understood that the minute was intended to read that if it was found out afterwards that this firm was not conforming the contract would bo stopped. The recognized rate of wages in the Stourbridge district, which he had specially visited, was 30s. a week and having interviewed five of the workmen he found that the men employed by this firm on this class of work got from 225. to 285., but very few indeed got the 28s. Me also related that a fortnight before Christmas, Messrs. Rhodes gave a number of their men notice of dismissal owing to shortness of work. When their notice had expired the men were told that they could be re-engaged under a sub-contractor at a reduction of wages. The majority took on this work, and after some weeks the sub-contractor was dis- pensed with and the men were re-engaged by the firm at the reduced rate of wages. It seemed to him that they had used the argument of slackness of work to create new conditions. He did not object to the members of the Council refusing to accept his information but he I objected to members of the Council objecting to the Iprinciple. Councillor Maginn had said it was a wrong principle entirely, but he should not sly that when they I had standing orders. He (Councillor Stott)remembered not long ago that Councillor Magian when seeking votes was agreeable to the principle and made certain promises. Another remark he had made was that it meant a loss to the ratepayers of .£l7 or £lB, when it was only half that amount.. It seemed to the speaker that Councillor Maginn's arithmetic was about as con- I •sistent as his promises. (Laughter.) He was willing to prove all he had said, and when the letter from Messrs. Rhodes was read to the committee he went to Stour- bridge on his own authority to have an interview with the men working there. He interviewed five men,some of whom still worked for Messrs. Rhodes, and they con- firmed what he had said in every detail. At this juncture the MAyort read the letter received from Messrs. Rhodes and Sons which read : "Dear sir,—We have much pleasure in confirming the statement made at the time of tendering, that we pay the rates of wages recognized in the district and also observe such hours of labour as are generally accepted as fair in our trade. We will give the Council such evidence as they require at all times, when properly called upon to do so, yours truly, RHODES and SONS. P.S. Furthermore we buy nearly all the wire from Warrington (White- cross.)" Councillor STOTr : I deny that those statements are correct, and move that the recommendation be referred back for the reasons I have stated. Councillor JAMES EvANs seconded. He took excep- tion to the words" recognized in the district," and said he was unaware that there were any other works doing this class of work in that district. Aldermen SUTTON and SMETHURST (together).—Yes, there are. The 11.1YOR : We'll have a non-trading inquiry to go into it if you like. (Laughter.) Councillor JAMES EVANS : Mr. Mayor, you are in- terrupting rue now as well as keeping me in order. (Laughte-.) He added that he was not prepared to accept the statement of the firm in view of what they had heard without further information. Councillor PEMBERToN supported the amendment,and said the matter should be probed to the bottom. Councillor MAGI said he was rathersorry for what he had to say. Councillor Stott had accused him of not keeping his word to the ratepayers. The MAYOR: That is not on the minutes. Councillor IVIA.GINN: Pardon me,l think I am entitled to defend myself. The MAYOR: Are you going to speak to the amend- ment. Councillor MA.GMT said he was, and added that he was as well prepared to keep his word as any'other member, and he thought he had done so up to now. He had said that the principle was wrong. The stand- ing order was also wrong. Their duty in that Chamber was to get full value for the ratepayers, and the lowest tender should be accepted unless the privilege went to a local manufacturer. They had the firm's guarantee and they were entitled to effect a saving for the rate- payers. If however for no other reason than that these men had been round their sanitary works it was sufficient for him. never to give them an order. I Councillor STaTr said he had never heard of the firm luntil they were mentioned in the committee. Councillor BURTON thought it would be better if the I matter was adjourned for a month for further inform- ation. In some trades the rate of wages for the same work differed in various districts. Alderman SUTTON supported the minutes. He thought it better for workmen to go to work fora reduced wage than have no work at all. Councillor TINNION said he knew both firms person- ally, and they were both highly respectable firms. He was surprised ht Councillor -Stott's statements, though the latter had been perfectly fair and straightforward in the matter. What was meant by the standard rate of wages? Was it that which the trade unionist de- manded or that which the non-unionist workmen demanded? That was a question that wanted settling. (Hear, hear.) In the interests of both firms he thought it better to refer the matter back. The-MAYOR thought thestatement of Messrs. Rhodes should be accepted without proof to the contrary, and the majority of the committee had accepted it. Councillor JOHN EVANS said he would withdraw the minute. Alderman SMETHURST objected, and said they were not fighting the battles of the ratepayers of Warrington but of the trade unionists. I The minutes were then passed with the exception of that one relating to the contract for pails. THE SEWERING OF BACK STREETS AND PASSAGES: Councillor TINNION proposed the minutes of the iPaving and Sewerage Committee which contained a</p>
        <p>The Original Cocoa. and a Speciality,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>BUDGET NIGHT-A FOUR HOURS' SITTING.</head>
        <p>BUDGET NIGHT-A FOUR HOURS' SITTING.</p>
        <p>Lively Scenes</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Council was held at the Town Hall on Tuesday evening, the Mayor (Alderman Bolton) presiding. There were also present,the Deputy- Mayor (Alderman Fairelongh), Aldermen A. Bennett, T. Hutchinson; W. Pierpoint, J. Smethurst. T. H. Sutton, Councillors T. H. Ashcroft, T. Barton, J. Dodds, J. Lord, J. Maginn, A. Peacock, P. Peacock, J. Pemberton, J. Roberts, W. Rogers. D. S. Duncan, James Evans, John Evans, L. Ellison, %V. Forshaw, A. Horrocks, T. J. Hulse, F. Stott, H. Thornton, D. Tinnion, J. Wilkinson, W. J. Wilson with Messrs. J. L. Whittle (Town Clerk), J. Fairhurst (Borough Treasurer), T. Longdin. (Borough Surveyor), J. Dens (Water Engineer), C. Madeley Librarian), W. S. Haddock (Gas Engineer), F. V. L. Mathias (Electrical Engineer), J. G. Gornall (Medical Oacer), and J. M. Murray (Director of Education).</p>
        <p>THE LATE MR. JOHN HARRISON. The MA.YOR observed that Mr. John Harrison had died since their last meeting. For nine years he was a member of the Council, being in office as far back as 1870. He had beenan elective auditor for 20 years and he was also a highly respected tradesman. He would be a much missed man. His Worship moved the usual resolution expressing regret at Mr. Harrison's death and condoling with the relatives. Alderman BENNETT seconded, and said he was asso- ciated with Mr. Harrison for mny years and learned to form a high opinion of his personal character and his honesty and ability. In the whole of the town there was not a man more generally • respected or beloved. (Hear, hear.) The motion was carried. WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT PROPERTY OWNERS' ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>Councillor BURTON asked the Mayor if he had received a communication from the Property Owners' Associa- tion. The MAYOR said it was not usual to discuss such matters in the Council. If a letter had been sent as suggested by Councillor Burton it would come under the notice of the General Purposes Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>AUDITORS.</head>
        <p>THE AUDITORS.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR fonnally announced the election of Messrs. Ball and Bradshaw as elective auditors. He (the Mayor) had appninted Alderman Fairclough as his auditor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>AN INIPROVENIENT IN LIGHTING.</head>
        <p>AN INIPROVENIENT IN LIGHTING.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Street Improvement and Light- ing Committee, held on the 24th larch, it was resolved that Pinners-brow and Orford-lane be widened to GOft. in width and that the building line be prescribed accordingly. Alderman Sutton moved the adoption of the minutes. Alderman Bennett seconded.—Alderman Smethurst said he heartily thanked the committee for the improvement in lighting which had been effected in Bewsey street and Lodge lane. At night it had been like going through a tunnel ; now the people were de- lighted. (Hear, hoar). The minutes were adopted. WATCH COnmITTEE. AN ECONOMY IN CONSTABLES.</p>
        <p>The M.A.Yoit introduced the minutes of the Watch Committee. Alderman BENNETT referred to the recommendition that four additional! constables be appointed, and said he noticed from the estitnttes that the Finance Com- mittee disapproved of this item. The MAYOR said the Government Inspector had re- commended these extra men and the committee desired power to appoint them when required. They did not desire that the expenditure should be immediately sanctioned.</p>
        <p>Councillor A. PEACOCK proposed that the reaom- mendation be deleted from the minutes.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON seconded and on a vote being taken the amendment was carried by 14 votes to 10. The minutes were then confirmed with this exception. HEALTH COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>THE DEATH RATE-SCHOOL CHILDREN AND INFECTIOUS DISEASES. A NEW PUBLIC ANALYST.</p>
        <p>Alderman SUTTON moved the adoption of the minutes He pointed out that the committee had felt that it was importanethat children who had suffered from infectious diseases should not be allowed to return to school until they had received a certificate from the Medical Officer of Health showing that it was quite safe for them to do so. The committee were of opinion that this was an important step in the direction of preventing the spread of infection. With regard to the death-rate, about which so much had been said in one of their local papers—references which had given the impression that Warrington had a higher death-rate that any other town in England—he would just like to give them a report upon the subject furnished by the Medical Officer of Health. He had asked him to supply this along with some of his remarks respecting some of the most prevalent infectious diseases they had had to deal with. This report showed that during the last week in March scarlet fever had decreased. It was also pointed out that last year measles caused the death of 97 children which increased the death-rate. Whooping cough was responsible for3o deaths. Infectious diseases had also been increased owing to overcrowding in the schools. For 1903 the death-rate for the borough was 18.4 as compared with 19.7 for the past decade, and the rate for'l9o3 compared favourably with that of Liverpool which was 20.5. The figures for Bromley were 19.2 Preston 18.7, Manchester 19.7, Salford 19, and Wigan, 22.2 per 1,000. With regard to smallpox they would all be rejoiced to learn that the town; was now free from the disease, and that all the patients had been dis- charged from the Sanatorium. He did not believe that this would have been possible at so early a date if re- vaccination had not been resorted to so promptly, and, therefore, he thought the committee were right in supporting a petition in favour of the Bill now before Parliament, which provided for the re-vaccination of children between 12 and 13 years of age except in cases where conscientious objections were proved and where there were medical reasons for exemption. With re- gard to the request of the Board of Agriculture and Pisheries that 200 samples should be taken and the committee's proposal to appoint Mr. F. G. Ruddock as analyst, he pointed out that Dr. Gornall found that his many duties prevented him altogether from being able to comply with this desire. Having regard to his in- creased duties the comniittee proposed to accept the doctor's resignation as analyst, and that he should still retain his present salary. The appointment of Mr. Ruddock was not an increloe of salary of £175, since Mr. Ruddock had done much analytical work, and this would be included in his agreement for the new office in addition to the 203 samples mentioned.</p>
        <p>Councillor JAMES EVANS seconded. Councillor ROGERS said with regard to over-crowding in the schools, there were 1,400 children attending school at Warrington under the age of five years. They were forced to take in these children if their parents presented them, but if instead these children were kept away from school, and allowed to play in a healthy atmosphere it was probable that the, schools would not be closed as often a 8 they were, and the death-rate would be: reduced.</p>
        <p>Councillor HORROCKS said he found that the school attendance officers were very hard on some parents whose children wore away sick and forced them to go to school. In cases where children were recovering from fever the officers were likewise too importunate. The INIA_You. : These are matters for the IVledictl Officer of Health.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT said he had no very decided opinion with regard to vaccinatißn but he rather objected to the Council in their amateur ignorance taking such a drastic step as to support a bill for compulsory re- vaccination.</p>
        <p>Alderman PIERPOINT urged that no sufficient reason had been given for the acceptance of Dr. Gornall's resignation as analyst and for the appointment of Mr. Ruddock. He thought that the Medical Officer was not so hard worked that he could not find time for the analysis of 200 samples. There was no sufficient reason urged for making a permanent charge upon the borough rate for £175 a year. The post should also have ;;een advertised in order that the young men of the town might have had 3 chance. He would move an amendment that Dr. Gornall's resignation as analyst be not accepted and that the appointment of Mr. Ruddock be not confirmed. COUBCiIiOr PEMBERTON said he would second. The present was not an opportune time to create a new office. Councillor TINNION said he thoroughly agreed with the appointment of Mr. Ruddock. It was not creating a new office. The unfortunate mistake had been made when they had appointed Dr. Gornall as both Medical Offieer and Borough Analyst. He had had some experi- euce of analytical chemistry, and attached very great importance to the work, and were theyto bein theposi- tion of having to appoint the medical officer of health again they would find that they would not be allowed to appoint himanalyst also,unless he had special qualifi- cations for the position. It was not quite fair to say it</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
