      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>EX-QUEEN /SABELLA OF SPAIN,</head>
        <p>EX-QUEEN /SABELLA OF SPAIN,</p>
        <p>The death of the ex-Queen Isabella in Paris recalls the romantic story of her early life which nowadays has almost been forgotten,: This queen in exile passed away last Satur- day morning in the presence of her three daughters, the Infantas Enlalie, Isabelle, and Maria de la Paz, and her son-in-law, Prince Ludwig of Bavaria at the age of seventy- three. Her life was a very romantic one, and, despite the oblivion in which she lived during recent years, if forms a striking page of history. Born in 1830, three years later she was declared Queen of Spain in her own right under the regency of her mother, the Princess Christina of Bourbon.; and ten years later she was declared to be of age. Mean- while the country had been in a state of ferment owing to the machinations of Don Carlos, whose claims to the throne were pre-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>SABELLA. O 1 S</head>
        <p>SABELLA. O 1 S</p>
        <p>EX-QUE ferred, and still are, by a certain faction, though of late the Carlist cause has been less to the front. However, under her rule the country progressed, and things settled' down quietly, till in 1868, when a revolution broke out at Cadiz. It spread throughout the country, and Queen Isabella after two years of compulsory exile abdicated in favour of her son, the father of King Alfonso. Since then she has lived in the greatest retirement in Paris.</p>
