      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>LATE SIR THOMAS SALT.</head>
        <p>THE LATE SIR THOMAS SALT.</p>
        <p>In Sir Thomas Salt, there passes away a well-known banker and railway magnate. He died at the end of last week in his seventy-fourth year at his residence in Staffordshire, Weeping Cross. A few weeks ago he was seized with a severe attack of influenz but seemed to be on the high road to recovery In fact, only two or three days before his death he was out in a bath- chair, and the end was quite sudden and un- expected. In his early days Sir Thomas Salt was a banker. but later on he added many other interests to a busy and varied life. For twelve years he presided over Lloyd's Bank, and for twenty years before his death he was</p>
        <p>SIR THOMAS SALT.</p>
        <p>chairman of the North Staffordshire Railway. As far back as 1859 he entered Parliament, representing Stafford in the Conservative interest until 1892 with only one short break during the whole period. From 1876 to 1880 he filled the office of Parliamentary Secretary to the Local Government Board, and in 1899 he was rewarded for his public services by a baronetcy. In 1861 Sir Thomas Salt married the youngest daughter of Mr. John AnderdoN, of Chislehurst, and in 1863 was born his eldest son, Major Thomas Anderdon Salt, who succeeds him. The new baronet, who was educated at Clifton and Oriel College, Oxford, is a major in the nth Husstirsd</p>
        <p>a, r , ' c / ' ----7-7 ,4 ~,,,,,,,,, ~,,,, --„\\\ \._.,,... 7/</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <p>EXHIMTION AT PRR ram..a.„ WARRING-TON,</p>
        <p>APRIL 19th to 29th.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <p>resolution from the Health Committee requesting this committee to cause all back passages to be sewered in order to carry off surface water.—Resolved, that the Health Committee be informed that this com- mittee will bs prepared to cause surface water drains to be placed in all back passages at the expense of the property owners. Councillor Joiri ROBERTS seconded.</p>
        <p>Councillor MA.GINN said there were back streets 30 and 40 feet wide in St. John's Ward without a sewer in them, and the surface water was floating about. Councillor ELLisoN made a similar complaint about Latchford Ward, and added that he was sure the state of the back streets and passages was responsible for a lot of the ill-health in the town,</p>
        <p>Alderman SMETHURST said that allowing property- owners seven years in which to pay their accounts had put the committee in a difficult position. Instead of being able to borrow money from the Bank for street- making they had now to receive the sanction of the Local Government Board for a loan. They were doing their utmost to get the streets attended to, and they proposed to borrow something like £lOO,OOO. With re- gard to ill-health, he believed most of the sickness had come from streets which were both paved and sewered.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON said other towns went to the Local Governunnt Board for the money for street- making and their was no need for such delay. ThelVla.Yoa : It strikes me that this Council is a place where everybody airs their grievances instead of supporting the minutes. _ - .</p>
        <p>'Aldernan SurroN : I quite agree with you Mr. Mayor! (Loud laughter). The minutes were then conarmed. BRANCH LIBRARIES SHELVED.</p>
        <p>Alderman Bmagfrr proposed the minutes of the Museum Committee, and alluded to the appointment of agents at three delivery stations. Tuey had fixed the sites for the branch libraries and he hoped the Council would sanction the small expenditure involved though the Finance Committee had struck the item from the estimates. Councillor ELLISON seconded. Councillor WILKDUSON said he thought the library question might wait twelve months. In answer to Alderman Pierpoint, the Town Clerk said if the minutes were passed the Finance Committee would have to find the money. Councillor LORD moved an amendment that the para- graph relating to the branch libraries be deleted Cot:minor JAMES EVANS seconded,and the amendment was carried.</p>
        <p>The remaining minutes were conftrmecl. COUNCILLOR STOTT AND TEIE WINWICK STOKER. "A SCENE."</p>
        <p>Couneillor Srarr asked the Chairman of the Water Committee the following qiesttons :—(1), Whether the stoker appointed for the Winwick Pumping Station in February last is being provided with house, coal, and gas, in addition to his wages of 3s. Bd. per day? (2), If not, why did not the Chairman of the Water Com- mittee correct the Mayor and the Town Clerk when in reply to my objection -to the wages offered, they stated that there wLs a house, coal, ecc., in addition to the wages specified?</p>
        <p>the tOWN CLERK said that in the absence of the Chairman of the Committee he would answer the ques- tions. The miii appointed to the Winwick pumping station was not correctly described as a stoker. His duties were to assist the stoker, who was responsible for cleaning the engines, &amp;c. He was receiving 3s. Bd. per day wages, seven days a week, but was paid as for 7i days, which made his wages 275. 61. a week. He was not allowed house, coal and gas, which was fully ex- plained to the applicants for the post. The stoker proper was allowed house, gas and coal. The answer to question 2 was really answered in the reply to question 1. He (the Tows Clerk) was mistaken in the statement he made at the last meeting, but unintentionally so, but it seemed to him that Couacillor Stott inferred that the statement was made intentionally. ("No, no.") He did not think his (Councillor Stott's) reference to the Mayor and the cb.airmin of the committee was at all courteous.</p>
        <p>Councillor STorT 1'333 but the gIYOR said he could not reply, Councillor SroTr: I was going to say that as we cannot discuss this under ordinary circumstances I move that the Standing Orders be suspended so that we can do so.</p>
        <p>There wer-e-several cries of "Order" in the course of this retne,rlc, and there was some difficulty in hearing. The MA.YOR: You have had the answers, you cannot discuss the matter now.</p>
        <p>Councillor STorr : No, but I think we might—. Alderman PEERPOINT, who wls also met with cries of "Order," said he wished to call attention to an import- ant point °lithe matter. Ile wished to know whether a member desiring the suspension of the Standing Orders could not move that? lie had a right to do so. The rows CLERK: The Standing Orders cannot be suspended without notice of motion. s</p>
        <p>Alderman PIRRPOENr (sotto voce): I don't think you're right. He thenasked fora copy of the Standing Orders.</p>
        <p>The HA.YOR.: You mn3t take my ruling gentlemen or I shall leave the chair. People have to be put in their places sometimes. The matter then dropped for the time being, Alder- man Pierpoint being engaged in looking up the Standing Orders. During the discussion on the Gymnasium Com- mittee's minutes, _ _ _</p>
        <p>Councillor DODDS referred to the matter again, and reading from the Standing Orders, said that with refer- ence to the suspension of the Orders the rule ran No previous notice need be given." He took it that the Town Clerk was wrong for once. The TOWN CLERK: Excuse me, I am not wrong. Continuing, he said that his predecessor thought that that rule was inadvisable and not legal, but it was allowed to remain as it was thought it might be possible to get a matter of urgency through. A Standing Order was never suspended for the purpose of creating a discussion. .</p>
        <p>Councillor-PrEaPoryr . I think I have the right of a person tl explaQation, Mr. Mayor? The IVLA.YoR : No. Alderman PIERPOINT : lam one of the oldest- The MAYOR: You are out of order.</p>
        <p>Alderman PIERPOENT : I submit I have a right. As I said, I am one of the oldest members of this Council, and would not willingly do anything to cause any dis- turbance of the ordinary ruling. Continuing he said he was sufficiently well conversant witk the Standing Orders to say that he had repeatedly seen them sus- pended simply on a motion like Councillor Stott's,and it was for the purpose of protecting what he considered to be ail important right—that of a member of -the Council to request the suspension of the Standing Orders—that he wished to bring it to a vote. The MAYOR: Is this intended as a lecture for me? (Laughter.) Alderman PIERPOINT : Oh, no.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: Questions have been asked repeatedly before the discussion or anything else has been over- ruled, Councillor Stott puts two questions and he has the answers, and then he brings a motion so that a dis- cussion can come on. (Cries of" No.") Alderman PIERPOINT : You have no right to assume that. The MAYOR: Will you come in this chair? Alderman PIERPOINT : I could have sat in it if I had wished, but I would not, and never intend to do. The MAYOR: Here you pay a lawyer at a salary of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <p>£7OO a year to advise you, and he does it, and you want , your own way then. I Councillor PEMBERTON (warmly): Yes, when the I Standing Orders are ruled one way for one and another way for another. It is not right. I The MAYOR: Well, we need not have all this heat. I We can do the business without that. 1 Councillor PEMBERTON :—Very well. I can— The MAYOR: What are you discussing now? Councillor PEMBERTON : We are discussing the rights of members.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR (warmly) : I say it is out of order. Sit down and let me carry on the meeting in some way or other. I never saw such a--(the sentence was not finished). Alderman Sutton rose to speak. The MA.YOR: Go on with the next business. (To Alderman Smethurst) : Go on with the Electricity and Tramways Committee. Move the confirmation of the minutes.</p>
        <p>The Alderman did so,and the matter thereupon ended. ECONOMIES ON THE TRAMWAYS. REDUCING THE STAFF.</p>
        <p>Alderman SMETHURST proposed the adoption of the minutes of the Electricity and Tramways Committee, which contained the following resolutions :—That the Engineer and Tramways Manager report upon the advisability of constructing tramways along Orford- lane, and as to the completion of the tramways in Pad- gate-lane. That in future all reports against men in the employ of the Tramways Department be made direct to the Manager; that he be fully authorised to use his discretion in dealing with the cases, and that only such cases as he thinks advisable be brought before the Com- mittee.—The Chairman presented a report on the" One Man" Car in use in Southport.—Resolved, that the manufacturers of such car be approached with a view to the introduction of them on the Winwick-road route.— Resolved, that the Winwick-road route be worked by means of one car only, and that the Manager re-arrange the running of such car. Councillor JAMES EvaNs moved an amendment to the resolution in regard to reports against men in the employ of the committee. He considered it in opposi- tion to Standing Order 47, which gave men the oppor tunity of appealing to the committee if they thought they were unfairly dealt with. He did not think it wise of the committee to empower their manager dismiss men, or use his discretion as to reporting to the committee. He moved that the resolution be re- ferred back.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON seconded. Councillor STOTT supported the amendment. He thought it against the interests of the men. The TOWN CLERK said the new resolution did not override the Standing Order :giving the men the right of appeal._ _</p>
        <p>Councillor P. PEACOCK : Only last week 16 men were dismisssd by the committee. It WaS a scandalous shame.</p>
        <p>Councillor THORNTON said they had placed too much confidence in the former manager. The staff was too large, and that was the reason they had to discharge 16 men. He thought it would be better to let the matter rest with the committee. Councillor WuarNsoli said they had had considerable difficulty with their employs. There had been two factions working against one another, and they had had men before the committee who had gone over the head of the manager. All reports should be made to him. Councillor JAMES EVANS : It does not say so. Councillor WILKINSON : That is the meaning of it. If the manager did something unfair to an employ he could come before the committee but reports must be made to the manager. They had received a legacy from the last committee. Wera they to go on over-staffed and losing money? At Southport for practically the same service they found five cleaners instead of 8 at Warrington and no checkers or inspectors. The com- mittee felt it a grievance that these 16 men had to go. Councillor MA.GILsig said there had been too much faction fighting on the tramway system. They could save £2O per week in wages and still run the trams as efficiently. Councillor DODDS thought the minute might be referred back to sea if it could not be drafted more satisfactorily. This was agreed to, and the balance of the minutes were confirmed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>RE-VACCINATION OF CHILDREN.</head>
        <p>RE-VACCINATION OF CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>Councillor STOTT moved that the recommendation of the General Purposes Committee that a petition be pre- sented to Parliament in favour of the Re-vaccination Bill be not confirmed. It was pointed out that the Bill referred to the re-vaccination of children. Coun- cillor Stott said he did not believe in the efficacy of vaccination and he thought once was quite sufficient to go before the magistrates to get an exemption order. Alderman BENIsTETT seconded on the ground that the bill was too drastic.</p>
        <p>Councillor TINNION said he believed in vaccination and he was not ashamed to own it. He would be pre- pared to be re-vaccinated every year if it would keep off that terrible disease of smallpox. It was not intended under the bill to enforce its provisions on those. who in the first instance, had received an exemp- tion order. He was only sorry that there was not com- pulsory vaccination.</p>
        <p>- Alderman SUTTON said he had been listening to all sorts of nonsense that evening and he would now ask the Council to listen to common sense. (Laughter). He fully believed in the proptiety of supporting this peti- tion. If it had not been for re-vaccination, Warrington would not at the present moment have been rid of the smallpox epidemic. On a vote being taken the amendment was lost, only 5 voting for it COUNCILLOR STOTT'S MOTION.</p>
        <p>Councillor STOTI' had given notice of the following motion : "That it be an instruction from this Council to the Paving and Sewerage Committee to expedite the paving of all streets and back passages where building operations are completed." He intimated that in view of the decision of the Paving and Sewerage Committee he would not move the motion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>FREE LECTURE. As will be seen7from our advertising columns, a free lecture, illustrated by limelight, on" Our Foods " will be given by that well-known lecturer Dr. Andrew Wilson in the Cs-operative Hall, St. Helens, on Wed- nesday night at eight o'clock. The lecture is intended Ito arouse public interest in food questions, and should • attract a large audience.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>NEW TABLE DAINTIES.</head>
        <p>NEW TABLE DAINTIES.</p>
        <p>The two special varieties of biscuits " Pat-a-Cake " and" Vanessa" made by Messrs. Peak. Frean and Co., and advertised in our columns are really so delicious as to warrant the special attention of our lady readers. At times like these when so many articles are being introduced, it sometimes requires a special word to induce a trial, but we feel certain that these little dainties will give pleasure to all who take them.</p>
