        <p>ALL KINDS (OF PHINTIN4 FOR CONCERTS TEA PARTIES SOIREES SERMONS BALLS MEETINGS &amp;c., 34c JOHN WALKER AND CO EXAMINER OFFICE, BEWSEy STREW. WARRINGTO</p>
        <p>Mr. BALFOUR has THE LICENSING BILL. postponed the in-</p>
        <p>troduction of the Licensing Bill. When it is introduced it is not likely to lessen his embarrassments. The situa- tion is remarkable. Throughout the country many Unionists are opposed to the Prime Minist3r on the question of Free Trade and many more on the question of Chinese labour. But it would be diffi- cult to find any Unionists outside of the liquor trade itself who are not opposed to the expected scheme of suspending the control of magistrates over the issue of licenses. Mr. BALFOUR must be Nell aware of the hostile feeling which his pro- posals to interfere with the discretion of the justices have aroused. County magistrates who have voted Tory all their Ives have been heard to declare that if the control over licenses is taken from them they will never vote Tory again. The problem for the Prime Minister is extremely delicate. The Arch- bishop of Canterbury, not a man of extreme views is strongly against any tampering with the magis- terial authority. "I am quite certain," he writes, "that we are doing right in emphasising by voice and vote the principle that the magistrates should retain a discretion which they cannot be shown to have abused." If this discretion be taken from them even temporarily, this will amount to an imputation that they have abused it ; and what will become of the safeguards for the good behaviour of license holders? They will be taught that the justices are of no account, for what other im- pression can be made by the abrogation of their authority. Is such an impression likely to make him more careful in the conduct of his business or less ? According to the forecast in Wednesday's " Standard " the Bill is to suspend magisterial control until a scheme of compensation is got into working order. This opens up a happy vista for the publican. Freed from the fear of the magistrates, he may have quite a rollicking time with the certainty that when the bench resumes its supervision and extinguishes his brewer, he will be compensated on a handsome scale. Thus it is said that the ground for compensation will consist chiefly of the extra duties imposed on the trade for the purposes of the South African war—that is to say, rather more than two millions of public revenues are to be set aside for the benefit of publicans retiring from the business, and this sum is to be supple- mented by fresh taxation of the same kind. This is certainly a novel principle of finance. Never before have taxes been set apart for the benefit of a particular class. It would be just as rational to propose that the extra income-tax raised for the war shall be devoted to providing annuities for small taxpayers. If the Bill is what the " Standard " says it is, Mr. BALFOUR will make the astounding suggestion that every Chancellor of the Exchequer for the rest of time shall keep certain duties in his Budget exclusively for the compensation of publicans. Such a scheme, if it should really come to birth, will be received with universal derision. We can scarcely believe that Mr. BALFOUR wishes to make his :Government so ridiculous as that, unless he has decided to go out on this Bill and appeal to the publicans as their knight errant. Even if he should Dress the Bill, as the" Westminster Gazette" says, it would be no benefit to the publicans, for the CHANCELLOR of the EXCHEQUER in the next Government will frame his first Budget without the slightest regard for Hr. BALFOUR'S absurd attempt to quarter his interesting clients On the national funds. Still, the anticipations of the Bill by Mr. BALFOUR'S friends may do him a serious injustice. We pre- fer to believe that the Bill will not prove to be such nonsense as they try to make out.</p>
        <p>AFTER all, Mr. BALFOUR THE TIBET suddeLly decided to discuss DE B A.TE. Tibet on Wednesday instead of compensation to publicans. It was a prudent manceuvre, and may turn out to have been inspired by the discovery that the 'Licensing Bill in its original shape wordd never do.i While Mr. BA.LFOUR was eularging,on: Tibet, the Parliamentary draughtsmen had probably got to work already on the remodelling of the Bill. It really looks as if the announcement in Tuesday's " Standard " was a "feeler," the effect of which was rather alarming. As for the Xibet debate, everybody knew that, as Sir HENRY FOWLER said, the necessary meAsures for safeguarding Colonel YOUNGRUSEAND'S</p>
        <p>mission would have to be taken. Mr. BRODRICK'S resolution, stated no more than that,but the divi- sion was by no means an implicit approval of Lord CURZON'S policy. It is one thing to authorise the employment of Indian troops to ptotect the mission, and quite another to uphold the policy of sending a mission at all. Colonel YOUNGHUSBAND and his comrades must be rescued if ne2essary at all hazards, but that view which was naturally expressed in the House by a very large majority has nothing whatever to do with the initiative adopted by Lord Cußzorr. If the Rouse had been asked some months ago whether it approve d of the mission, the division list would have been very different. Wednesday's deb.tte disclosed a serious difference of opinion between the Government and L )rd Cultzox. He is in favour of the extreme measure of making British influences predominant in Tibet by t/ stablishing a British resident atLhasa. Mr. BALFOUR is opposed to that, and his attitude had its effect ,no doubt</p>
        <p>upon the division. The line taken by the Goverw ment is that we wish the Tibetans no harm what' ever, but that they have given us the cold shoulder for fourteen years, and must now be compelled to negotiate. What is the secret of this policy ei forcing our goodwill upon people who do n°t want it? Lord CURZON makes it plain that 1° his opinion we must forestall Russia. Mr. 8.."' FOUR said quite frankly that he accepted the assurance from Russia that she had no desire t° control Tibet. Manifestly LORD CURZON does nut accept it. His eagerness to set up a British Resident at Lhasa can only mean that ho distruete Russia profoundly, but it looks as if the upshot ni the Younghusband mission will be to increase Russian influence by embittering the Tibetane against everything British. That can scarcely be regarded as a diplomatic success. The Russian assurances accepted by Mr. BALFOUR may ha" been given in perfect good faith, and 37e RuEsia is likely to be charged with intrigue when she has simply profited by a blunder of the Indian Government. It will not be th° first time that has ;happened. Mr. BALFOUR le unable to tell ua what will be gained by coercing the Tibetans into negotiations. It is not even certain that any coercion will be eftective,short an occupation of a country which is little better than a wilderness. The mission has arrived st Gyantze, and that is satisfactory. But if the Lamas should refuse to talk it over with Colonel YOUNG-HUSBAND, what is to happen then ? Is be to penetrate to Lhasa, and complete the favourable impression on the Tibetan mind? If the Lanifin shut themselves up in their monasteries, are they to be dragged out and forced to talk? As Lord CURZON has undertaken this business solely t° impress the Oriental world with the power and determination of the British in India, he certainly running a grave risk of defeating /lie lown object.</p>
        <p>The Town Council assembled on Tuesday none too happy a frame of mind. A. heavy budge' and an extra sixpence in the pound on the rates had to be faced, and the effect on the majority 0.1 the members was early apparent. They brougl,l" long faces and a mental resolve to be sternl economical, and it was not long ere they found se opportunity of saving something, which opPeir tunity, moreover, was seized with both hanoS; The minutes of the Education Committee se': that authority's estimated expenditure firs" came up for confirmation, and an inmediate onslaught was made by the economists. Coue cillor James Evans raised an objection to the re- vised scale of teachers' salaries, which it has taken the Education Committee considerable trouble to formulate, and notwithstanding Aldern3se, Bennett's despairing protest against this undolulf; of the committee's work, the scale was referre" back. Now we are firmly of opinion that theA Council acted hastily in this instance. The spree' and development of education all over the countd under the new Act has raised the market value of the teachers, and unless the Warringt° . auothorities are prepared to pay salarie' commensurate with the scale generally in force elsewhere they will lose their teachers. It is e° use members of the Council, who have not made themselves thoroughly acquainted with educatiou requirements, deciding that the teachers are slit ciently well-paid, and refusing to sanction an; increase. The Education Committee is the or.aY competent judge in a professional matter this, and the scale of salaries, as submitted, shogie in our opinion have been unhesitatingly approved.; This sudden saving fit on the part of the Councl' was an unthinking and unwise econon3lii In all probability the Education Committee vn; refuse to be dictated to in this matter and a des"; lock may ensue which the Board of Education sw be called upon to end. We trust that those members of the Council who voted for the amend' ment will carefully reconsider their actiou,l especially in view of Alderman Fairclough statement that" any reduction in the expenditure on education which might lead to MefficieucY would involve a much more serious loss in g. refits which are approximately three times more tl3ee the amount raised by the rate."</p>
        <p>Economical as the Council desired to be 00 Tuesday it was compelled to sanction rates for the ensuing year which total 75.10 d. in the pound Ot an increase of 6d. in the pound. Only one pennio of the sixpenny increase is attributable to the e°Bl of Education, so that those people who are inclinqi to charge the educationists with extravagance scl; have to moderate the tone of their complain!' Really, the love of expending more Clan the° allowance which is characteristic of most of the Corporation Committees is responsible for ea° deficit on the past year and the increased call fot the next 12 months. If the Committee rigidly 0111 their coats according to the cloth at their dispogse there might be some hope of reducing the rates.: There is good reason to doubt whether the es.° mates as sanctioned will suffice for the prospect.il requirements. Ouuncillor Dodds, who has studio' the Budget :figures to some purpose, seriouslY questions this, and in the course of a letter in Or correspondence column he. points out that, seen:lg,, the Corporation commenced last year with a bair balance and finished with a deficit thelf expenditure actually exceeded the estimate for the ensuing 12 months by £4,109 16s. 4d. A glance at the Budget totale apparently shows that the Corporation is provid: ing £13,000 more than was required a year ago,blo an analysis of the figures disproves this assumption. Councillor Dodds throws much needed light 00 a subject—borough finance—which concerns everY layman but which every layman noes not under stand, and the ratepayers, will, we do not doubt, be grateful for his exposition of the status in To' That the practice of a wise economy in Warringt°. ° is an imperative necessity, Mr. Dodds makes 1.131 creasingly clear. In fact the Corporation villa not be able to pay its way unless a very tight hat is kept on the strings of the public purse. If voi be no mean achievement if the year terminate with a balance in the bank instead of, as at pre' sent, "duo to the bankers." Let us see what out local administrators can do when on their mettle</p>
        <p>Many of the owners of property in Warrington are evincing symptoms of alarm over the increase, of rates in the town. We learn that it is intendun, to hold a general meeting of the Property Owner°, Association, which is becoming a very powerful body, upon the question of the recent advancer with a view to consider how far rents are bein,g effected by these continual increases in the town rates. It is contended by many of the propeel owners, that although rates have gone up ved considerably during the past four years, there, has been no corresponding increase ot rents, ati'l that therefore they are being placed at a considerei able disadvantage. In fact some go further ali,r say property in Warrington is not worth "holding., One suggested remedy is that the tenants shoul°, be called upon to pay the rates direct, instead 9` as at the present time indirectly in the rents. This would, it is alleged and with very good reasu°' cause the inhabitants of the town to take nanr,e interest in the management of he town by their representatives, viz : the Town Councillors.</p>
        <p>There were two or 'three lively passages at are° in the course of the debate on Tuesday evening' and more than once decorum seemed to have flovgit to the winds. His Worship the Mayor has se himself a task which he might with advantage t()„ all modify. It is no use—if he will excuse ntl` making the suggestion—endeavouring to kee,P„ speakers to the minutes and allowing a litt;,," latitude at the same time. Many of them wilt given an inch take an ell, and it would probab.o conduce to order dud despatch if his Worsll,llls favoured the .Council with fewer remsrl',l and firmer decisions. He has a difficult tastcs.o; we can heartily sympathise with him. Durll:l2' one dispute Councillor Pemberton was told Councillor Wilkinson to "shut up" and really, 1</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <p>be had complied, it would have been an unmixed blessing. Even if the Electricity Committee had bought coal from the Sultan of Turkey last year, this had nothing to do with the estimates for the 'next twelve months, and the persistent member for Latchford ought to have been ruled out of order at once. He has an unpleasant habit of re- tailing a grievance ad nauseaum, which he had better rid himself of if he is ever to obtain a patient hearing in the Town Council.</p>
        <p>Economy is certainly in the air just now. The -Electricity and Tramways Committee is to be con- gratu;ated on taking she bull by the horns and making a considerable reduction in establishment -expenses. It has been discovered that the Depart- ment has been too generously staffed, and some 16 carmen and cleaners have received notice, while -other economies in management are being effected. Altogether the Committee hopes to save something like £1,500 a year, and at the same time maintain the efficiency of the electricity and tramways • service at its present high level. Branch Libraries have also been shelved owing to the plea of Poverty, but we hope the Council will soon see its Way to proceed with so admirable and inexpensive -a scheme as that formulated by the Museum Com- mittee. The Gymnasium Committee, too, has had 2,50 knocked off its estimate, and the local .athletes will have to do without•their cold shower after" bars and rings." It is to be hoped that in , looking so carefully after the hundreds' the lhonsands will not dribble away as they have done in the past.</p>
        <p>Whatever charge may be brought against the Tramways Committee it cannot be said that the members of it have acted harshly in the matter of the sir een men who have been discharged this week. They have each received a gratuity of an extra week's wages and efforts have been made in neighbouring towns to find them employment under some other Corporation or tramway corn- Pany. Two Corporation officials journeyed to Blackpool yesterday with this object in view, and we hope success will attend their efforts.</p>
        <p>The Howley Bridge scheme seems likely to be " hung up" for at least twelve months. Nothing was allowed for it in the estimates, and it is very unlikely that the Corporation will embark upon such an extensive scheme until the finances are in •a more buoyant state. The Bridges Sub-Coal- rnittee has already had the subject under consider- ation and the result of their deliberations is a recommendation to the General Purposes 'Committee to proceed with the construc- tion of cantilevers alongside Warrington Bridge and defer the construction of the Aowley Bridge to a more favourable time. The construction of Howley Bridge is a desideratum Which, we hope, wi I not be lost sight of altogether, for we believe many advantages will accrue to the Borough from a new bridge at that point. Coun- cillor Maginn has shown such a warm interest in the matter that he is not likely to allow it to •be indefinitely shelved on the ground of expense.</p>
        <p>There is a pleasant augury for Warrington Liberals in the result of the annual billiard matches 4whieh have just been held between the represen- tatives of the two political clubs. For the first time the Liberals hive secured'a majority of points -on the two contests, and, coincident with the revival of Liberalism all over the country,this'small Party victory may, we think, be not unreasonably looked upon as the forerunner of greater triumphs to come. Mr. Pierpoint will surely tremble in his seat when he reads of the latest Liberal achieve- ment in the town he is understood to represent. Many of his friends have already " gone wrong "on the fiscal question or Chinese labour,and now that supremacy in billiards has departed from the party they may have to play second fiddle at the polls. The wish is, of course, fattier to the thou ;ht. •</p>
        <p>Admiral Makaroff was singularly free from bureaucratic corruption. He wss popular, as a great leader should be, with every line of the ser- vice, and therein lies the traaedy,of the loss which the Russian Navy has suffered. High character, no less than great attainments, had won him res- pect, and the blow which has befallen Russian arms will he felt and realised in every quarter. That loss is irreparable. Admiral Makaroft's Courage was always conspicuous] v evident—indeed, the one provocation to recent criticism in Britian naval circles has been the freedom with which the Admiral again and again put out from , Port Arthur while a shrewd and wily enemy Was in the vicinity. The Admiral wrote with authority on Nelson and those methods of handling which distinguished the Victory, and his work on this topic, possessing, as it does notable technical qualities, is well known and valued. His ice-cutting steamer—built, by the bye, upon the Tyne—is to-day successfully employed during the winter on the Nova and in the Gulf of Finland carving channels for the passage of merchant vessels. lie was many times in England, and he visited the United States during the, time of the-World's Fair with a small squadron. He won distinction as a commander by dealing execution among the Turkish fleet in the Russo-Turkish War; and when Russia and Japan were at loggerheads in 1894—by a fatal irony over Port Arthur—be proceeded to 'Far Eastern waters with the Mediterranean fleet. His inechanicarand administrative genius were every- where acknowledged. Russia has lost her Lord Charles Beresford, for Makaroff possessed that dash in action and those social attributes which may be said to distinguish the most popular officer in the British Navy to-day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>Index to Inside Pages.</head>
        <p>Index to Inside Pages.</p>
        <p>PA.GE 2, Education Expenditure. Teachers' Salaries. Women's Chat and Fashions. "A Ghost Hunt " by Cutcliffe Hyne. 3. Events of the week (illustrated). Town Council. 6. The Budget. Rates for the Year Fixed. Trades Council Delegate's Report on Education. Police News. Silcoates School Burnt Down. Earlestown.Runcorn,and St. Helens News. 7. Football. The Weeks Matches. Mr. Balfour's Dumping.</p>
