      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>PROGRESSIVE WESLEYANISM.</head>
        <p>PROGRESSIVE WESLEYANISM.</p>
        <p>A Fourth Minister Decided Upon.</p>
        <p>At the last quarterly meeting of the Warrington Viresleyan Circuit. the question of appointing an ad- ditional minister for the circuit was discussefl and re- .erred to-a subsequent meeting. The adjourned meet- ing was held on Thursday evening, when the church Toom at Bold-street was crowded to its utmost capacity. The Rey. G. H. Carnson (superintendent of the circuit) 'Presided. The meeting was practically unanimous as to the urgent need of appointing a fourth minister, but there was a divided opinion as to whether the applica- tion should be made before the impending Synod of the Liverpool district, which is to be held at Warring- ton on May 10, 11 and 12. It was eventually decided, after a long discussion, to make the application this Year. If the proposal meets with the appro7al of the Synod, it is probable that a re-arrancement will be made of the local ministers' residences. One will most likely live at 80ld-street, one at Bewsey-road, and one each at Padgate and Penketh respectively. The Rev. John Gunnell (formerly superintendent of the Warrington -circuit) who has for many years efficiently filled the office df supernumerary at Penketh, is about to retire from that honoured position. The meeting of the Liverpool and District Wesleyan s'3'llod at Warrington in May next, will be an event of great importance m local Wesleyan circles. The sittings rill extend over three days and each night there will De a public gathering. There will be from 100 to 200 Ininisters in attendance.</p>
        <p>POOP Children's Treat, 1904. The committee met on Friday, Mrs. James Richard- .l,3°ll presiding. A. very satisfactory report and balance sheet was presented by the hon. treasurer. After being audited it was passed and a balance carried forward for next year. Hearty votes of thanks were given to Colonel Edelston for the use of the hall and to Mr. Morris for providing the excel- lent tea.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Off-License Holders' Association. The Warrington Branch of the Off-License Holders' Association was represented at the annual conference at Leeds, held on Thursday by Mr. William Throssell (president of the Warrington Association) and Mr. Robert Smith (treasurer.) FIRE GRATES of every description.—J. S. GAR- LAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street,Warringion.—Advt. People's Services. Owing to the exhibition in Parr Hall the Rev. H. Dunnico will preach for two Sundays in the Royal Court Theatre. The subject to-morrow evening. is the first of two or three sermons on "Is Christianity True?"</p>
        <p>Death of Mrs. Kenworthy. The death occurred at Cliffe View, Stockton Heath, on Sunday of Mrs. Kenworthy, widow of the late Rev. Samuel Kenworthy, formerly pastor of Hill Cliffe Baptist Chapel. She had been ailing for some time; and pneumonia was the immediate cause of death. She was 57 years of age. Her late husband was for seven years pastor at Hill Cliffe and was succeeded by the Rev. J. S. Hughes. The funeral took place on Wed- . nesday. the officiating minister being the Rev. J. A. Harris, of Stretford. A brief service was held in the house and the interment afterwards took place in the burial ground, Hill Cliffe. The relatives and friends present included the four sons: Mr. Arthur, Mr, James, Mr. Bradford and Mr. Lawrence Kenworthy, . Mr. T. Blackhurst, of Longton (brother of the deceased). Mr. A. Blackhurst of Tunstall (brother) Mr. R. Blackhurst (nephew), Mr. James Kenworthy. Mr. W. H. Bradford, Mr. B. Bradford and Mr. W. Arnold Bradford (sons-in-law), Mr. John Bradford, Mr. Johnson, Mr. J. Longland, Mr. Booth. A large 1 number of wreaths had been sent. ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS :MINERAL WATER S.—Advt. Marriage at Christ Church, Latchford. A quiet but pretty wedding, and one which aroused considerable interest, took place at Christ Church, Latchford, on Tuesday, in the presence of a large congregation. The contracting parties were Miss May Balharry, daughter of the late Mr. William Balharry, of Newton-le-Willows, and niece of Mr. John Fairclough, "The Grange," Latchford, and the Rev. H. Leslie Parker, chaplain of thern naval training ship "Conway." The church had been beautifully decora- ted with flowers and plants. The Rev. E. Lambert, M.A., of the Mersey Mission to Seamen, conductod the service, assisted by the Rev. G. C. Briggs,, M.A., Vicar of Christ Church. The bride, who was given away by her uncle, Mr. John Fairclough, was attended by one bridesmaid—Miss Lindsay Balharry. The Rev. A. Hobson, of Liverpool, discharged the duties of best man. Mr. T. L. Fry presided at the organ and played appropriate selections, including Smart's "Festive March " and Mendelssohn's "Wedding March." The reception was held at "The Grange," and later in the day the newly-married couple left for Donedale where the honeymoon is be- ing spent. Over 100 handsome presents were re- ceived, including a pair of silver candlesticks from Captain Broadbent, of "The Conway "a silver salver from the committee of the ship; a brass standard lamp from the junior chaplains ; and silver entree , dishes from the boys. PUBLIC BENEFIT. UNDER the CLOCK for BEST _ TIOCTS in LANCASHIRE.—Advt. The Proposed Memorial to Fallen Soldiers. The effort to raise funds for the proposed memorial to the men of the South Lancashire Regiment who fell in the South African campaign is now being taken up in earnest by the employees at some of the large k works. £1,500 is the amount required to erect a statue of the late Golonel O'Leary, of the old "Fighting Fortieth," and it is fitting that workingmen should have an opportunity of subscribing. A further meeting of the Workingmen's Committee was held at the Town Hall on Thursday, Mr. W. Haigh presiding in the absence of Mr. D. Davies. It was decided to appoint Mr. Haigh vice-chairman of the committee. The hon. secretary (Mr. C. J. Newton) reported that 18 works were not yet represented, and expressed the hope that they would soon see their way to join in the scheme, The employees of Messrs.Rylands Bros., Limited, had agreed that id. per week should be de- ducted from the wages of every man earning up to ..£l, and 3d. per week from the wages of those earn- ing over 30s. until £5O had been raised dt the works. An effort on similar lines would be made by the employees of the Whitecross Co., Ltd.— The hon. treasurer (Mr. Hope) said he had not yet received any subscriptions, but he felt sure that within the next six months at least £lOO or £.500 would be 4 raised. The employees at all the large works were taking up the matter heart and soul.—The Mayor has been elected president of the committee, and Messrs. Wadsworth, J. J. Nield and Charnock, vice-presidents. UNDER the CLOCK for GOOD BOOTS.—Advt. Burning Fatality.—A Sad Case. At the Police Station, yesterday, Mr. S. Brighouse, county coroner, held an inquiry into the circumstances attending the death of Annie V. Cann, aged 6 years, who resided with her parents at 7, Clara-street.— Deceased's mother stated that on Thursday afternoon of last week she left deceased and two younger chil- dren alone in the kitchen, while she went to make a purchase at a shop half a mile away. There . was, scarcely any fire in the grate. On return- ing half an hour later she found that deceased had sustained serious burns, and had beon taken to the Infirmary. She visited the institution, and deceased told her that she was putting some sticks on the fire when her pinafore got alight. The child died at 4.35 on Wednesday last.—ln answer to the Coroner, witness said there was no- fireguard in the kitchen. The Coroner said that in consequence of his remarks with reference to fatalities which occurred through the non-provision of fireguards, he • had been inundated with letters and circulars from all over Lancashire from Otentees and inventors of fireguards. He had a stereotyped reply to the effect that 1 he would be happy to meet those gentlemen at Orms-1 kirk any Thursday afternoon; when they could submit guards for his inspection. Recently a gentleman brought a most efficient fireguard, the cost of which was only is. 6d. It could be fixed so firmly that no child could possibly get to the fire.—Mrs. Schule, 18, IPercy street, stated that she saw deceased running from the passage at the roar of Clara street with her clothing on fire. With the assistance of neighbours witness extinguished the flames.—The jury returned a verdict of" Accidental death."—Detective Barlow , informed the Coroner that deceased's parents were in ' extremely poor circumstances —A juryman offered to provide the woman with a fireguard—lt was decided to make A collection for the family, the Coroner start- ing it with 10s. The jury increased this amount to 11.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>Personal.</head>
        <p>Personal.</p>
        <p>The many friends of Colonel Richardson (command- ing the 8-40th regimental district, and stationed at brford Barracks), who has for the last eleven days ben suffering from pneumonia, will be pleased to earn that he is now almost convalescent. _</p>
        <p>Major-General Sir Francis Howard, Inspector- General of recruiting, has been appointed to command the North-Western Military District, the headquarters of which are at Chester, in succession to Major- General Hallam Parr, who resigned on account of ill-health.</p>
        <p>At St. George's Church,Hanover -squdre,L-indon,on Tuesday, the marriage will be solemnized of Mr. Cyril Haslam, of Frodsham, Cheshire, and Miss Marjorie, younger daughter of the late Mr. Randolphe Pigott, J.P., of Grendon Hall, and Mrs. Pigott, of Bremley, Kent, and Grendon, Bucks. Mr. Haslam is associated with Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons Ltd., and since he came to reside in the district he has taken an interest in the Warrington Volunteer force more especially in connection with the cycling corp, H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC is the KING of TONICS.-63. SANKEY-STREET, Warrington.—Advt. A Local Composer. Mr. Fred Podmore, a local musician, has just pub- lished a festival hymn, entitled " Soldiers of Temper- ance," composed especially for the forthcoming Band of Hope Festival, and will be sung by a chorus of over 200 voices. Mr. Podmore is very popular as a com- poser, one of his hymn tunes " Evening Shadows " having been sung in many places of worship. It was originally composed for the Leeds Whit Festival, 1902. Another composition " Warrington March " will be played in the Park this season.</p>
        <p>H. M. ASHTON'S LrFrc TONIC is the KING OF TONICS.-63, SA.NKEY-STREET, Warrington.—Advt. Ragged School and Mission, Bank-street.</p>
        <p>On Sunday last, the Sunday school anniversary in connection with the above mission took place. ' In the afternoon the preacher was the Rev. H. Dannico, who spoke admirably of the good work of the school, and urged the workers to press on in spite of the criticism of those who took no interest in the poor children. In the evening the preacher was the Rev. B. Percival, vicar of St. Peter's, who urged the children to be firm to the wright things that were taught them there,and try to live better lives. Mr. H. Wright read the lesson. The children and friends rendered saitabh3 hymns at each service, assisted by an orchestra consisting of Messrs. T. Blakemore, W. Wright, j. Booth, and Whitfield. Mr. F. Owen led the children and Mr. Blakemore was at the organ. Solos were rendered by Miss M. Green and Miss E. Wright. The congrega- tions were good and the collections amounted to £3 4s. 6d.</p>
        <p>ASA for CIINNDTGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINFIRA WATERS.—Advt. St. John's Literary and Social Society. The members of this Society wound up the session of 1903-4 with a social evening on Tuesday last. The President, Mr. W. E. Rees, occupied the chair and during the evening read the secretary's and treasurer's reports. These, which showed the society to be in a flourishing condition, were unanimously adopted. The following were elected officials and committee for the ensuing year, viz :—Hon. President, the Rev. J. L. Milne, MA., 8.D., President, Mr. P. H. Brown, Vice- presidents, Messrs. D. R. Hodge and W. E. Rees, Treasurer, Mr. J. Bennett, Secretary, Mr. J. Robert- son, Committee,Mesdames Barlow,Brown and Hodge, and Messrs. J. Barlow, J. Hunter, and R. Watson. An excellent programme had been arranged for the evening, songs being sung by Mrs. Brown, Misses Chesworth and Hughes, and Messrs. Hepburn, Hunter and Robertson. During an interval in the proceed- ings tea was served by the ladies of the committee. Altogether a most enjoyable evening was spent.</p>
        <p>LADIES BEST GLACE BOOTS and SIIOES, UNDER the CLOCK, BRID GE-STREET.—Ae..7k. Fatal Accident to a Carter.</p>
        <p>Coroner Brighouse held an inquest at the Police Station yesterday, touching the -death of William Hyde, a carter in the employ of the Midland Railway Company, who resided at 59, Scott-street. Inspector Groves represented the company.—Robert Radcliffe, clerk, Earlestown, stated that at 3.25 on Tuesday afternoon he was in Wargrave-road, Earlestown, near the railway juaction, when he saw the deceased driving two horses attached to a heavily-laden lurry. A. passing train •caused the horse in the shafts to be- come restless, and the driver, who was sitting on the front of the vehicle, was in the act of getting off, when he overbalanced himself and fell against the front near wheel. He was knocked under the lurry, aa-d the back wheel passed over his chest. Assistance was obtained and deceased was removed to Dr. Handyside's surgery. After his injuries had been attended to, he was conveyed to the Warrington Infirmary in the horse ambulance.—Nurse Robinson said deceased was suffering from a scalp wound and internal injuries, and died at 12.35 on Wednesday morning.—A verdict of "Accidental death" was returned.</p>
        <p>BASSINETTES AND MAIL CARTS in latest art shades.—J. S. GARLAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Catholic Association. A re-union of past and present members of this association was held on Thursday. A company of about seventy sat down to supper and afterwards an exhibition of billiards was given by Messrs. J. Quinn, of Widnes, and D. Walsh of the home club. Mr. Quinn played a superb game and won. The Rev. Father T. 0. Swarbreck, 0.5.8., then presented the following prizes gained in the billiard handicap :-1, marble clock, Mr. John Durkin ; 2, g Ad-mounted umbrella; Mr. Thomas Newnes; 3, a selection of books, Mr. Alban Richardson; 4, handsome umbrella, Mr. Joseph Cunningham—The Rev. Father Mercer re- minded the members that they had a great work before them in Warrington in helping to better the social standing of their co-religionists. He suggested all the societies joining hands and opening a central Catholic Hall, where all could meet. —During the evening a capital musical programme was submitted by the Borough Band Prize Quartette, the Rev. Father Primavesi, Messrs J. Rabbit, Haslam, Gorst, O. Toole, J. Flynn, A. H. Cummings, T. Mullins, J. Gleason and Salmon.—A hearty vote of thanks was accorded to the artistes, and to Mr. Owen Toole who had generously provided the supper. Death of M. William Jones. It is with extreme regret that we have to announce the death of Mr. William Jones, which took place at his residence, 50, Bewsey-street, on Monday evening. Mr. Jones was 54 years of age, and had been in the service of the Town Council since the year 1880, first under the late School Attendance Committee as Assistant School Attendance Inspector, and, since 1895, as Chief Inspector. He was transferred to the service of the Education Committee on the Ist April last and during the whole of his service his work had been zealously and successfully carried out. Mr. Jones suffered from valvular disease of the heart for some time past, and had been more or less confined to his house since October last. His medical attend- ants were Dr. Wilfred Curtis and Dr. Reginald Smith. The deceased gentleman was a regular worshipper at St. Paul's Church. He leaves a widow, four sons, and one daughter.—The funeral took place at the Cemetery yesterday afternoon, the Rev. W. Brace- I camp officiating. The chief mourners were the I deceased's four sons : John W., Charles, George. and Arthur. There were also present :—Councillors , Ellison and Rogers, and Messrs. W. Bibby and E. ' Plinston (representing the Education Committee), Mr. J. Moore Murray (Director of Education), Mr., W. Flood (representing the Health department), Mr. W. T. Crank (Town Hall dApartment), Mr. H. Lawrence (Borough Treasurer's department), Messrs. Roberts, A.. S. ' Elliott and H. Parkinson (Education depart- ment), and Mr. J. H. Scarisbrick (School Attendanee department). The bearers were Messrs. W. Bamber, W. Rutter, T. Holbrook and W. Farrar (school attend- ance officers), J. Pharoah and E. Haigh (education office.) The wreaths included one from the school attendance officers, and one from the office staff of the Education Committee.</p>
        <p>CRAWFORD'S</p>
        <p>OVAL</p>
        <p>RICH TEA</p>
        <p>BISCUITS. (7n -Vb. Packets, Sze.)</p>
        <p>THE CE{WFORD" QUALITY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, April 16, 1904</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, April 16, 1904</p>
        <p>English Cup Final. For the convenienoe of residents in this district who desire to witness this tie, the London and North- Western Railway Company have arranged a cheap excursion to London, leaving Warrington at 1.3 on Saturday morning. There will also be special trains • from St. Helens, Widnes, Runcorn, and Earlestown. BaCE BOOTS FOR ALL.—UNDER THE CLOCK.—Advt Captain Crosfield at the Liberal Club. On Thursday, Captain G. R. Crosfield attended at the Liberal Club to present the prizes to the success- ful competitors in the New Year's billiard handicap. The gathering took place in the reading room, Mr. John Worrall presiding. In the course of the pro- ceedings, Captain Crosfield took the opportunity of explaining in a chatty way the proposals of the National Service League. He was attentively listened to and replied to several questions. At the close, Captain Crosfield was cordially thanked on the pro- position of Mr. S. W. Jeffery. The prize list was as, follows :—Case of cutlery, M. Cunningham; ring, W. Whittaker; pair of gold links in case, J. Davies; cruet, R. Kerr; case of carvers, S. Jeffrey; electro- plated teapot, J. Shaw; breakfast cruet and case of carvers, W. Ford or W. Woods. FOR CLASS A..ND CHINA. of every description try _ _ -</p>
        <p>J. S. GARLAND, Furnishing IronniOnger, si, 53, and 55, Bridge-street. Warrington.—Advt. Ragged School Soup Kitchen. This kitchen has been open again this week, the demand being even greatAr than before. The follow- ing are heartily thanked for gifts of meat :—Messrs. G-. and J. Bowcock,Peak, Mynekyme, Owen, Tunstall, G. Cross,Mr. Finnerty, J. Shaw,Singleton, and a large quantity of swedes from Mr. Bennett, of Padgate. Mr. James Harding, m ire weaver, has given 100 loaves. Mr. William Furnival sent 3 dozen cans to give to those who had nothing better than empty jam jars to carry the soup away in. The money received almost defrays the total outlay during the time the soup kitchen has been open. The distribution will be continued for 2or 3 days next week at the ex- pense of the school funds, and then, unless further help be forthcoming, will close.</p>
        <p>The May Horse Parade. As the Fair Ground behind the Blue Coat School will not be available f)r May 21st, the judging in connection with this parade will take place in the field at Arpley between the cricket ground, and Arpley Station. It has been decided by the sub-committee, that, in order to encourage farmers to join in the parade to be held on Saturday,May 21st,special prizes shall be given to that class. A silver cup will be awarded to the owner of the best horse, and a silver medal presented by Mr. E. Barrows will be awarded to the owner of the second bast horse entered for com- petition. R.S P.C.A. diplomas first, second, and third prizes, as well as highly commanded and commended cards, which carry with them a money prize, will be (riven to the drivers for cleanliness at the discretion of the judges.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>IN AND AROUND WARRINGTON. Our Local Parliament.</head>
        <p>IN AND AROUND WARRINGTON. Our Local Parliament.</p>
        <p>BY THE FREE CRITIC.</p>
        <p>Our Local Parliament, better known as the War- rington Town Council, held their usual lively pro- ceedings last Tuesday. As I have on my previous visits to the Town Hall been somewhat interested if not altogether edified, I thought I could while away a few hours last Tuesday evening by listening to the cream of the town (for I opine that the electors only select those who are or should be the cream of the ratepayers,to represent them) debate and pass resolu- tions for the benefit of the" good old town" and all that dwell therein. After spending a few hours in the gilded chamber, I came to the conclusion that last Tuesday's meeting, to use a common phras2,about "took the biscuit "for most of the time was taken up by wrangling, personalities, and unnecessary explana- tions.</p>
        <p>•To say the least of it,many of our Town Councillors lack manners, an:: it would perhaps shock many to hear that one of the best mannered gentlemen of our local parliament is the workingman's representative, namely, Councillor Stott. The Council is also remark- ably short of good debating power and probably the number of good speakers can be counted on the fingers of one's own hand. Humour is also sadly lacking,and the importation of two good tempered and humorous speakers into the Council would be a distinct gain and a blessing. Most of the speakers have the habit of wandering sway from the business under discussion and the only two who appeared to keep to the points last Tuesday were Councillors Dodds and Stott. Perhaps our Mayor will excuse my criticising too hi conduct in the chair. It was a mixture of firmness and looseness. He would at one time keep the speakers to the subject, at an )ther time allow them to wander, at others apparently attempt to stifle dis- cussion, and at another time give a free hand. It was a most extraordinary method of conducting a Town Council meeting, and it did not tend to bring harmony into the Chamber. In justice however to the May Ir, it must be said that the disorder that occurred was not caused by his chairmanship, but arose from the evident lack of knowledge by most of the Councillors of" How the business of a Town Council meeting should be conducted" and also as to the rules of debate. _ _ _ .</p>
        <p>I hops our Councillors will take my criticisms in a. friendly spirit, for I feel they will themselves be con- scious that they can improve on their display of last Tuesday, otherwise their bast friends and the electors will be deceived.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>RECEPTION AT THE PARR HALL. Sir. Gilbert anti Lady &amp;mail Entertain</head>
        <p>RECEPTION AT THE PARR HALL. Sir. Gilbert anti Lady &amp;mail Entertain</p>
        <p>Conservative Workers.</p>
        <p>A large assembly of Warrington Conservatives was indebted to Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bt., and Lady Greenall for a pleasant evening's entertainment at the Parr Hall on Thursday. ' Ths function took the form of a reception, and there was a hearty response to the 1,600 invitations issued to members of the Conservative Associations and clubs and their wives and friends. The hall had been exquisitely decorated by Mr. Ralph Johnson, and there was a profuse display of " the red, white and blue." The effect was heightened by the introduction of choice flowers and plants from Walton Hall. The guests entered the hall by a covered way. electrically illuminated, and were received by Sir Gilbert and Lady -Greenall, Mr. Robert Pierpoint, M.P., and Mrs. Pierpoint. Lady Greenall was wearing the diamond star presented by Warrington workingmen to the late Lady Greenall. Amongst those who attended were :—Mr. and Mrs. John White,Mr. and Mrs. Henry Greenall, Mr. and Mrs. J. E, Seeker, Mr. and Mrs. William Tinsley, Mr. and Mrs. W. E. Atkin, the Revs. E. C. E. Carleton, W. Bracecamp and T. W. Barber, Aldermen Fair dough, Sutton, Smethurst, Bennett, and Hutchinson, Councillors Hulse, Roberts, Ashcroft, Pemberton, Thornton and Lord. After greetings had been exchanged, the guests were free to spend the evening in mutual intercourse and in listening to a high-class musical programme submitted by well-known artistes. The Carmen Sylva Ladies' Orchestra played light selections with taste and finish, and individual members added to the enjoyment of the audience with cleverly executed violin and 'cello solos. There were also a couple of acceptable baritone songs by "a gentleman friend of the orchestra." Mr. Leslie Harris provided the humour of the evening, and fully maintained his high reputation. He had little di(liculty in raising a Chorus in his "Laughing song," for the audience could not restrain their mirth. A piquant rendering of the sweet duet " Prithee Pretty Maiden," by Miss Anna Park and Mr. Leslie Harris was one of the best items of a programme , which was interesting throughout. During an interval there was a little speechqfiaking, but no attempt to go into serious politics. Mr. PIERPOINT said that during the many years he had been most intimately connected with the Conserva- tive party in his native town, he had been at many gatherings, but he had never been at a social gathering connected with the party so large, so pleasant, and so thorough as their meeting that night. That meet- , in,, they owed to, the generosity and courtesy of° Sir Gilbert and Lady Greenall. (Cheers.) He rejoiced to say that from the time when he first sat in Parliament in 1892 up to the present, his majorities had increased at every election, and he trusted and believed that he would have nothing to complain of in the future. Mr. Pierpoint urged all his -hearers to work for the "good cause" as though an election wen; going to COM very soon, although he confessed that he had no expectation that it would come very soon. (Hear, hear.) They must always be ready for the time whenever it. came. In conclusion Mr. Pierpoint moved a hearty vote of thanks to Sir Gilbert and Lady Greenall for their hospitality. ' Mr. J. H. BOTTOMLEY delivered an address in which he commented upon speeches recently made by Mr. A. H. Crosfleld and Mr. Pierpoint, and contended that their member was more than a match for his opponent. The vote of thanks was carried with great enthusiasm -and Sir Gilbert briefly responded. 1 During the evening refreshments were served, the catering being in the hands of Mr. S. Woodcock, of Grappenhall. The arrangements had been delegated to a catering committee, with Alderman Prancomb as 1 chairman, and a room and entertainment committee I presided over by Mr. E. Draper.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>NATIONAL SERVICE-LEM, ITS AIMS AND OBJECTS EXPOUNDED.</head>
        <p>NATIONAL SERVICE-LEM, ITS AIMS AND OBJECTS EXPOUNDED.</p>
        <p>Meeting in the Parr Hall Last Evening.</p>
        <p>SPEECHES BY MR. F. W MONKS, LORD</p>
        <p>NEWTON AND OTHERS.</p>
        <p>Whatever views Warrington people may hold on the aims and objects of the National Service League, they cannot plead that they have not had an opportunity of hearing them expounded. In Captain Crosfield, the League has a zealous local secretary, and he has organised in conjunction with Mr. Haslam, a series of meetings in the town and district, with the object of educating public opinion on the subject of compulsory military training. A. well attended public meeting was held in the Parr Hall last evening, when some interesting speeches were delivered in further explana- tion of the subject. Mr. F. W. MONKS presided and he was accompanied on the platform by Lord Newton, Mr. Shea (secretary of the League), Colonel Ridgway, Major Roome, Mr. H. Roberts J.P. Dr. Markel, Dr. Westmacott, Professor Ross, Colonel Mac Fie, Colonel Pollitt, Mr. Peter Rylands, Mr. Milne, Captain Bundock, Colonel Edelsten, Mr. C. Maclver, Rev. Canon Willis, Rev. W. Bracecamp, Captain Croslleld and Mr. Haslam (hon. secretaries of the Warrington 4ranch). During the period of waiting for the meeting to com- mence, Mr. W. H. Payton Mus. Bac., played a selection of music on the organ.. . _ _</p>
        <p>The who had a hearty reception, said letters of apology had been received from Mr. Lyon (president of the Warrington Branch of the National Service League), Mr. Robert Pierpoint, M.P., Mr. A. H. Crosfteld, Sir Gilbert Greenell, Sic Alfred Jones (Liver- pool), Colonel Cornwall Legh, Mr. Charles E. Parker. Proceeding the Chaif man said he was not occupying that position as a member of the league or because he knew much about it, but rather because he really knew nothing. To him that meeting was an opportunity of learning something of the proposals which the league desired to put forward. There was a strong feeling on the part of many people against any kind of compulsory military service in this country. It was thought that we had managed to get along very well with our present military system for so many years and it was quite unnecessary to establish what some were pleased to call such severe measures as compulsory service. That to him was rather an argument on the other side, because it did not follow that beeause we had got along for so many years satis- factorily that we shouldcontinue to do so in the future. The conditions here and all over the world were con- stantly changing, and changed conditions rather sugges- ted to him new methods. (Hear, hear.) The establish- ment of this league and the proposals put forward, backed as it was by so many influential names was in itself a very strong recommendation to these proposals. In connection with military proposals one naturally connected them with war and the evils that followed in the train of war. As far as he understoodj the proposals of the league there was no tendency towards increasing the risks of war but rather on the other hand the avoidance of war —(hear, hear)—and the more we could avoid war the better it was for this country. It was because he thought the proposals would help to maintain a lasting peace that he would suggest that they ehould receive the earnest consideration of everyone in the country,. During a period of peace there was time and opportun- ity to discuss these matters. For himself he thought that a certain amount of military drill and discipline was good for every young man. (Hear, hear) He had seen the beneficial effects of discipline amongst the people, more particularly amongst continental nations, who had found it a direct benefit not only from a mili- tary point of view but also from a physical point of view (Hear, hear.) He only wished people would realise their own responsibilities in carrying on the work of this great empire. We had as a nation stood in the van of civilization and progress and he wouldlike to think that we should be able to maintainthat posi- tion. (Applause.) Mr. SHEE, who had a cordial reeeption, moved the following resolution :—" That this public meeting of townsmen is of opinion that no scheme of army, reform which does not provide for a moderate form of com- pulsory military training for all the able-bodied young men in these islands can be considered satisfactory or adequate for the safety of the nation ; the meeting is also of opinion that the matter is one of urgent national importance (yid requires the immediate attention of His Majesty's Government ; and that our Borough Member be requested to convey this resolution to the Prime Minister." He pointed out that the matter they were called together to con- sider was in every way the !nest important that could be placed before the people of this country. That was perhaps a bold statement to make in view of the great subjects which had been put before the country in recent months, and which had been so hotly debated on both sides. Bat this subject of compulsory military service went to the very rout of national life in the sense of giving us—or striving to give us—national security, national health and national happiness. (Hear, hear.) He hoped to succeed in persuading many of them that the idea deserved the support of 1 every thinking Englishman or English woman, of whatever party, creed or class of society he or she might belong to, because they were dealing not with a party question but one that was above and outside party—one that was essentially national. (Hear; hear.) Li detailing what forces were necessary for national defence, Mr. Shea said we needed not only a supreme aavy with a great naval reserve and a highly efficient or professional army, but also a great national reserve of trained men, not enlisted as soldiers but enlisted for a time, making themselves efficient and ready to serve their country in time of need—ready te go forward in time of war, bit instead of having no other stock in trade than goodwill, having the a.b.c. of military training behind them and able to step into the ranks in time of need. This was a practical and feasible scheme—one that should appeal to every reasonable and patriotic Englishman. It might be difficult to get people at' the present moment to appreciate the urgent necessity of the scheme, more especially when the ink was hardly dry on the treaty with France, which we§ one step forward in the security of peace, which they all desired, and which the National Service League desired to forward. (Hear,hear.)At the same time there was in the Far East a tremendous war going on between two great powers in which there was for every thought- ful man, a lesson in the dangers that lay hidden in a false security that relied upon tradition instead of upon the efficiency and earnest endeavour of the citizens in serving their country. (Hear, hear.) In referring to our position during the Boer war, Mr. Shea said there was then nothing in this country but a nation, untrained and unarmed. It was a terrible and dangerous state af things, and one that ought not to exist. The voluntary system of recruiting our army, he contended, had been gradually and steadily breaking down. Whereas in 1899, thearmy was 75,000 men below what was asked for in 1904 it was 191,000 below the required number. It was not altogether a question of nnrnbers, but a question of the quality of men we got under a voluntary system. There had been a marked deterioration in the physical standard of our soldiers for many years past, and the requisite chest measurement and height of recruits had been reduced time after time. The expense too of the vol- untary system was enormons, due to the wastage caused by disease, inefficiency and non-effective service. The Militia and Volunteer services were also below strength. Under the existing system,the Volunteer was called up- on to do the work of ten citizens and make many sacri- fices. Sometimes he had to leave his work in order to put in the number of drills required, and when he went away for the annual training he perhaps lost his employ- ment. This duty which was undertaken voluntarily by so many men should be undertaken by every patriotic and in fact every Englishman. (Hear,hear). It was essentially a system of compulsory volunteering that the National Service League aimed at. Perhaps in the minds of some present there lurked the terrible bogey of conscription. The League aimed at warding off that bogey. Conscription was the absolute enrol- ment of citizens in regular forces and nearly always with the permission of the well-to-do to buy substitutes among their poorer fellows. That was a system which was contrary to all justice and all right and was a system which the National Service League opposed. Mr. Shee pointed out that what the League advocated , was more on the lines of the Swiss military system. 1 Every boy and girl in Switzerland went through a course of physical training and development. When the youth was 20 years of age he was called upon to do his first real service which in the infantry was 45 days' training. He then entered the active service in which he remained for 13 years, and was called up every second year to do 14 to 16 days' training. There was not a citizen in Switzerland who did not feel that he was a a di, important to his country: and tied to it by honourable bonds of patriotism. The proposals made by the National Service League were not something new and un-English, but were abso- lutely historic and In accord with the spirit of the English people. In effect, the proposals were a return to the ancient system of militia which obtained in this country centuries ago. One hundred and two days taken from the life of an Englishman for the service of his country, and for rendering himself capable of defend- ing it in time of need was not a great thing to ask in return for the privileges of British citizenship. It was not simply a question of necessity or of sacrifice, but more a question of the advantages which would follow such sacrifice. He contended that, if the scheme pro- posed were carried out, weishould secure that peace and security which were seriously needed. We should free the regular army and navy for important service, and have behind them a great reserve of men able to go forward in time of need to the help of their country. Further we should improve the physique of the nation enormously. The training would develop the physical capacity of the manhood of the nation, without the men losing anything by it except the time they gave in fulfilling a patriotic duty and making them- selves better citizens and better workers. In a nation like our own, so largely engaged in industrial employ-</p>
        <p>Tifbenk</p>
        <p>some n'tcbndt tdpthb AgIIY.IiOS of physical deterioration which were at work. By this scheme of national service, Mr. Shoe contended that' they would supply the 111139.11 S of physical regeneration which were so urgently needed. %%That could be grander or nobler than to let all men serve under a common flag and under the commit ideal of the defence of their country? Surely the people of this country must be beginning to realise the enormous responsibilities upon them. He felt the time had come when this nation would realise that in order to maintain its position and its greatness it must return to the old idea of personal sacrifice to King and country. (Applause.) Lord NEWTON seconded the resolution. and observed that the question of national defence was an extremely simple one. It was a question which was within the intellectual capacity of the ordinary individual and he would go almost as far as to say that any fool could understand it if he tried. (Laughter.) Referring to .the auxiliary forces he said they were composed of those persons—a small proportion of the populace—who were prepared from patriotic motives to take upon themselves the burden of the general community. (Hear, hear.) It was clear to him that the great obstacle to reform was the existence of these patriotic men because so long as this empire found a limited number of men who were kind enough and patriotic enoughto discharge the whole duty of the community so long would this anornalyand absurdity continue. To his mind the best and wisest and most patriotic thing these men could do would be to resign in a body and then the Government would be obliged to find a way out of the difficulty and the nation brought to its senses. (Hear, hear.) The voluntary system, so far as this country was concerned, was played out and evely other civilised nation had discovered it The difficulty in this country was not to be solved by the establishment of rifle clubs or giving; everybody aslouch hat or even by making ladies mem- bers of rifle clubs. (Laughter.) Why did we refuse to face plain facts? The moral of all this was that as a nation advanced in progress, in civilization and in comfort, a military career ceased to attract. jt was absolutely absurd to suppose that by reas- ing the soldiers' pay by an infinitesimal amount, we should overcome the difficulties of recruiting. Already we were paying four times as mach for the British ,1 soldier as was paid for a Continental solder and he would be a bold man to say that the British soldier was worth four European soldiers. (Laughter.) Home defence was the duty of able-bodied inhabitants and the daty should no longer remain optional. There was compulsion in regard to taxes, rates, sanitary matters, and education, and it was quite as essential that there should be compulsion in regard to military servioe for home defence. (Hear, hear). The League believed this could be clone with the minimum amount of inconvenience to the individual. Ha had sufficient confidence in his fellow countrymen to believe that they would be willing to undertake the moderate sacrifice demanded of them. (Applause.) Professor Ross then moved the sceond resolution which read as follows : "That this meeting is heart by in sympathy with the objects aimed at by the National Service League and pledges itself to further them by every possible means, and to obtain recruits." He said he would just like to impress one more point and that was the physique of the public. Ile pointed out that in the last 103 years the conditions of life in Great Britain had materially changed. Mr. P. W. MONKS, before putting the resolution to the meeting, said the first local branch which was estab- lished in the provinces was the local branch of War- rington. Votes of thanks to the speakers and chairman were carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>STOCKTON HEA</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEA</p>
        <p>THE WEDEMING Or OLD LONDON Ron.—The palling down of Bea-3onsfield-terrace and the shop and dwelling- house adjoining,and recently occupied by Miss Welsby, is expected to be completed to-day.</p>
        <p>FURTHRR EXPECrED DgMOLITION OF C3TrA.GES.-It is expected also that the old cottages at the corner of Grappenhall-ratd and Lou ion-road will ba pulled down in the course of the next few weeks, this being the site of the intended new handsome shil)ps with arcades to be erected for Mr. Egerton Warburton, of Arley. ' FURIOUS DRIVING.-At the Dareshury Petty Sessions on Tuesday, William Thomas Stephens, Elaine street, Warrington, was summoned for furiously driving a horse and cab in Old London road, Stockton Heath, on Feb- ruary 15th. Acting Sergeant Bowyer stated that defend- ant drove so recklessly that the cab collided with the gate at the swing bridge. Although witness called out to him he would not stop, but urged the horse on.—Do- fendent said the vehicle did not collide with the gate. The chairman (Colonel Ridgway) said such driving might have resulted in a serious accident, and defend- ant would have to pay 10s. and costs. PAROCHIAL COMVIITTEE.</p>
        <p>The concluding meeting of the year was held on Thursday evening, Mr. G. Piling presiding. There were also present:—Aessrs. Whitley, Bouchier, Pear- son, Mclntyre, Mountield, Clarke, G. F. Ashton (clerk), J. Swinton (surveyor), and J. Farrington (inspector of nuisances). The only business was the reading of a letter from the Ship Canal Company stating that they had given Instructions for the paving of the footpath adjoining the Swing Bridge with concrete flags. Mr. Whitley said it was only right and proper that they should shoci4 their apprviation of the services rendered by Mr. Pilling as chairman during the past year. (Hear,-hear.) He had conducted the business ma most estimable, gentlemanly courteous, and businesslike manner; and he (Mr Whitley) moved that a hearty vote of thanks be accorded to him. —Mr. Pearson seconded and expressed the hope that Mr. Pilling would be their chairman for the ensuing year.—Messrs. Bouchier and Clarke supported the motion, which was cordially passed, and Mr. Pilling briefly acknowledged the compliment. ,ACCIDENT.—Price Sheen, a drayman, in the employ of Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co., and residing at 21, Gaskell-street, met with a serious accident at Frodsham, on Monday. He was driving through the village, when he fell from the lurry, and the wheels pissed over him. He was attended by a doctor at Frodsham and afterwards conveyed to the Warrington Infirmary, where it was found that he was suffering from internal injuries. On inquiry yesterday we were informed that he was still seriously ill. PARISH COUNCIL. _ -</p>
        <p>Tie first meting of the newly-ele3ted Council was held last evening. There were present :—Messrs. Clarke, Bouchier, Whitley, Mclntyre. Hallows, War- burton, T. R. Westbrook, Fairhurst, and E. Bradshaw (clerk.) —Mr. Clarke was unanimously re-elected chair- min. Messrs. Whitley and Fairhurst were ra-ele3ted overseers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>PROPOSED ELECTRIC SUPPLY.</head>
        <p>PROPOSED ELECTRIC SUPPLY.</p>
        <p>The Clerk reported the receipt of a letter from the Warrington Corporation, stating that they had been approached on the subject of supplying Stockton Heath and adjoining districts with electricity for lighting and power purposes. To do this w3uld raquire Parliament- ary powers, and before considering .the nutter further they would 133 glad to hear th 3 views of the Parish Council.</p>
        <p>The Catlin-Ilan palatal out that th 3 Chaihira Electri city and Power Gas Bill, which had been promoted with the object of supplying all the districts in Cheshire with electricity, WAS at present before Parliament. The Warrington Corporation were protected by a clause which gave them the power to apply to Parlia- ment for power to supply electricity. If they obtained the approval of Stockton Heath the Corporation in- tended to promote a Bill.</p>
        <p>It was de-cided to obtain a copy of the Cheshire Elec- tricity and Power Gas Bill,and a committee, consisting of Messrs. Clarke, Bouchier, Warburton, and IL J. Westbrook, was appointed to consider the details. A private meeting of the Council will also be held to draw up a reply to the Corporation's letter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>COST OP THE ELECTION.</head>
        <p>COST OP THE ELECTION.</p>
        <p>The Chairman announced that the cost of the recent Parish Council election was £2O 1.55. id. and the Parish Council had to provide half of that amount. Mr. H. J. Westbrook: Unfortunately the gentlemen who ought to have listened to that bill are not here. (Laughter.) Mr. Pearson: I don't think it is unfortunate. (Laughter.) . It was decided to issue a precept for £l5 to cover the election and general expenses.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>FOOTPATH TO BE CLOSED.</head>
        <p>FOOTPATH TO BE CLOSED.</p>
        <p>The Council granted Messrs. Greensll, Whitley and Company permission to close a footpath leading from Fairfield-road to Mill-lane, on the understanding that the Company substitute a 6ft, footpath forming part of a new roadway running in the same direction.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON CONSERVATIVE ASSOCIATION. Annual Meeting. The annual meeting of this association was held last evening under the presidency of Sir Gilbert Greenell, Bart. • Alderman SUTTON submitted the annual report in which it was stated that their position was considered perfectly satisfactory. The association was in a firmer position than everand the clubs continued to prosper. Alderman BENNETT, hon. treasurer, gave his report on the finances which showed a satisfactory position. He pointed out, however, that although the population was increasing and Conservatism was not losing its hold, their subscription list showed a continued tendency to decline. Out of all the enthusiastic Conservatives in Warrington only 45 put their hands in their pockets to help them in their financial position. The reports were adopted. Sir GILBERT GREENALL was unanimously re-elected president for the ensuing year; Mr. R. Pierpoint. M.P., and Mr. J. C. Parr, vice-president ; Alderman Sutton, secretary; Alderman Bennett treasurer; Alderman Francomb and Alderman Bennett, delegates to the National Council. Mr. R. PIERPOINT, M.P.,'deiivered an address and alluded to the "thorny subject" of Chinese labour, which he advocated in the interests of the country. For every ten natives or Chinamen who were employed in unskilled labour in the mines, three white skilled men would be required to superintend. As soon as the shortage could be filled up, it would be so much the better for the wealth and the labour of the country.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>WARRINGTON PUBLICAN'S CLAIM.</head>
        <p>A WARRINGTON PUBLICAN'S CLAIM.</p>
        <p>Au injured Son and His Wages.</p>
        <p>INTERESTING CASE.</p>
        <p>At the Wigan County Court, an interesting case was heard before his Honour Judge Bradley, in which John Henry Teece, landlord of the White Hart, Market- place, Warrington, claimed the sum of £l4 Is. 2d. wages alleged to be due from the Corlett Electrical Engineer- ing Company Limited, of Rowbottom-square, Wigan. The claim was made up of eleven weeks' wages due to his son at £1 3s. a week, from 3rd December, 1903, to 19th February, 1904, amounting. to £l2 135., and 18s. 2d. costs. Mr. Madden, barrister (instructed by Mr. .B. Fox, solicitor of Warrington), represented the claimant and Mr. Allen (Messrs. Peace and Ellis, Wigan), appeared for the defendant company. Mr. Madden, in stating the case, said the plaintiff was a young man, 23 years old. About July, 1902, he was induced by a relative, a brother-in-law, to become con- nected with the defendant company. At that time he was 21 and was living in Warrington, and he was engaged to work at wages of 265. a week. He was in reality an apprentice, picking up a knowledge of the business. He worked at Newcastle at these wages until Easter 1903, when he was informed by Mr. Kirk, the manager there, that his wages had been a mistake, and that he wouli have to be put on the footing of an ordinary apprentice, and get 15s. a week. He worked until July 1903, when his wages were raised to Li a week, with sixpence a day expenses, so that at the time of the accident he was receiving 235. a week. On the 20th November he was putting a small installation into a house about ten miles from Newcastle when a piece of his chisel flew off and struck him in the left eye, and, in consequence of thataccident, the father had incurred some £3O or £4O expenses with specialists and other things. The claimant was engaged at a weekly wage, and he had had notice, and the point was whether the defendant company were not obliged to give the claimant notice before they could escape responsibility. Mr. Allen said the question was whether they were in law liable, and whether the case was covered by any act of Parliament.</p>
        <p>John Harry Teece, who received the injury, said that from the time he got 20s. a week he was paid sixpence a day for expenses, in addition, making 235. a week. He stayed away from work the day after the accident, and the day following Mr. Kirk, the manager, came to see him when he told him to rest away from work. His in- jured eye began to get worse,and he went and told Mr. Kirk, whereupon Mr. Kirk said, "What do you suggest?' Witness replied, "I thought of going home," and Mr. Kirk assented, saying "Yes, that will be the best." Then Mr. Kirksaid, "Oh, you will want your wages for this week," and went into the other office and got the wages for him. Mr. Kirk then asked him, "Oh, what about coming back?" Witness said," As soon as my eye is better," and then Mr. Kirk said, " That will do nicely." Witness's tool box was still at Newcastle. He was eleven weeks under the doctor, and his eye wasn't right yet. John Henry ....eece, licensed victualler, of Warring- ton, said that at the beginning of this year he wrote to the company asking for compensation, and as he didn't get a satisfactory reply he went to see Mr. Corlett, the managing director. When he found he could not claim under the Workmen's Compensation Act, he went to Mr. Corlett to see what the company coulei do for him. Mr. Corlett said he would write to the insurance com- pany, and see what they had to say, and later he wrote to tell him that the insurance company could not do any- thing. Mr. Corlett said to witness, "1 suppose your son will come back when he is ready to work?" and witness replied "Yes." Mr. Corlett then asked witness if he would like him to be at Wigan so that he could go home to Warrington at the week-ends, and witness said he would, whereupon Mr. Corlett said he would see what he could do.</p>
        <p>Mr. Madden at this point read a letter from the corn- pany saying there was no opening in Wigan and asking the complainant to put himself in communication with Mr. Kirk, at Newcastle. Witness said be went to see Mr. Kirk, of Wigan, who said, "If you will communicate with my brother when he is ready to go back, I am sure his wages will be all right, but they will have to come through as wages and not as a gift.'; His Honour : The question is whether there is any liability.</p>
        <p>Mr. Madden said it was a continuing liability until the defendant was dismissed by notice. _ _</p>
        <p>His Honour: The que-s-tTon-ls";i;i-her the defendant is entitled to go on receiving his wages. Mr. Madden quoted cases in point at some length, and contended that notice must be served to terminate</p>
        <p>[ability,</p>
        <p>Mr. Allen said that that would really mean that in every little case of accident they would have to run after a man and give him notice, but there was no pro- vision that he knew of which said that that was neces- sary.</p>
        <p>His Honour: The question here is whether the incapacity put an end to the contract. • You say it did. Mr. Allen: I say it did. ; His Honour : Your point is this. That this man having by the accident become incapable of doing work, that that in itself, without anything more, put an end to the contract, and, therefore, he cannot sue for wages. Mr. Allen : Yes. I think it only right to say that so far as the directors of this company are concerned it is not a question of sympathy. If it was,there would have been no reason for the case to come into Court.</p>
        <p>His Honour: As soon as a case comes here the ques- tion of sympathy and all that sort of thing goes, and it is a question of legal liability only. Ultimately His Honour said he would look up cases bearing upon the matter, and give judgment at a later date.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>BILLIARDS.</head>
        <p>BILLIARDS.</p>
