      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>LIBERAL CLUB v. CONSERVATIVE CLUB.</head>
        <p>LIBERAL CLUB v. CONSERVATIVE CLUB.</p>
        <p>The return match betweenteams representing the above clubs took place at the Conservative Club on Wednes- day evening. A very keen struggle was witnessed, the home team striving hard to wipe out the adverse majority of 321 they sustained at the Liberal Club a. fortnight earlier. At one period of the evening several heavy defeats to Liberal players looked like giving the victory to the Sankey-street team but later on the visi- ting players showed such stubbOrness that they suc- ceeded in finishing with an advantage of 30 points on the two matches. This is, we understand, the first time the Liberals have 'succeeded in defeating their political opponents at billiards, though at bowls they have been more successful. At the conclusion of the play on Wednesday, Councillor Tinnion thanked the Conservatives for their hospitality in suitable terms, and Mr. T. Horrabin replied. Gratification was expressed all round at the resumption of these friendly matches, and cheers for both winners and losers terminated the proceedings. The individual scores are as follow :</p>
        <p>LIBERALS. CONSERVATIVES. Dr. Smith 9l T. E Sangster l5O E. V. Walker R. Woods l5O C.Oldfleld 144 J. Halliwell 142 F. Redfern W. Woods • 132 F. Aukland E. Wilson S. J. Beaman J. McKenzie W. Whittaker J. Davies G. Baker C. Jordan 123 J. Sampson l5O G. Darton l4-0 T. Sutton 144 E. J. Mitchell l5O D Bawling J. Jennings - 2211 1920</p>
        <p>1920</p>
        <p>Cons. majority... 291 On the two games the Liberals have a majority of 30 points, having defeated their Conservative friends by 321 points at the Liberal Club. .</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON CONSERVATIVE CLUB V. SOUTHPORT CONSERVATIVE CLUB.</p>
        <p>The representatives of these clubs met at the Sankey street-club on Saturday. Scores :</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON. SOUTHPORT. F. H. Aukland l4l F. PAge l5O J. Podmore T. Sutton T. Horrabin D. Rawlings l5O F. Taylor lO6 T. Cooper l5O W. Hall 35 F. Redfern W. Taylor l5O A. Winstanley lOB T. E. Sangster F. Barber l5O C Archer F. Fieldhouse l5O A. A. W. Phillip, l2B J. W. Bullen - 1414</p>
        <p>1723</p>
        <p>Warrington thus won by 309 points. In the matcW;ii. Southport, Warrington lost by 246, but. on the • two games they had a majority of 63. .</p>
        <p>Sir Gilbert's Foxhounds on the Stage.</p>
        <p>The novel sight of foxhounds on the stage was witnessed at Grantham on Thursday night on the occasion of the popular comedy opera Dorothy," played by the local Amateur Operatic Company. Sir Gilbert Greenall gave his consent for ten couples of the famous Belvoir Hounds to figure in the well- known scene. They were led on to the stage during the second act by Ben Capell, the huntsman to the famous pack, and their appearance was greeted with tremendous enthusiasm. The proceeds of the enter- tainment were in aid of the Grantham Farmers Benevolent Institution.</p>
        <p>6 .._ . ~ :.,..„...,;, ~.i.,.:,..;,,,,,_,„,„:„. . ,_,,:,..,5....„:„.41.,._ ~,„...,,„,,</p>
