      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>"TB AN ILL WIND."-</p>
        <p>The old saying had another practical illustration on Saturday evening. Jane Murdock Ritchie, a married woman, residing at 17, Mill-lane, visited the Warrington Market Hall and purloined a silk blouse from the stall of Thomas Leigh, a draper. %Hastily concealing it under her cape, she was making good her escape by means of the King-street exit when a gust of wind blew up the cape and disclosed the stolen garment. An employb of Mr. Leigh was on the look-out, and immediately detected the blouse as one she had just missed from the stall. Mrs. Ritchie was given into custody and appeared in the dock at the Police Court on Monday.—The Chief ,Constable said this was one more of the pathetic cases in which women, for the sake of a few pennyworth of finery, sacrificed good characters and brought disgrace upon themselves and calamity upon their families. Prisoner's husband held a good position and gave her sufficient money.—As prisoner made her first appearance, the magistrates imposed a fine of 30s. including costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>Minor Cases.</head>
        <p>Minor Cases.</p>
        <p>Warrington Borough Police Court.</p>
        <p>MoNDAY.—Before the Mayor (in the chair), Alderman Smethurst, Councillor Thornton, Messrs. Birchall,</p>
        <p>Hayward, and Wright.</p>
        <p>. , A DESERTER.—CharIes Leach was charged with hav- ing been a deserter from the Royal Artillery stationed at Seaforth since March sth.—Detective Foster said the prisoner gave himself up at the Police Station.— He was handed over to the military authorities. MAINTENANCE CASE.—George Forster, Helsby-street, was ordered to contribute is. per week towards the maintenance of his son on the training ship "Clio." AN OLD OFFENDER.—Mary Broderick, Dallam-lane, was charged with having been drunk and disorderly in Foundry-street on March 26th.—Prisoner made her 34th appearance within a short period, and the Chief Constable said she was on the rough edge of getting sent to an inebriates' home.—A fine of 20s. and costs was imposed. FIGHTING.—Harry Jones, 34, Forster-street, and Harry Jenkins, 15, Fothergill-street, was summoned for committing a breach of the peace by fighting in West- street.—They were bound over for six months and ordered to pay the costs.—Edward Walmsley, 17, Turner- street, was similarly dealt with for fighting on Pinner's- brow on April sth.—William Edelsten, 21, Bowers-street who was also summoned, did not appear, and a warrant was issued for his arrest.</p>
        <p>- ------- 0134CENE LANGUAGE.—Edward Mee, 38, Cockhedge, Lane, was fined ss. for making use of obscene language on March 27th.</p>
        <p>DISORDERLY CONDUCT.—EIIen Griffin,4,James street, was fined ss. and costs for making a loud and unseemly noise in James street on Nlareh 27th—Sergeant Smith proved the ease.—Six-boys named George Gleave, 198, Winwiek road, John Hughes, 25, Warburton street, William Caddiek, 16, West street, David Caddiel, 16, West street, Robert Bradshaw, 1, Warburton street, and 'Thomas Tingle, 288, Winwick road, were summoned for making loud and unseemly noises in Winwiek road on the Ist inst.—P. C. Stewart said defendants were shouting and jostling each other.—They were fined Gd. each.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY.—Before Aldermen Sutton (presiding) and Hutchinson, and Dr. Fox. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—Mark O'Brien and Clara Newnes, both of 5, Stanley-street, were charged with laving been drunk and disorderly in Bridge-street, at 12.30 that morning.—Sergeant Barnett proved the case, and the male prisoner, who had been convicted thirty times previously, was fined 10s. and costs or fourteen days, while the female prisoner was discharged. FRlDAY.—Before Mr. H. Roberts (in the chair),Alder- man Smethurst,Councillors Thornton and Roberts. THEFT OF A SKIRT.—Mary Jane McMiles, Cross-st., was charged with stealing a dress skirt, of the value of 75., the property of Harry Buckley, pawnbroker,Orford- lane.—Superintendent Monaghan said the garment was seen safe at three o'clock on Wednesday afternoon, and it was missed at four o'clock. At 3.30 prisoner pledged it at Mather's pawnshop, Pinner's-brow, for 3s. 6d. giving a wrong name and address. She was arrested on Thursday by Detective Meaeock and himself and ad-</p>
        <p>PURE COCOA is BEST</p>
