      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>Dareshury Petty Sessions.</head>
        <p>Dareshury Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY.—Before Colonel Ridgway (presiding), and</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Malin E.-dela-ten':</p>
        <p>"HARD-SWEARING."—DRIVER AND CONTRA.CTOR SUMMONED.—Micha3I Toole, Cloth Hall-yard, Warring- ton, was summoned for ill-treating a horse by working it whilst in an unfit st.ate at Moore on April 7th.— Arthur Lunt, contractor, Warrington, was summoned for causing the horse to be worked.—Mr. Browne defended. —lnspector Gibson, R.S.P.C.A., said Toole was working a brown gelding attached to a cart laden with sand. The horse was very lame and scarcely able to move along. The off-fore and off-hind heels were badly cracked and suppurating. There was a wound on the near hock,which was swollen, and blood was oozing from it. -Altogether, the animal was in a most deplorable condition and totally unfit for any kind of work. When interviewed, Mr. Lunt said he was acting under the advice of Mr. Hewitt, veterin- ary surgeon, in allowing the animal to be worked on sat land. Mr. Hewitt had not,however, seen the horse for three months.—Evidence was given by a police constable and Mr. Dixon, veterinary surgeon.—For the defence it was contended that there were no signs of lameness about the horse, when it was sent out. The defendants and Mr. Buckley, the foreman, were called. —After hearing all the evidence, the chairman remarked that there had been some very hard swearing.—Defend- ants were ordered to pay the cost of the summonses. IVIrNOR CASES.—Two Stockton Heath men named James Hamblett and John Smith sere fined 2s. 6d. and costs each for obstructing the footpath,—Charles J.nes was fined ss. and costs for making use of obscene language at Stockton Heath.—Elizabeth Ridgway was fined ss. for not having . a lighted lamp attached to a trap, of which she was in charge at Antrobus at 8.10 p.m. on March 26th.—Ernest Atkin was fined 2s. 6d. for allowing a dog to be at large unmuzzled at Latchford Without on April sth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>BILE BEANS AND INDIGESTION. Four Transformation Scenes.</head>
        <p>BILE BEANS AND INDIGESTION. Four Transformation Scenes.</p>
        <p>This article is written solely for the benefit of readers who suffer from indigestion, or any of its symptoms. From it, it is seen that even the 'worst case must yield to Bile Beans.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>SCENE L</head>
        <p>SCENE L</p>
        <p>Thomas Watson, miner, of 10, Victor Street, Cutsyke, Castleford, was an unusually I strong man and a champion weight lifter. Of all men illness seemed furthest from him, but—he began to suffer from indigestion. His strength faded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>SCENE IT.</head>
        <p>SCENE IT.</p>
        <p>He felt as he had never felt before! In the morning awakened with foul taste in mouth, bad breath, hands trembled. Didn't want breakfast. At work fell sick, faint, a..d dizzy. Head ached,and felt too heavy for his neck. Pains in chest, back, and round waist. Grew weaker and weaker. No food would stop on his stomach. Couldn't work any more. Twelve months went by. Still unable to work. Had got so weak that couldn't walk across kitchen floor without being supported. Gave up hope. Began to take Bile Beans, but didn't believe they would cure him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>SCENE 111.</head>
        <p>SCENE 111.</p>
        <p>Notwithstanding his disbelief they did cure him. After taking a course of this medicine he was cured of every trace of indigestion, was back at work, and was rapidly regaining strength. Cure remained permanent. welve months after being cured (during last month) a reporter interviewed him on his cure. "Has your strength been restored ?" he asked. For answer Watson picked up two 561 b. weights, and raised them over his head. He then lifted one of the weights with one finger, and hoisted it above his head. Moreover, he held it there until he was photographed, and the photograph is now in the possession of the Bile Bean Co. SCENE IV. • ___ _</p>
        <p>Before a Commissioner for Oaths Watson made the following sworn statement "I, Thomas Watson, miner, do hereby solemnlyand, sincerely declare as follows :—That from being a man able to lift up two hundredweight above my head with one arm alone, I was reduced by indigestion and kindred ailments to the weakness of a child, unable to walk With- out assistance. My health has been fully restored, and my strength renewed, solely through taking Chas. Forde's Bile Beans. This after being attended by two doctors without success, one doctor having declared I would never again be strong enough to go to work." To those with indigestion, nothing more need be said except this.—ln purchasing Bile Beans from any chemist the 2s. 9d. size is the most economical. This box con- tains three times of the is. lid. size. Also, don't take substitutes.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES. THE ATTEMPTED SUICIDE AT RUNCORN. At the Runcorn Police Court on Saturday, before Mr. E. Imison, Julia Cooke a well-known character, living in Robert-street, was charged on remand with having attempted to commit suicide by jumping into the Bridgewater Canal on the evening of Good Friday.— Robert Henry Moores, Ship Canal constable, said he was on duty near the Big House, Bottom Locks, on the evening mentioned in company with his brother. The woman passed them and shortly afterwards they heard a curious gurgling noise. They ran in the direction the woman had taken, and found her in the old basin of the old line of locks. They got her out and she said "God bless you."—Defendant promised to abstain from drink, and she was discharged. RUNCORN MAGISTRATE AND CYCLISTS.</p>
        <p>_ On Monday, at Runcorn, Albert Charlesworth, Green- way-road, was summoned for having, at Sutton,on Good Friday, ridden a bicycle to the danger of the public. Police-sergeant Morgan said the defendant came down the hill at Frodsham at a speed of twenty miles an hour, and the road was packed with people, as the Volunteer manceuvres were on. The defendant said he was not travelling at more than thirteen miles an hour, and there were no people in the road. The magistrates' clerk said there was no fixed speed. The question was whether he was going quicker than he should have done under the circumstances.—The Chairman said many cyclists thought the road belonged to them. The defendant was fined 10s and costs, 95. 6d. A RUNCORN POLICE CELL DIALOGUE. An amusing case was heardat Runcorn Petty Sessions on Monday. Charles Stansfleld and his wife, Sarah Jane having all the appearance of " old travellers" were charged with begging, and Charles attempted to lead the magistrates into the belief that this was their first appearance.—Detective Mottershead said the woman was hard of hearing,and)after he had _locked the prisoners in the cells he heard the following dialogue :—Sarah Jane : Where shall we go to from here, Charley ? Charley: Knutsford.—Sarah Jane: Lichfield ? Charley: No, Knutsford.—Sarah Jane: Stafford (laughter) ?—Charley (very loudly) : No, Knutsford.— Sarah Jane: Oh, Knutsford. We have not been there before, have we, Charley ?—The Magistrates' Clerk remarked that the couple evidently knew where the gaols were.—The prisoners were each sentenced to seven days' hard labour.</p>
        <p>FAILURES IN ENGLAND AND WALES.—According to Kemp's Mercantile Gazette," the total number of failures recorded in England and Wales during the week ending Friday, April Bth, was 88, viz.:—New Bank- ruptcy Proceedings published in the London Gazette, 28; deeds of arrangement registered, 60. The res- pective numbers in the corresponding week of last year were : Bankruptcies, 90 ; deeds of arrangement, 73 total 163; being a net increase in bankruptcies of 14, and a net increase in deeds of arrangement in 1904, to date, of 111.</p>
        <p>ENDLISII CuP FINAL.—For this great event at the Crystal Palace on Saturday next, the Great Central Railway Company announces a special excursion. A train will leave St. Helens at 11.40 on Friday night, calling at Haydock, Ashton-in-Makerfleld, Golborne, St. Mary's and Culcheth and proceeding to London (Marylebone) by the new route.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>NOW 'IS" THE-11ML</head>
        <p>NOW 'IS" THE-11ML</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S EDUCATION.I WARRINGTON'S BUDGET.</p>
        <p>TRADE UNIONISTS' REPRESENTA- TIVE'S INTERESTING REPORT.</p>
        <p>An interesting report on the first year's work of the Warrington Education Committee was submitted at the meeting of the Trades and Labour Council on Tuesday evening by Mr. Edwin Plinston,the Council's representa- tive on the authority. The difficulties of the Educa- tion authority in Warrington had been, Mr. Plinston said, very great, by reason of the fact that in the past they had had no school board in the borough,and where they found an education authority coming into exist- ence where there had not been a school board, they found in was very hard work for that authority to formulate a satisfactory scheme. (Hear, hear.) The formulating of an education scheme for the town had from the commencement been a question which had demanded and had received their closest attention.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>CLASSIFICATION OF. TEACHERS.</head>
        <p>CLASSIFICATION OF. TEACHERS.</p>
        <p>This was a question which they were early called upon to consider, and in conjunction with that question came the question of teachers' salaries. It was felt that teachers should be paid the best possible wage which could be given them, and accordingly a scale of salaries was drawn up, which in many instances meant big increases in the remuneration of some teachers. At the present time there were many uncertificated teachers working in the town, and the Education Committee had decided that unless within five years those teachers made themselves qualified they should be discharged. He did not agree with that proposal, as in many cases an unqualified person was actually a better teacher (though not so clever), than persons holding thehighest certificates. (Hear, hear.) He did not agree with the Education authority when they adopted the course which they had adopted with regard to uncertificated teachers, and intended to raise the question in the future.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>DIRECTOR'S SALARY.</head>
        <p>THE DIRECTOR'S SALARY.</p>
        <p>Mr. PLINSTON then referred to the granting of an in crease in salary to the director of education, and re- marked that he always opposed the increase, not be- cause he did not think Mr. Murray was worth it, but because he objected on principle to the salary of any official being increased until he had been connected for a longer period than had been so in Mr. Murray's case with the Corporation. THE APPOINTMENT OF CARETAKERS.</p>
        <p>When the committee came into existence they found that the schools were being looked after by persons who only devoted part of their time to the work. The consequence was that the schools were not looked after as well as they might have been. The committee there- upon decided to appoint persons to look after the schools, who would devote the whole of their time to it. With re gard to the salaries of these people it was decided to pay them £52 per year for the first 300 children, and is. per week for every additional hundred children. That in many cases meant increases of 100 per cent. upon the wages previously paid, though, as these people were devoting the whole of their time to the work, he did not think any person would object to their being paid according to that scale. SCROOL ATTENDANCE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Pr.msTos next referred to the question of school attendance and expressed the opinion that the School Attendance Committee should exercise a little more dis- cretion in their consideration of those cases which came before there. In many cases the children were kept from the school because their parents could not afford to pay the schoolpence. He quite agreed that that should not be the case and yet he did not believe in dealing harshly with such people, as he believed in the stomach of a child being looked after in preference to its brains. It might be argued that the parents could send their children to school free if they chose to do so. That was correct, and yet in order to .doso,applica- tion for the school pence had to be made to the reliev- ing officer. That meant the pauperising of the parent, who made the application,, and therefore he did not a all agree that people should be forced to make th., application. The question could not be settled until they had free education all round, though the Educa- tion Committee could not tackle that question as yet on account of the great expense which the adoption of free education would entail. With regard to the deal- inz with children who did not attend school, Mr. Plinston expressed his strong disapproval of the send- ing of those children to the ship "Clio," as after they had been on the ship they were only fitted for a sea- faring life, and were ruined so far as the following of occupations on land was concerned. MEDICAL CERTIFICATES.</p>
        <p>Mr. PLusTsTos referred to the decision of the Educa- tion Committee that children who were sick would only be exempted from attending school on the production of a doctor's certificate. The committee felt that every parent did not believe in, and could not afford to see a doctor every time a child was sick, and that therefore the committee were taking steps to introduce a system whereby medical certificates could be obtained free for the purpose of exemption from attendance at school. SECONDARY EDUCATION.</p>
        <p>With regard to secondary education he had all along objected most strongly to the price (9d.) charged for that education. If education in Warrington was to be carried on to the fullest extent and to benefit every child alike it was necessary that that education should be absolutely free. The same thing applied to the scheme of higher education which had been drawn up. For that scheme to be of real advantage brains should be absolutely the only qualification necessary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>SCHOOL ACCOMMODATION.</head>
        <p>SCHOOL ACCOMMODATION.</p>
        <p>In the immediate future the committee had to deal with the question of school accommodation. The school inspectors had reported that there was hardly a single scheol in the borough which was in proper condition. Why they did not discover that fact before the Education Committee came into existence he did not know, but the fact remained that they had discovered it within the last 12 months, and that the Education Committee were faced with the necessity of providing more school accommodation and also the provision of better sanitary arrangements. That would mean the incurring of a heavy expense, but the cost would have sto be borne and that within the next few years. IThere was, therefore, the prospect of a heavy expense in the immediate future, but he was convinced that a great deal of this was necessary. He hoped, how- ever, that when the committee did provide thenecessary schools they would provide buildings of sufficient capacity, but free from expensive elaboration. (Hear, hear.) The committee had a great deal of work to do in the immediate future, and he could assure the Council that it would be his endeavour, as it had been in the past, to look after the interests of the working classes so far as education was concerned. (Applause.) In answer to a number of questions Mr. Plinston ex- plained that the Act said that if necessary education should be given free. Application, had however, to be made to the Board of Education for free education. That should not be the case, as he contendedand always would maintain that education to be of any value should be absolutely free. He could not give the exact figures with regard to the children under live years of age who attended school, but he could say that if it was possible for the Education Committee to prevent those children attending school,accommodation would be provided for several thousand older children.</p>
        <p>PLINSTON explained that the Education Com- mittee would be sole governors of any new schools which were erected, and that no sectarian party what- ever would hold the sway. He could assure them, however, that so far, there had not been in Warrington the slightest attempt to thrust sectarian principles down the throats of any of the children attending school.</p>
        <p>Mr. PLINSTON was heartily thanked for his report. It was decided to adjourn the discussion on the re- port to the next meeting of the Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>ST. HELENS AND CHINESE LABOUR.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS AND CHINESE LABOUR.</p>
        <p>Tory Speakers get Heckled.</p>
        <p>Feeling runs high in St. Helens on the Chinese labour question. Recently there was a great demonstration in the Hippodrome, when 3,000 working men entered a protest. The Conservative member, Si. Henry Seton- Karr,and his organisers were unwilling Chat this con- demnation of the Government should lack a counter- blast, so on Monday night a demonstration wasarranged in the Town Hall.</p>
        <p>Despite the fact that admission was by ticket, and that the credentials of everyone who presented himself at the door were carefully scrutinised, a fairly large number of opponents found their way in. There was some booing when Sir Henry Seton-Karr got up, and h.. was continually interrupted. "I ask you to listen to me," he shouted, "and not go on with this-noise." The disturbers shouted with laughter, and there were cries of "Better language, please." Sir Henry proceeded: "I have heard a great deal of political humbug and cant in my time, but never any- thing to equal the lying that has taken place on thi subject." An even more unfavourable reception was accorded Mr. William Mather, formerly a miners' agent in the Transvaal.</p>
        <p>He declared that a native miner on a wage of 545. a month could save £33 a year, and in three years have sufficient to buy five wives and enough stock to main- tain him on his mealie patch as long as he lived. A white man with a wife and family could not live there under £3OO a year—an argument against the employment of whites for the lower class of mining work. Subsequently a resolution approving the policy of the Government was put, and, although largely opposed, declared carried by the chairman. The meeting broke up in some disorder.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>SIXPENNY INCREASE OF RATES.</head>
        <p>A SIXPENNY INCREASE OF RATES.</p>
        <p>Explanation by Alderman Fainlough. COUNCILLOR DODDS UTTERS A WORD OF WARNING.</p>
        <p>Tuesday was Budget night in connection with the Warrington Town Council, when the rates for the ensuing year were fixed. Another sixpence in the pound was added to the rates, making a total rate of 7s. 10d. in the £. The reasons for this increase were exulained by Alderman Fairclough, the Chairman of the Finance and Estate Committee, and in the dis- cussion which followed a note of warning was sounded by Councillor Dodds on the policy of extravagance which, he contended, had been followed in the past.</p>
        <p>ALDERMAN FAIRCLOUGH'S DETAILED STATENIENT.</p>
        <p>Alderman FAIRCLOUGH said: In submitting the es- timates for the current year I wish to point out that the total amount required is £74,420 3s. Bd., which is in excess of last year by £12,683 2s. 6d. The excess is made up as follows :—Borough rite, £8,046 17s. 6d.; improvement rate, £5,473 lls. id. ; total, t 13,520 Bs. 7d; less general sewers rate#lB32 6s. id.; leaving £12,688 2s. 6d. In the borough rate we close the year with a bank deficit of £3,362 12s. 10d.,being approximately the amount overspent by the following committees ; Gen- eral Purposes ; (a) Parliamentary and legal expenses mainly Ship Canal, £1,067 us. Bd. (you will remember that we deleted £l,OOO from this item last year on hearing the result of the Ship Canal action) ; (b) Non- Trading inquiry payments to date, • not included in estimate, £179 195.; total. £1,247 10s. Bd.—Finance Committee: (a), Exchequer zontribution and County Rate Purposes deficit, through final adjustment re- quiring repayment of amount paid to us in excess by Local Government Board, £1,138 Os. 9d.; (b), adjust- ment of new loans on Police Station and Baths, £218; (c), deficiency on extended area, estimated before it was known at what the Overseers were intending to fix the rate, £329 17s. 3d.; total £1,685 18s.; Park Com- mittee: New Greenhouse, £72; work in connection with new conservatory, £218; roofing port:on of old greenhouse, £l7; bowling green, £6O; asphalting, £99; repairs, £7O; £566. This bank deficiency of £3,362 12s. 10d. is included in the total estimates now sub- mitted. In addition</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>CURRENT ESTIMATES</head>
        <p>THE CURRENT ESTIMATES</p>
        <p>of several committees are in excess of year. The Watch Committee requires £4Ol more, which is largely accounted for by provision having been made for four additional constables. In the General Purposes estimate, Item 1, for municipal election expenses has been in- creased by £lOO, and Item 2, Parliamentary and legal expenses by £l,OOO. Items 14 to 18 are new. They include Von-Trading inquiry £5OO, accrued interest on land, Howley £756, Branch Libraries £202, Ground Rent of land Winwick-road £54, Analyst £150; total, £2,762. Items amounting to £l,OOO in last year's estimates are • not repeated, leaving an increase of £1,762. The -Park Committee have included £l5O for completion of bowling green in Victoria Park, £5O for new front to aviary, and £l3O for increase of wages,and other smaller items. The Gymnasium Committee requires £lOO more to cover wages of assistant instruc- tor. The Education Committee requires £1.117 19s. sd. more than last year. It will be remembered that we adopted the Education Act at the earliest possible date, viz. :-Ist April, 1903, and so secured the benefit of the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>INCREASED GRANTS</head>
        <p>INCREASED GRANTS</p>
        <p>from the Board of Educatio.W. This was of material assistance in enabling us to make a less call on the rates than otherwise would have been necessary. Not having had a School Board in Warring- ton there has been much leeway to fetch up both in re- gard to Educational methods and equipments, but in spite of this we are still better off than most towns where there were previously School Boards; for in- stance, Barrow, before the Act, had a School Board rate of is. 2 3-sd.,yet in spite of the higher grants they have had also to increase their rate for the year just closed by id. in the 2. Similarly Bootle with a School Board rte of Bd., had to increase id.; Rochdale with had to increase 2d. ; Burnley with Is. id. also had to increase 2d. ; Darlington with is. litd. increased 11d.; whilst Halifax although it had a rate of is. 81d. had to increase it another Id. to meet its responsibility- under the new Act. It is only fair to say that in a few in- stances where there has been a School Board rate, the increased grants have enabled the local authority to reduce the call on the rates. For instance, Rotherham, which had a School Board rate of 2s. 1 3-sd. is now able to carry on with a rate of is. 10d. Even this is just double the rate now required in Warrington.The amount called for is intended not only to meet all the ordinary expenditure of education, but to build up a working balance as required by the Local Government Board, which in our case involves the equivalent of Id. in the .2. The net increase in the rates in consequence of the duties imposed by the Education Act is equal to 9d. in the 2. It should be borne in mind that any reduction in the expenditure on education which might lead to inefficiency would involve a much more serious loss in the grants, which are approximately three times more than the amount raised by the rate. The Finance Com- mittee at a special meeting called for the consideration of the final estimate decided to recommend the Council to make the DEDUCTIONS NOTED IN THE BUDGET. _ _</p>
        <p>These deductions consist of :—Watch Committee £320: stimated cost of the four extra constables, which the committee consider are unnecessary at present. The Mayor who was present at the meeting agreed on behalf of the Watch Committee to this course being adopted.—General Purposes: Item 16. Accrued interest on purchase money was deleted for the time being, as the question of the resale of the land is still under consideration.—ltem 17. Branch Libraries: The committee were strongly in favour of this scheme not being carried out, unless the Museum and Library Committee could deal with it as part of their rate. Town Hall and Park Committee: Item 13 for new front to avairy was abandoned this year. Gymnasium: The item 10 for shower bath was considered unnecessary. Finance: Item 19 for the annual instalment of Parliamentary Bill expenses was reduced by £5OO for this year. If the Council agrees to these suggestions, the amount originally shown by the estimates will be reduced by the sum of £l.,B7B—this will enable the Overseers to levy a rate of 4s. 2d. instead of 4s. 4d. In</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>IMPROVEMENT 'RATE</head>
        <p>THE IMPROVEMENT 'RATE</p>
        <p>we close the year with a bank de cit of £2,766 10s. 2d., being the amount overspent by the following 'com- mittees—(l) Fire Engine: Repairs, new hose pipe,ecc„ in excess of estimate, £100; (2) Health: Port Sani- tary Authority, £B9 (the precept was not to hand when the estimate was made, and it has been ex- ceeded by this sum); smallpox epidemic, £B5 (extra expenses in connection with disinfect- ing and quarantine) ; (3) Street Improvement and Lighting : Extra cost of lighting and improving streets, £541 ; (4) Highway': Overspent, £2,520 (this sum, however, included £607 spent on Stock, and £697 for work done, to be repaid); (5) Hospital : Smallpox Hospital accouut overspent by £3o9,repairs and renewals Aikin-street. £380; total, £3,974; less sundry com- mittees underspent, £1,208—£2,766. In addition the estimates of I several committees are in excess of last year. The Nightsoil Department are extending the num- ber of iron pails at an extra cost of £725. The Long- ford Department are making extensions and putting in a new boiler to get more evaporating power which has increased their item for fuel by £256. The Healt and Hospital Departments through extensions of premises and increase in the staff are becoming a heavy charge upon the rates, their requirements apart from interest and sinking fund being £790 in excess of last year. The Highway Department are asking for £BO5 more owing to the gradual increase in roads withih the borough, The Markets Committee are extending the lights at an extra !cost of £5O, and other items for maintenance bring the total required to £167 more than last year. The Street Improvementand Lighting Committee are asking for £905 more. During the year many new lamps have been erected, and a considerable number of old ones have been converted ,to incandescents. The extra cost incurred by these changes is considerable, and I may point out that the charge on the rates for this depart- ment alone is 60. in the pound. The Finance Com- mittee requirements are £714 more. The charges for sinking fund and interest have increased by £1,716, but some other items have been reduced. I may say that to carry out the estimates with the alterations as recom- mended. and to provide for the bank overdraft of last year will require</p>
        <p>AN INCREASE IN THE RATES OF 6d, in the pound. This is a very serious matter, and it behoves the members of every committee to realize the gravity of the situation. You will see from the summary of estimates that the present requirements arel £74,420 3s. Bd. less the proposed reduction of ' £1,878, whilst in 1900 the amount required was £40,571 an increase of say £32,000, 78 per cant., whilst I find that in the same period the ratable value has •mly increased seven per cent. The amount overspent on the estimates of last year is equal to 6id. in the £ •, the increase in the rate of interest payable on the debt and the payment of the first instalment of new loans is equal to 2d. in the pound. Had it not been for the amount received from the Gas Department last year, viz :—£8,381, £4,381 more than we had in the estimate, the rates would cer- tainly have been higher. The Gas Committee had sug- gested to the Borough Treasurer that he might reason- ably expect £5,000 from the profits of the year just ended, but in view of the large increase in the Esti- mates the Finance Committee thought it better to anticipate a similar sum to that received last year, viz: £B,OOO, (Hear, hear). I think the committee are justified in anticipating thatamount as the price of coal has been reduced.(learhear.)The Finance Committee suggests that each committee should carefully avoid exceeding their estimates,and should examine the details of their-depart- ments see that due economy is being exercised.(Applause.) Alderman FAIRCLOUGH moved the following resolu- tion in conclusion:—' That the Council hereby estimate the amount of £35,636 12s. 3d. will, in addition to the</p>
        <p>Borough Fund, be required and suEsaent for the pur- poses to which the Borough Fund is applicable, under the Municipal Corporations Act, 1882 or otherwise by law, for the year to end 31st March, 1905."</p>
        <p>ALDERMAN BENNETT AND FALSE ECONOMY. ' Alderman BESTNEPT seconded the motion. He was bound to say there were many thingsin the Budget which were unpleasent and alarming, but there was one thing whichwa,s comparatively satisfactory.lt had always been ! held over their heads that any prospective increase of the rates to any serious extent would be due to the Education Act. He was therefore pleased to find that of the 6d increase, only ld., was attributable to the Educa- tion Act. He could say with assurance that the esti- mates had been prepared with the greatest possible care and thrashed out through quite a series of channels. It was necessary to point out—and he wished their friends I had borne it in mind when they took the action they , had done with reference to teachers' salaries—that 21 out of the 36 members of the Educationeommittee were members of the Council, and they had unani- mously adopted the education budget. The in- crease in the amount of the education rate as compared with last year was £1,117. That was practically met by the proportion of the loan which they were able to borrow and which amounted to £950. Tnat enabled them last year to levy a 10d. instead of a 16d. rate, so that really the extra amount of rate they were called upon to endure was due to the saving 'secured last year by means of the loan. If they had faced the whole 16d. last year they would have had to pay Id. less for several years to come. Alderman Fairclough pointed out that the increase in the rate 'due to the Education Act was 9d. It was nominally 11d., but prior to the adoption of the Act the Council had to pay for the work of the School Attendance and Technical Instruction Committees which was now absorbed by the Education Authority. Education cost the tovvnroughly about £40,000, but it was necessary to bear in mind that in routid figures three-fourths of the amount was received from the Government, and the Council, there- fore, only had to bear a fourth. The grants from the Government were largely dependent upon the efficiency of the work of their various schools, and if from any false notions of economy they understaffed or starved the schools, or neglected to provide the requisite accommodation, it meant they were incurring a most serious risk, in other words to save one-fourth they were jeopardizing the three-fourths they were receiving from the Government. (Hear, hear.) Theyhiad notany administrative expenses under the old condition of things • they had no School Board or education office with all the necessary staff. That expense was entirely new and due to the Act. It meant about lid. in the 2, but he thought those who were familiar with the work of the committee would agree that they were getting full value for their money and that the Education of the town was making substantial progress in all directions. They had the satisfaction of knowing that that was the opinion of Mr. Richards, H.M. Inspector. We also pointed out that owing to the deficiencies under which they bad laboured in the past, they had to bear to some extent undue burdens now, and they would have to face considerably increased expenditure to pro- vide proper accommodation in overcrowded schools. )It was absolutely necessary on grounds of health and convenience that they should take early steps to pro- vide increased accommodation and thus put their educational system upon a footing which was necessary in a town of the size and importance of Warrington. Mr. Frederick Monks had pointed out that if they were to fulfil their duties in that respect they would probably have to spend £50,000 during the next few years, and one could not help feeling that Mr. Monks was, not wide of the mark. They must always have that prospective expenditure in view,and endeavour to be as economical as possible. But whatever they did in their desire to •be economical, they must not run the risk of incurring dangers which would altogether counterbalance the small economies which would be effected. It seemed to him that what the Council had done in regard to the Education Committee's minutes was ex- tremely unwise; and possibly it might dislocate the whole of the work of the committee. They could not maintain the schools unless they had proper staffs; and they could not get the staffs unless they paid proper wages. If they entered into an agree- ment with members of the staffs on the faith of a scale adopted by the Education Committee and approved by the Council, and then tried to revoke that scale, they put themselves into a thoroughly false and impossible position. If they broke faith with the teachers many of them might resign, and they now found the greatest possible difficulty in getting a staff of teachers adequate to their necessities. He was not a special economist. He was very much in favour of improvements and liked to see a public spirit manifested, but when hd heard that the extra rate was to amount to 6d. in the £— ( The DEPUTY-MA.YOR : Bd.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT said it was Bd. originally, but it was reduced by one foolish economy at any rate. When he heard that the rate was to be 6d. extra he felt alarmed, and for once he began to feel some modicum of sym- pathy for the Non-traders who had been preaching economy in season and out of setison—very often out of season. (Laughter). He felt in face of the prospec- tive increase, that there was a very serious need for them to cry" Halt "and to look around and endeavour to economise wherever they cduld,without sacrificing real efficiency and progress.rhe fact that the)expendiure in five years hadincreased from £40,000 to £61,000 exclu- sive of education, was an alarming fact, and when it was also remembered that the rateable value had only increased 7 per cent. during that time it was enough to make the most enterprising, idealistic and Utopian councillor wonder if it was not time to stop and take stock of their position. (Laughter.) He congratulated the Gas Committee on the fact that they had been able to come so substantially to the rescue. They would not however, have to be satisfied with £B,OOO. The way in which affairs were being over- hauled, would lead them to look with some reason to a substantial contribution in years to come from the Tramways and Electric Lighting committees. Although they were bound to proceed with public improvements, he thought that bit by bit new sources of revenue would come, and that they would be able to adjust their backs to the burden.</p>
        <p>A wow) OF WARNING FROM COUNCILLOR</p>
        <p>DODDS.</p>
        <p>Councillor DODDS in the course of a criticism of some of the Rams of expenditure, said Alderman Bennett had told them that five years ago the estimate was £40,591 lls. lid It was really £33,231 ss. 4d.; now it was £74,420 3s. Bd. or 100 per cent. increase. The DEPIITY-iVIAYOR : I say 78 per cent. Councillor DODDS : Igo back a year further than you and say that it five years—and Alderman Bennett said five years—our estimates have increased 100 per cent.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT : It was my mistake, I meant to say four years.</p>
        <p>--do-tincilloi DODDS, continuing, said that there were some figures which were not clear to him, although he ought to understand figures. It would appear from the budget that last year the estimated expenditure was £61,731 17s. 2d. ; as a matter of fact the estimated ex- penditure was £66,178 14s. and the actual expenditure (last year) £71,419 7s. This year the I estimated expenditure would be £69,158 10s. Last year instead of £66,000, £71,000 was spent and he was afraid that this year they would overstep the estimates a great deal again. It therefore behoved every member of the Council and every ratepayer to take to heart the alarming, the appalling rate at ,which their expenditure was increasing. In the case of a business concern,expenditure at this rate would land them in the bankruptcy court in a few weeks. There was not the slightest doubt about that. In the return they had not got any information as to the deficiency from tramways; and he contended that some of the items might be cut down. There was £llO for the audit for instance. He understood that the father of their finance audited the accounts without charge. It was a wonderful thing. In the old days when they wanted a nurse for Moses, they got Moses' mother and paid her. (Laugh- ter.) When the 3 wanted someone to audit the accounts they got the Chairman of the Finance Com- mittee to do it himself. (More laughter.) That was a most extraordinary thing to do. He (the speaker) knew there were difficultiea in the way, and that they could not get anyone else to act. He refused to do so because he had not the time.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: lam sorry you refused. Councillor DODDS, said his point was that as Alderman Fairclough audited for nothing, thp £3O formerly paid to the Mayor's auditor should now be saved. On the other hand there was a sum of £1,500 put down as a pro- vision for Parliamentary and legal expenses,but everyone knew that £15,000 woul I be nearer the mark in connec- tion with the rivers question. (Hear, hear.) It might be that they would get some of the costs back, or that they would not all be payable this year but he ques- tioned it. He did not wish them to anticipate that next year the rates would go down. They were go- ing up steadily,and what was the cause? The men wh,) started the purity agitation were the men who were right as to the main cause. There was an apparent deter- mination on the part of men in the Councilllnd out of it that their positions should be utilized to make something for th3mselves, so that they could get work and orders for their business or places or advances for their friends, and in this way such laxity had there been that for instance £30,000 —three or four times as much as was necessary—was spent on the new police buildings, and last year there</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>HIS PAINS ARE GONE.</head>
        <p>HIS PAINS ARE GONE.</p>
        <p>From the North Seaton Colliery, Northumberland comes the report of a remarkable case of a man who has been 22 weeks off work with pains in his back and kidney trouble, and who has been restored by Dodd's Kidney Pills. Many remarkable cures are being credited to this wonderful remedy, but none more satisfactory and perfect than this one. The man's name is William Levison, and he has testi- fied as follows :—"I suffered from pains in my back and across my loins; when I stooped I could scarcely straighten up again. My kidneys were not acting pro- perly. I had no appetite and lost flesh. I tried many medicines, but nothing helped me till I heard of Dodd's Kidney Pills, and in a very short time after I began using this remedy I noticed an improvement. My kidneys improved very rapidly, the pains in my back decreased and my food seemed to do me more good. "Dodd's Kidney Prlls have done me more good than anything I have ever taken. I can thoroughly recom- mend them to all who suffer in the same way. lam at work every day now.,</p>
        <p>• .••••••••••••••••••• 616 MSS au attempt to a</p>
        <p>_ but they (the Non-Traders) did all they could to stop it and it was stopped. They stopped £ll,OOO on the Teohnioal Schools. They didall that and a great deal more. He himself opposed every advance of salary last year, but the ratepayers did not seem to mind, as they had not supported him as they ought to have done. If the ratepayers did not want salaries keptldown let them not find fault in the newspapers, but support economy at the polling booths. The Aavoit: You must not take all the credit to yourself. We have all bean in the same boat. Councillor DOD Os said he agreed as far as the Mayor was concern4d. They foresaw what was coming. They saw that an increase of the rates was bound to come on the policy which had been started, the policy of i extravagance and the policy of waste, resulting in orders I for certain people connected with the Corporation. He ' had said that publicly, but now all that had been stopped. The father of the Council, Alderman Sutton said, and very happily—it was a delightful remark— "they were all non-traders now." If that was so, and they could come together,without trying to do anything for their personal friends, brothers and bro;hers-in-law, or relations, or friends socially; if they would forget all that and strive to cut down expenditure—and they would have to do it keenly and systematically, day by day, year by year—the time might come when they would get' the town into a financial condition which would be a credit to the Council instead of, as at present, a disgrace. (Applause). A LIVELY SCENE.</p>
        <p>lena £76,000 an water works,</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON said he would like to say a few words on this "enormous expenditure," and could endorses great deal of what Councillor Dodds had said. He had sat on Committees of that Council and could say that there was no private business concern in the country—whatever its profits—that could be carried on in a way their business was conducted in the com- mittees. New members were hampered by "old forms of etiquette" which would not be allowed to exist in a private concern. In the Electricity Committee Stand- ing Orders had been set at defiance—</p>
        <p>Councillor WILKINSON (heatedly) : I deny it. You are a member of the co,itnittee and you ought to have seen that things were done properly. Alderman SMETHURST : He ought to be ashamed sf himself. Councillor WILKINSON: If Councillors Pemberton and Dodds would do a bit more work on the committee and not simply come here talking- The MAYOR: We are not discussing the Tramways Committee.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON : We are discussing the estimates and the expenditure and the economy of the Town Council.</p>
        <p>The MAYO: No we are not discussing the economy of the Town Council. We are considering the amount of money we must have to carry on the affairs of the town, and we want you to understand that this time of the night is not the time for grumbling at everybody else because they are not of the same opinion as you. It is easy to find fault. Councillor WILKINSON: Too easy. Councillor PEMBERTON: I am speaking of facts. What I say I can substantiate. I say the Electricity Committee have bought coal contrary to the Standing Orders.</p>
        <p>Councillor WiranisoN (warmly) : We will not do it again. What are you bothering about ? We have got a new chairman %nd vice-chairman now. Sit down and don't waste time.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: We have the coal question at every Council meeting. _ _</p>
        <p>Councillor PIMBERTON : Mr. Mayor, lam entitled to express my views. CouncillorVlLKlNsoN : But we are not entitled to listen to them. (Laughter.) Councillor PEMBERTON : This is the course we ought to take- Councillor WILKINSON: Get off. The MAYOR: This has nothing to do with the estimates,</p>
        <p>Councillor WILKINSON :We discussed it in committee, The MAYOR: Never mind the coal now. When the orders for coal come up again you must object to them. Councillor PBMBERTON : Don't these estimates in- clude the Electricity Committee's expeaditure ? The MAYOR: No. Nothing at all. If everyone is going on the same lines as Councillor Pemberton we shall be here all night.</p>
        <p>7Ciie-bmPuTY-Mairoa : I should like to point out that the Electricity Committee have nothing on the estimates at all. - -</p>
        <p>-64;t7i-n-Cillor PEMBERTON : I could have said twenty times over what I have got to say if you had allowed me to go on. We are paying 2s. id. more for coal than you have paid other people in the town— The MAYOR: As far as lam concerned I don't want to keep squabbling with every member as to what he ought to say. You are trying by a side wind to bring in that red herring of the coal contract every time you possibly can. Councillor PEMBERTON : I beg your pardon. I do nothing of the kind. The MAYOR: You know how offensive it is to some members.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON : It is offensive to some members to have anything that is going on wrong to be brought before the public notice. Tho NIA.voR :- Let me give you a little bit of busi- ness ideas. When you want to make money for a firm you tackle the big things—the thousands of pounds. You don't fall out about the threepenny-bits. Councillor PEMBERTON : lam talking about 2s. id on 6,000 tons of coal-6,000 2s. id's. Alderman SUTTON: Deal with the estimates. Councillor PEMBERTON : In dealing with the estimates all this general extravagance comes in. Councillor WILKINSON: Oh shut.up. Councillor PEMBERTON : If the Council would place their orders—</p>
        <p>Councillor WILKINSON: If you would do the work on committees instead of telling others to do right it would be better. lam sick and tired of it. Councillor PEMBERTON: I voice the opinions of thotftnds of people outside the Town Council, of some of the most prominent trades people and largest manu- facturers. It is the unanimous opinion that the rates are too high. They are keeping works from coming here. I say as a practical member of the Town Council that the business of these committees is not carried on like private concerns. Councillor WILKINSON: You are not a practical member of the Town Council. Councillor PEMBERTON: No, you know it all. (Laughter.) No private business would be carried on in the same way. The MA.YOR: I am afraid no business could be carried on like some members of the Council want the business here to be conducted. Alderman SMETHURST (indicating CouncilloK Pemberton): When a man can get up and make state- ments like that, he is not worth listening to. He does not attend to his own committees. (To Councillor Pemberton), You are on the Electricity Committee. Why don't you attend? Councillor PEMBERTON : Ido attend. Alderman SMETHURST : If you can manage the Electricity and Tramways Committee I will resign for you. Councillor WILKINSON: SO will I. Alderman SMETHURST (to Councillor Pemberton) : Every meeting you come to, you annoy everyone. Councillor PEMBERTON : Put down the extravagance. Alderman SMETHURST : It is most scandalous. "AN ANNUAL FEVER."</p>
        <p>Councillor TINNION said he would like to make a sug- gestion. It seemed to him that the increase in the rates was a sort of annual fever, which died out immediately- the rate had been sanctioned. He did not agree alto- gether with the way the estimates had been made out, and would suggest an improvement for another year, because he was not absolutely satisfied that they were footing the bill as it should be footed. He was afraid if they did it would be Bs. on the rates. He would propose that next year a committee should be formed to go into the matter and see where expenditure could be curtailed. The various committees' estimates could come before them and be properly considered. If there were any leakages they could find out where they were, and have full time to consider the estimates, instead of them coming up at the last five minutes. As Chairman of one of the committees he had not been consulted in reference to a reduction which had been made by the Finance! Committee. The chairmen of Committees might be consulted more than they were, and the esti- mates considered a little earlier than they were. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>RATES AGREED TO.</head>
        <p>THE RATES AGREED TO.</p>
        <p>The resolution proposed by Alderman Fairclough was then carried unanimously. The following resolutions were also agreed to :—That the overseers of the town- ship of Warrington be and they are hereby ordered to pay to the treasurer of the borough, by instalments,and on or before such dates as may be fixed by the Finance and Estate Committee the contribution of the sum of £35,636 12s. 3d.,out oCthe poor rate made or to be made for the said township of Warrington, as provided by the Municipal Corporation Acts, 1882. That the estimate for an improvement rate as recom- mended by the Finance and Estate Committee be con- firmed, and that the Finance and Estate Committee do make an improvement rate accordingly at three shillings and sixpence in the pound, to be collected by two half- yearly instalments, and be authorized to affix the com- mon seal of the Corporation thereto. That the estimate of the General Sewers Rate as recommended by the Finance and Estate Committee be confirmed, and that the Finance and Estate Committee do make a General Sewers Rate accordingly at twopence) in the pound, to be collected by two half-yearly instal- ments, and be authorized to!affix the common seal of the Corporation thereto. The Council then rose.</p>
        <p>A German professor has invented a process of silver plating dead bodies so as to convert them into metallic images of the individuals as they were when in life. Gold plate can be used if the relatives can afford it. But as the expense of silver-plating a body is £2,500, there are probably few relatives who can deem them- selves justified in squandering the deceased's estate on such a memorial.</p>
        <p>At the funeral of a well-known local cricketer, Mr. E. Spittle% atTTunbridge Wells on Thursday the cricket lub with which he was connected sent a broken bat as n emblem of sympathy from the members.</p>
        <p>- AGAIN, "Ariii AAftl, AM AGAIN. •</p>
        <p>Again we ask you to drink Vi-Cocoa. Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa is neither a medicine nor a mere thirst-assuager. It is a food at the same time that it is a beverage,and thus answers a double purpose in the building up of the human constitution. You can try it free of expense. Merit alone is what is claimed for Dr. Tibbles' Vi-cocoa,and the Proprietors' are prepared to send to any reader who names this Journal a dainty sample tin of Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa, free and post paid, upon receipt of a post card to the Head Oflice, 60, Bunhill Row, London, E. C.; or you can purchase a 6d. packet or 9d. or is. 6d., tin from any grocer or stores. Vi-Cocoa is the cheapest and best food beverage in the world.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>SILCOATES SCHOOL BURNT DOWN. A Serious Loss to Congregationalism.</head>
        <p>SILCOATES SCHOOL BURNT DOWN. A Serious Loss to Congregationalism.</p>
        <p>The Northern Congregational School, Silcoates, near Wakefield, was entirely destroyed by fire on Wednesday night. Fortunately the scholars were at home for the holidays, so that there was no danger on that score. Many of our readers will recollect that the headmaster of the school is Mr. J. A. Yonge M. A., the eldest son of the Rev. John Yonge, Warrington, both of whom are , to be sympathised with, in the severity of the blow. Mr. J. A. Yonge was in France when the catastrophe occurred, jusebringing to a close a holidAy in Switzer- • land, but he has since hastened to the scene of the disaster. Fortunately most of his household effects were saved.</p>
        <p>A Yorkshire Post "correspondent states that the outbreak was discovered about ten o'clock, and an alarm was sent to Wakefield, with a request for the aid of the fire brigade. Unfortunately, a year or two ago, the City Council passed a resolution that the brigade should not attend fires outside the city boundary, and as Silcoates is just beyond the limit, Captain Harris refused to allow the brigade to go. He did depart from the regulations, however, so far as to send a number of firemen. When these arrived they found that no water was to be had within a quarter of a mile of the place, so that the school was practically at the mercy of the flames.</p>
        <p>The school was, as its name indicates, a semi-denom- inational institution, owned and managed by a com- mittee. It is usually occupied by 80 to 100 boys,mostly the sons of Congregational ministers from all parts of the country. Fortunately the scholars have not at present returned to the school after the Easter holidays. As a matter of fact, the premises have for the last feW days been in the hands of the painters and decorators. The premises were extensive, the main block being three storeys high. No trustworthy estimate of the damage has, of course, been formed, but it will prob- ably be not less than £lO,OOO to £l2,oooand it is under- stood to be covered by insurance. The "Post "correspondent adds that when the fire was discovered. Mrs. Gill, the housekeeper, was having_ supper, along with the servants. During supper, one of the maids was led by a smell of smoke, to suspect an outbreak of fire, and a search being at once made, it was found that the third storey of the older portion of the premises was on fire.</p>
        <p>In a short time the flames had got completel6sses- sion of this portion of the premises. Fanned by a stiffish breeze from the west, the fire spread to the newer portion of the premises in front, used chiefly for scholastic purposes, and this part of the building waS also destroyed. Silcoates School was established in 1820, then being known as the Yorkshire Dissenters' Grammar School, but in 1832 it assumed a new basis, and was restricted to the education of ministers' sons. In 1855 it was decided to admit the sons of laymen, and this course has been followed since, although the majority of the pupils have always been ministers' sons. Some of the seholars have attained distinction in after-life among these being Sir George Newnes, M.P., Mr. W. T. Stead, and Mr. J. S. Fletcher, the novelist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>CATARRH AND INFLUENZA.</head>
        <p>CATARRH AND INFLUENZA.</p>
        <p>CATARRH is the result of a series of colds; the symptoms are :—Nose stopped up, difficulty in breath- ing, loose cough, with-continual hawking and expector- ating. VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE, quickly cures this condition.</p>
        <p>INFLUENZA is epidemic and contagious, most prO- valent in low-lying districts in spring months. During the severe epidemic which raged in North America, in 1897, VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE was the remedY chiefly used, and it reduced the death rate to such an extent that doctors never hesitated to prescribe it. It. is peculiarly well adapted to the speedy cure of both Influenza and Catarrh. Ask for VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE. Price Rd., is. lid., and 2s. 9c1., at Chemists and Drug Stores everywhere.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>No FREE LIBRARY FOR EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>The Newton-in-Makerfleld Urbah District Council, its closing meeting:of the year on Monday evening dr- cided by seven votes to six not to adopt the Public Free Libraries Act, despite that Mr. Carnegie had, offered £4,000. Lord Newton had promised a site, and a public meeting and a poll of the inhabitants had de- cided in its favour. The meeting was of a protracted and heated character.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>ASHTON COLLIER FOUND_ DROWNED.</head>
        <p>ASHTON COLLIER FOUND_ DROWNED.</p>
        <p>On Monday Mr. Brighouse held—aninCinei-i at GarS- wood on Thomas Naylor, aged fifty,who lived at Ashton. The deceased, a single man, had been off his work since Good Friday,and•had been drinking heavily. On Thurf" day night last his stepfather spoke to him about 1115 drinking habits, and the deceased left the house in e temper. The next morning his dead body was found iI a pond. The jury returned a verdict of " Founa drowned." •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>STREET_ BOOKMAKERS FINED.</head>
        <p>STREET_ BOOKMAKERS FINED.</p>
        <p>At thesNewton Police Court, this morning, Mattheif Seton, a bookmaker, of Earlestown, was fined £5 au" costs for having used Wellington-street, for the purpose of betting on the 4th April last. It was stated that the police were in hiding, and saw defendant accept e° fewer than 85 bets in less than an hour. For a similar offence on the 12th April he was fined £5 and costs.-- Frank Dale and Benjamin Howarth, both bookmakers, were also each mulcted in £5 and costs for betting in tbe same street • and Owen Moris, and James Twist were each fined,4os. and costs for aiding and abetting the bookmakers by acting as their touts.</p>
        <p>_ - FIRE- AT EARLESTOWN. Shortly after two o'clock on Monday afternoon the Newton-in-Makerfield Fire Brigade were called out to the private residence of Mr. Charles Waring, 5, Taro' worth street, Earlestown, a fire having occurred in the back bedroom. On the arrival of the brigode the fire had been extinguished, but not until about £lO worth of damage had been done. Some clothing, &amp;c., hod, been placed before the fire to air, and it was surmise(' that a spark from the fire caused the ignition. Ilea' window frame, together with a couple of chairs and be linen, was damaged. The brigade was Under the super' vision of Superintendent Hozzard. OLD FOLKS' CONCERT AT EARLESTOWN. _ _</p>
        <p>On Thursday evening a concert in aid of funds for the provision of a treat to the old folks of the district, Wag given in the Town Hall, Earlestown. The artistes well: all local, but an excellently varied programme was go' together and admirably submitted. Amongst those wile gave their services were the Brunswick Quartette Par* who submitted "Dear Little Shamrock," " The Fishing Story," and "Ye Catte," Miss Stronach, and Mr. J. Higham who sang with effect the Bell Ringer " and "Asleep in the Deep " ; Miss, Roberts, whose items were The Heavenly Song" au; " Tell me, my heart "and Mr. G. H.Herrod who recito• "The Lifeboat "and "In a signal box." Mr. Ashley gale a couple of clarionet solos " Maritana " and ,6 Scotep Melodies." The Viaduct Band Prize Quartet Party ga/e two selections " Scotia " and 66 Oberon" ; Mr. 3,6 Huddleston supplied the humour in "Poor, Prodig9 and Proud" and "Second time on earth." Mr. Dring presided at the piano. There was a large an; enthusiastic audience, and doubtless a substantiel amount will be secured.</p>
        <p>The Late Mr. James Tinsley, J.P. Before the commencement of the ordinary busineel at Daresbury Petty Sessions on Tuesday, celortei. Ridgway, who presided, said that since the last-meet- ing of the Court they had had a very great loss in the death of Mr. James Tinsley. As long as his health permitted, Mr. Tinsley was in regular attendance °Li the bench, and he (Colonel Ridgway) felt sure the sympathies of the Court would be with the family ill the, great loss they had sustained.—Mr. Browne, ell behalf of the solicitors, practising in the Court, endorsed the Chairman's remarks. They had alwaYe: he said, received the greatest courtesy,apd he though' he might say invariable justice at the hands of the late Mr. Tinsley.</p>
