      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>LEIGH T. BUNCO RN</head>
        <p>LEIGH T. BUNCO RN</p>
        <p>Played at Le1;71; windy weather, before 1,000 spectators. Runcorn had a weak team. Four quarters were played. owing to the wind. In the first quarter Johnson placed two penalty goals for Leigh. In the second, quarter Inglesfield, after a grand run, scored for Runcorn, a goal resulting. Nothing was scored in the last two quarters. Result—Leigh, 4 points; Runcorn, 3 points. SWINTON v. HULL KINGSTON ROVERS._</p>
        <p>_ Played at Swinton, before 1,000 spectators. The Rovers utilised the strong wind to advantage, and over- played their opponents, Gordon scoring a try in the first half, which Carmichael failed to improve. On crossing over the same player dropped a goal, the horn e lot being well beaten in the open game. Result—Hull Kingston Rovers, 5 points; Swinton, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>WIDNES v. OLDHAM.</head>
        <p>WIDNES v. OLDHAM.</p>
        <p>At Widnes, before 2,000 spectators. Both teams were well represented. Oldham had the advantage of a strong wind in the first half. They attacked strongly, but could not score. In the second half, Peacock and Alf. Taylor scored tries for Widnes,and Alf. Taylor and Ike. Taylor kicked goals. Result—Widnes, 2 goals 2 tries (10 points) ; Oldham nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>BROUGHTON RANGERS v. HUDDERSFIELD.</head>
        <p>BROUGHTON RANGERS v. HUDDERSFIELD.</p>
        <p>Played at Broughton, in fine weather, before 4,00 0 people. Wilson and Hogg were off the home side. Huddersfield attacked,lnd Larard and Deere each scored Holroyd kicked a goal. S. James kicked two penalty goals. Result—Huddersfield 1 goal 2 tries (8 points) ; Broughton Rangers; 2 goals 1 try (7 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>RUNCORN V. SALFORD.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN V. SALFORD.</p>
        <p>Owing to the cup ties, this match was postponed on April 2, and was played at Runcorn on Monday, before a poor attendance of spectators. Salford were strongly represented, but Runcorn turned out with a weak team. They had the assistance of the wind in the first half, but Salford quickly forced their way inside the home quarter, and White and Lomas scored tries, neither of which were converted. Salford continued to hold the upper hand, but no further scoring took place up to half-time, when Salford led by six points. The game in the second half continued in Salford's favour, and Lomas scored another try, which he failed to improve. Williams scored a try for Runcorn, and Brown dropped a goal for Salford. Result :—Salford, one goal and three tries (11 points) ; Runcorn, one try (three points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>SWINTON v. HUNSLET.</head>
        <p>SWINTON v. HUNSLET.</p>
        <p>This match, which had to be postponeci on March 19 owing to Hunslet being engaged in the cup ties, was played on Monday on the Swinton ground, before 2,000 spectators :—Hunslet, three goals and three tries (15 points) ; Swinton, three tries (nine points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>KRIGHLEY V. BRADFORD.</head>
        <p>KRIGHLEY V. BRADFORD.</p>
        <p>At Keighley, before 4,000 spectators. Bradford started uphill against a stiff breeze. Hutt scored smartly, and Mosby converted. Myers responded with a penalty goal. Eagers took a pass and scored, and Mosby again added the goal. Stacey scored a fine try, Myers failing. Keighley played well in the second half. Result—Bradford, 10 poihts ; ReighlM 7 points.</p>
        <p>BULL V. SALFORD. At Hull, before 5,000 spectators, Hull started against, a strong wind. Jackson kicked a penalty for the hoinesters three minutes from the start. Play fluctuated considerably during the remainder of the first half, both full backs displaying grand form. Half- time—Hull, 2 points; Salford .nil. The wind diminished when Lomas restarted. Hull assumed the aggressive Carvill scored, Jacltson converting. Salford were dis- appointing, the wing men particularly being weak. Lomas tried hard, but lacked support. Hull played strongly, and deserved their victory of 7 points to nil. OLDHAM v. KEIGHLEY.</p>
        <p>Oldham had Keighley for visitors on Tuesday. Cash scored two tries and A. Lees one, Fergusson kicking a goal from one of them. In the second portion Fergus- on kicked two penalty goals, and just before the close Stacey scored a try for the visitors, Oldham winning by three goals and three tries (15 points) to one try (three points).</p>
        <p>BRADFORD V. BATLEY</p>
        <p>At Park Avenue on Tuesday before 5,000 spectators. Bradford only put in a moderate team. Batley won the toss, and had the benefit of the wind in the first half. Neither side, however, could score. After twenty minutes' play in the second half Barker put on a try, Mosby improving. Bradford pressed to the close. Result :—Bradford, one goal and one try (five points) ; Batley, nothing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>LEEDS V. LEIGH.</head>
        <p>LEEDS V. LEIGH.</p>
        <p>I Played on Wednesday at Leeds, and resulted in a t draw of three points each. Grace and Burgess scored the tries.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <p>MR. BALFOUR'S DUMPING. I</p>
        <p>THE CHAMBERLAIN SCARE.</p>
        <p>NO CURE REQUIRED.</p>
        <p>BEYOND giving us the interesting informa- tion that he is opposed to "dumping," the Prime Minister has not given us any particu- lars of his Fiscal proposals. "The Govern- ment policy," says Mr. Balfour, "is a policy of Retaliation, and is opposed to dumping," but there he leaves the matter. If pressed for further information as to the details of his policy he begins to talk about Home Rule, Education, Temperance, or any other question under the sun save that which he is asked to answer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>Silence is Golden.</head>
        <p>Silence is Golden.</p>
        <p>TBERE is a method in all this madness. Mr. Balfour refuses to reply, for the very excellent reason that he has no reply to give. He does not explain his policy because he has no policy to explain. He does not give particulars of his proposals, because they will not stand either definition or description. Worked out to their logical conclusion—</p>
        <p>Retaliation means Protection. The only cure for dumping is prohibition of</p>
        <p>imports. But Mr. Balfour tells us he is opposed to Protection, and must, of course, be still more opposed to Prohibition. Thus faced with his own complete refutation of his policy, he finds that, under the circumstances, silence is the only means of safety.</p>
        <p>Dumpophobia. MR. CHAMBERLAIN has the proud reputation of being the originator of the fear of " dumping " in the mind of the British elector. It has been described as " Dumpo- phobia." At one time many people were bitten by this form of Fiscal madness.. Some- thing like a panic seized numbers of respect- able and prosperous citizens who had previously lived in peaca and contentment, all unconscious of the fell disease with which the hated foreigner had impregnated British trade by sending us his materials under cost price. Lulled into security by the numerous evidences of prosperity, fattening upon the very cheap imports which Mr. Chamberlain now described as though they were deadly poison to our trade, the• British electors, for the most part, had never heard of "dumping." Even the Prime Minister appears :to have been all unconscious of the ten ible disease in our commerce. In agonising tones Mr. Chamberlain warned us of the terrible results of buying cheaply, and pictured to our frightened imaginations—</p>
        <p>hundreds of thousands of English workmen thrown out of employment in order to make room for the cheap imp rts of hundreds of thou- s Ands of American workmen, who are kept in cmployment during bad times by this system (dumping).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>Philanthropist and Demon Combined.</head>
        <p>Philanthropist and Demon Combined.</p>
        <p>• HE told us how the terrible American manufacturers ran their works at a loss for the two sole objects of not injuring their country and keeping their men in work. Noble, patriotic. self-sacrificing American capitalists, employers, trusts I now sadly you have, hitherto, been slandered by an ignoi ant public! It required Mr. Joseph Chamberlain, that distinguished friend of all foreigners, to unearth your virtues! The only sad part about your noble conduct towards your country and your workmen is that, according to Mr. Chamberlain, it is aimed at the dgstruction of the trade of Great Britain and the happiness of the British worker. The American employer seems to be, according to Mr. Chamberlain, a sort of philanthropist and demon combined.</p>
        <p>The Awakening. THE result of Mr. Chamberlain's scare about the awful result of importing " dumped " goods from the foreigner led to a careful examination into international trade, and a special inquiry as to the origin, causes, and cure of this awful disedse "Dumpophobia." The result was to assure the public that it was only a disease of the imagination, and not a disorder of the national organisation. The report of the examination. produced the follow- ing definition of " dumping " : _ 1. The foreigner cannot "dump" into this counf ry at will. 2. Dumring only takes place by the consent of the people of 11-;is country. 3. Dumped goods are introduced into this country throut7h the bad habit of the British . people, who will persist in buying in the cheapest market. 4. The foreigner only offers to dump goods into Great Erit,iin when: (a) flis own countrymen are not able to buy them. (b) When he has more goods than he can dis- pose of at their normal price in any other part of the world. (c) When lie is in such financial difficulties that he is forced to sell his goods for anything • they will fetch. 5. Dumi big is a sign of adversity on the part of the dumper, and not of prosperity. 6. The forei.ner, by selling his goods to us cheaper than he sells them to his fellow-country- men, inflicts a far greater injury upon them than upon anybody in the United Kingdom. 7. By purchasing "dumped" materials cheaply, the British manufacturer is able to undersell the foreigner manufacturer in the very country from which the "dumped" materials come. 8. More people are employed in Great Britain in making up the "dumped' material into manu- factures than would be employed if that material were produced at home. 9. The wages of workmen are higher and the skill is greater in manufacturing that in the unskilled labour of producing "dumped" material. British workmen are, therefore, better employed in skilled manufacture than in unskilled labour. • 10. Since Mr. Chamberlain discovered "dump- ing" there has been more material produced in Great Britain and more material manufactured than there has ever been known before in the history of British trade.</p>
        <p>No Cure Required</p>
        <p>THE above statements clearly prove that as "dumping" is not a disease, no cure for "dumping" is required. This may account for Mr. Balfour's silence on the question. But even if it were necessary to try and stop "dumping," nobody has yet told us how to accomplish that cure except by prohibiting all foreign goods from cording into the country. But that cure is akin to having a man's head cut off to prevent toothache. The foreigner does not send his goods labellcd " Cumpecl." A protective euty 1‘ Cu'rl not shut out "dumptd " pc.ri ro high that pobody . tl7cm at all.</p>
