      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>'11Mt"" ar-lee"1"411111</head>
        <p>'11Mt"" ar-lee"1"411111</p>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR. RESYO 16.</p>
        <p>Authors of letters intended for publication must mall eases send their names and addresses to the Editor as a guarantee of good faith. Matter intended for publication must be written on one aids only of the paper wad seat in, if possible,not later than Thursday.</p>
        <p>Councillors Maginn and Stott. Sir,—As one of the public who was present at the I Town Council meeting last Tuesday evening, I wish to offer a strong protest against the violent and disgrace- ful attack made on the tabour Councillor by Councillor Maginn. Such base insinuations as were made by him against Councillor Stott were nothing less than a scandal and ought Us have been protested against by Councillor Stott at the time and a withdrawal of the slanderous remarks demanded. I have known Mr. Stott for a considerable number of years and in all that time have found him to be exceed- ingly straight and honest. He is at the present time devoting himself to the improving of the conditions of his fellow trade-unionists and the working classes generally. He is not only sacrificing his leisure, and money,but is also endangering hishealth by hisstrenuous work for the Labour cause, and I trust that the War- rington workingmen will offer a strong protest against the unwarrantable attack of Councillor Magian. What was the cause of this violent outburst by the representative of St. John's Ward? It was simply be- cause Councillor Stott thought it necessary to correct the statement which Councillor Maginn made at the Council meeting held in March and to point out his in- consistency. In the discussion on the tender for sani- tary pails and ashbins at that meeting, Councillor Maginn stated that the acceptance of the tender of the Manchester firm meant a loss of £l7 or £lB to the ratepayers, and at the same meeting he also said that the standing order of the Conncil,which stipulates that all contractors shall pay the recognised rate of wages for the district to all those engaged on the works, was a wrong principle altogether. Mr. Stott pointed out that the difference in the two tenders was only half the amount stated by Councillor Maginm and also reminded him that when he was the candidate for St. John's Ward he promised to support the principle of trade-union wages being given in all Corporation contracts, and the result of these promises was that Mr. Maginn secured a large number of trade unionists' votes. He now declares that itis a wrong principle, and that in his opinion the lowest tender should always be accepted, except it be to give a privi- lege to a local firm. I therefore contend that the charge of inconsistency made by Councillor Stott was perfectly justified.—Yours etc.,</p>
        <p>TRADE UNIONIST.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>Budget.</head>
        <p>The Budget.</p>
        <p>Sir,—The introduction of the Budget for the current municipal year was delayed until so late an hour on Tuesday night that any really serious or useful discus- sion was practically impossible, and those members of the Council who had the temerity to speak at all, realised they were in an atmosphere of impatience, and had to so condense and hurry their remarks that their purport was not clearly established. I feel that this was so in my own case, and I should therefore like to recapitulate the only points I really desired to make. First as to the comparative estimates of last year and the current year. An analysis of the Budget estimates for the two years,shews that the estimated expenditure for last year was £66,178 145., but as at the beginning of that year there was £4,456 16s. 10d. in the hands of the Bankers, it was only necessary to raise the differ- encejnamely £61,731 17s. 2d. out of the rates. The actual expenditure last year, however, amounts to £71,440 75., and the resulting deficit of £5,261 13s. id. In other words the overdraft at the Bankers at the close of last year has had to be added to the amount of the estimated expenditure of £69,158 10s. Bd. for this or the current year, making a total sum of £74,420 3s. to be now provided by the rates. The estimated amounts to be taken from the rates for the past five years have been, according to the printed Budgets, as follows:</p>
        <p>£ s. d. April 1899 ... 5 years ago ... 36;224 5 4 , 1900 ... 4 „ „ ... 40,591 11 11 1901 ... 3 „ „ 53,509 4 4 f „ 1902 ... 2 53,161 16 2 ft 1903 ... 1 „ „ 61,731 17 2 f t 1904 ... this year ... 74,420 3 8 The actual expenditure for last year, as I have said, totalled up to £71,440 7s. and the estimated expenditure (originally £69,158 10s. Bd.) was finally fixed by the Council on the recommendation of the Finance Com- mittee on Tuesday night at £67,330 10s. Bd., or an estimated reduction as compared with last year's actual outlay of £4,109 16s. 4d. As I stated at the Council meeting, I have grave doubts as to the sufficiency of this final estimate. I think the provision for Parliamentary and Legal expenses altogether inadequate, I deprecate the deletion of the £756 for actually accrued interest on the pur- chase money for the Howley land, and r consider the striking out of the £202 for the Branch Libraries is altogether indefensible. Nevertheless as the rates are to be put up 6d. in the £, despite the earmark- ing £B,OOO of gas works profits—profits arising from the excessive price of gas, exacted largely from the ratepayers,—and, notwithstanding that only £1,117 19s. sd. more than for last year is required by the educational authority, I had not the heart to move any further increase in the rates. How the manufac- turers, the struggling tradesmen and workers are to face and survive these ever increasing burdens I can- not conceive. Assessments have been raised without easing the rating, which makes the incubus all the more burdensome : indeed there is something of the tragic in the whole subject. I feel so very strongly that I can hardly abstain from attacking what I conceive to be the main origin and cause gf these lamentable consequences. But I forbeir; for while I cannot understand the lavish and reckless outlay of many past years on any other grounds than twit of creating business, I cannot bring myself to think that any man I know would deliberatelyso prosti- tute his position. Second, the ratepayers must unite to bring about, at the earliest moment a drastic change; as Mr. Alder- man Sutton, the father of the Council, so admirably put it in the Council Chamber: "We are all 'non-traders' now. If that be no, and I certainly believe" trading" will never again be tolerated inWarrington—something has been achieved. The plague has been stopped, but it may take a generation to restore healthy finance and the pristine purity of Warrington public life. What a suggestive account is that of the retirement, of William Beamont, our first Mayor, from our Connell ! There was a probability that some property in which he had some slight—l believe indirect—interest might be required for some public purpose. He at once re- signed his aldermanic position, stating that there ought not to be a breath of suspicionabout the bona-fides of a public man. The Council thought he was too sensitive. and an address was presented to him, signed by every member of the Council asking him to withdraw his resignation and assuring him that everybody knew that be of all men was incapable of a selfish thought. But the grand old man would not budge; he said that public men could not be too sensitive, and on no account could he allow himself to be in a doubtful position, even before his own conscience,however completely his colleagues and the public might confide in him. This is delightful, and if the electors of Warrington are as capable and as shrewd as I believe them to be, II feel sure they will realise the dire necessities of the present position, and despite all party machinations ' and compacts—th, curses of municipal life—see to it that only disinterested and fearless repre- sentatives are henceforth elected members of the Council. That the ratepayers are begin- ning to stir themselves I am satisfied. A letter from a large and leading ratepayer lies before me now. He desires that T should give notice in the Council of a motion, which he has drafted. to change the date of the introduction of the annual budget from April to October, so that the electors may vote imme- diately upon the fixing of the rates. Another ratepayer tells me of the abandonment of a project to establish a nem works at Latchford last summer,. owing to the heaviness of the Warrington rates. It is a hopeful sign to have these indications of renewed and growing interest in the vital crisis whichhas overtaken us in our local public affairs.—l am, &amp;c.. April 11th.</p>
        <p>JAMES DODDS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>Chinese Labour.</head>
        <p>Chinese Labour.</p>
        <p>Mr. Grimshaw's Reply to Mr. Parker.</p>
        <p>- - Sir,—l did not anticipate to convince Mr. Parker that the introduction of Chinese into the Transvaal is either wise or right, for it is easy to see that Mr. Parker has a great deal to learn of the economic condi- tions of South Africa before he will realize the necessity of such a step. I objected to some of his assertions in his previous letter—drst, because they were not right, and, secondly, because they would convey a wrong idea to others who know nothing of the question or South African conditions. T notice Mr. Parker does not attempt to substantiate his former assertions in any way, but proceeds to put many ques- tions and which, I should like to answer fully. but I am afraid, Mr. Editor, you would object to me taking up so much space in your paper. First it is impossible to work the mines of South Africa under conditions they are worked in other parts of the world, because white men cannot live decently under £l6 a month; the per- centage of gold obtained from the ore is so small and the depths of the working so great. Quoting from my letter "The people are not desirous of seeing white men do natives' work Scc," he asks "Who are these people who desire to govern the situation ?" 1 think I made it pretty plain that I spoke on behalf of the working men and residents of the Rand, who are quite competent to 'govern the situa- tion' and who have not let the mining magnates have all their own way. A man in the Colonies does not as a rule think any work degrading, be it mining or any- thing else. but. say that it is absolutely essential that the prestige of the white race over the natives in South Airica should he maintainel, forif ever there is trouble there again,it will not be with the Dutch but the natives. Mr. Parker goes' onto quote the words .of one of the mining directors, and though he speaks in the first per- son does not give the name. The words of</p>
        <p>Liao mining dife3t3ri I hav'e no sympathy with, but I can I tell Mr. Parker this: that though the mine managers I did reduce the native wages, they very soon found out that they made a mistake (as employers of labour in this country do sometimes in doing &amp;similar thing) and the native wages were very soon raised, and on the whole natives are better paid now in South Africa than ever they were: and that speaks well for the Chinese being treated properly, for the mine managers would not be so foolish as to do anything to jeopardise the supply of labour. So far hack as last June when I was, with the majority of the people of the Rand, opposed to imported labour, I heard a Dutch farmer at a public meeting in Johannes- burg speak in favour of importing Chinese, because, he said, he was sick and tired of the cheek of the Kaffirs, who wanted more wages now than ever, and he thought the Chinese would do good by creating competition in the native labour. Mr. H. P. and a Rensourg member of the Transvaal Legislative Council, speaking at Pretoria on behalf of the farmers, said that complaints regarding the shortage of labour came from them and I not from the mines. f the shortage of labour continued the farmers' wives and children would have to work the farms, as some indeed were doing at the present ' time. Mr. Andries Cronje also a member of the Coun- cil, spoke on the suffering of the farmers whose chil- dren could not go to school because they had to do the work in the fields which should be done by Eaffirs. Will Mr. Parker admit that the mining magnates are not the only people to be considered now? He says that he does not believe it is "the only way out of the difficulty." I still say it is,and main- tain that it has been proved by the general opinion in South Africa. What do the Transvaal ministers say in a recent letter? "Many, nay, indeed, the vast majority of our laymen, members of our churches, earnest Christian men, men on whose judgment we have long relied and of whose integrity we have no shadow of doubt, feel utterly convinced that such imported labour is needed and desirable." • Mr. Parker says those who have visited the Com- pounds tell us that the conditions are degrading to humanity. Who are these people? I have never heard anyone say so and in my opinion (which perhaps isn't worth much), the conditions are not half so degrading as the way they exist in their kraals,which is demoralizing and disgusting. There you see them lying on the ground like fat hogs and never doing any work while their wives (perhaps two or three to each man) are working away in the mealie patch and their piccan- ninies pounding mealies for food. When the final vote was taken on the importation of Chinese Labour by the Transvaal Legislative Council, Sir God- crey Lagden, Commissioner of Native Affairs (and what greater authoirty can we have ?). spoke at great length on the improvement now manifest in the condition of native labourers in the mines. The native labourer on the Rand, he declared, was as comfortable and as well looked after now as the miners in any part of England. T:ze opinions of Archdeacon Wilson on this question are to me unworthy of consideration, for he also tells us that he does not believe the ministers of the Rand. The Archbishop of Canterbury declared that he could not have voted for the measure had he not been in the Transvaal. Again, quoting from the letter from the Nonconformist Ministers of the Rand, "We beg once again that our brethren at home will refrain from language which is evidently based on imperfect information and apparently influenced by party exigencies. But we protest against the imputations conveyed in the language used,a,s being grossly unjust to those we represent." Surely any right thinking person ought to admit that the people in South Africa are quite competent to govern their own affairs. In conclusion I would ask Mr. Parker to com- pare the terms for the Chinese with the terms under which a man joins the Army. They are quite as good for the former, as the terms are for the latter. Is this slavery or serfdom !—Yours, Scc., F. B. GRTMSHAW. Sankey, April 14th, 1904.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>TRADES AND LABOUR COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>TRADES AND LABOUR COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>MR. PIERPOINT, M P., AND YELLOW</p>
        <p>LABOUR.</p>
        <p>" Protest Based on Insufficient Foundation."</p>
        <p>Thera was some interesting business at the monthly meeting of the Warrington Trades and Labour Council held in Bold-street Wesleyan schools on Tuesday even- ing. Mr. J. Podmore, jun., presided. The Secretary (Councillor F. Stott) was engaged at the Town Council meeting, until nearly ten c'lock and in his absence Mr. Fargher discharged these cretarial duties. THE TARIFF COMMISSION'S QUESTIONS.—</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>CO UNCI L'S RETOR,T.</head>
        <p>CO UNCI L'S RETOR,T.</p>
        <p>The Executive Committee, at the request of the Coun- cil, recently considered a series of questions forwarded by the Tariff Commission. The following resolution was passed :—" Having regard to the insult which Mr. Chamberlain offered to Trade Unionists and their leaders in his Liverpool address on the fiscal question, also that the Tariff Commission has been unofficially appointed and is composed almost entirely of men holding protectionist views, and does not include a single direct labour representative, it is the opinion of this committee that no useful purpose can be served by forwarding answers to the questions received from your committee." Mr. BUCKLEY thought it would have been useful to have drawn up replies to the questions, simply for the information of the Trades Council, but he believed it would have involved a great deal of work. He quite agreed with the Committee's decision not to send answers to the Tariff Commission.</p>
        <p>The Council approved the action of the Executive Committee.</p>
        <p>MR. PIERPOINT AND CHINESE LABOUR.</p>
        <p>CORRESPONDENCE WITH 1:11IE SECRETARY</p>
        <p>' It was reported that the Council's resolution p—r-o-test- ing against the introduction of indentured Chinese labour into South Africa had been forwarded to Mr. R. Pierpoint, M.P. who replied to the Secretary as follows: —" I have duly received your letter of March 10th. If , you will look at the "Times " for March 12th or some other newspaper of the same date you will find much about the labour ordinance which shows that the reso- lution, a copy of which you have sent to me, is based on insufficient foundation." To this Councillor Stott , replied, asking Mr. Pierpoint if he could supply him with a copy of the ' Times ' for March 10th, as he had been unable to get one, and also aski9ghim for a copy of the labour ordinance. Mr. Pierpoint acknowledged the receipt of this letter, and wrote later enclosing a copy of the labour ordinance, and the extracts from the "Times "to tvhich he referred in his previous letter, expressing thehope that they would be of assistance to Councillor Stott in understanding what was a most difficult question. The letter continued" Apart alto- gether from what are broadly speaking party politics, I desire to draw your attention to the grave responsi- bility which any man takes upon himself, when being far away from the country concerned, he disagrees with the almost unanimously expressed desire of that country expressed by those who live in it, and who presumably know its requirements." To this letter Councillor Stott had beea unable to reply through pressure of business. He, however, in- tended to do so at the earliest possible moment, giving th reasons why the Council described the question as slavery and protested against it: Mr. BUCKLEY said he thought, that the correspond- ence at least showed that Mr. Pierpoint was taking an interest in the Fluestion, although their opinions might differ especially with regard to the so-called unanimous expression by people in South Africa in favour of the ordinance. The working classes of the Transvaal had not expressed themselves in favour of the ordinance and the petitions in favour of it had been got up by coercion. It was decided to leave the matter of continuing the correspondence in the hands of the secretary. CORPORATION CONTRACTS.</p>
        <p>Mr. PODMORE read a letter from the National Amal- gamated Iron Plate Trade Society desiring to tender their thanks to Councillor Stott for the interest taken by him in Corporation contracts, especially with refer- ence to the contracts for sanitary pails and ashbins. Whatever the result of that action might betthey would always hold in grateful remembrance the attempt he had made to get those articles made under fair con- ditions. The like courage and determination as was shown by Councillor Stott they had not found in any other labour representative on a municipal local govering body, and they hoped that the working men of Warrington would strengthen his hands by sending more men of his stamp to the Town Council. TRADES DISPUTES BILL _</p>
        <p>- —.— It was decided to write asking Mr. R. Pierpointe M.P.. to support the Trades Disputes Bill to be intro- duced into the House of Commons. THE CORPORATION AND THE UNENIPLOYEb. Mr. BUCKLE'( pointed out that the Corporation were providing work for the unemployed, but a case had been brought to his notice in which the standard rate of wages was not being paid. The Water Committee had engaged a joiner, but they were paying him only Bd. an hour which was 11:d below the standard rate. He moved that the secretary be instructed to inquire into the matter and that the Council protest most strongly against the actiop of the Water Committee. The motion was unanimously carried. THE DISCHARGE OF TRAMWAY EMPLOYEES, Mr. PLINSTON called attention to the action of th Tramways Committee in discharging 16 of their em- ployes. The men affected were mainly motor men and conductors. He did not object to the Corporation dis- charging employes if they found themselves overstaffed, but he thought it strange that under the old manage- ment the present staff was considered necessary. The grievance was that the men dismissed were not all new-corners, but in several cases they had been employed on the servicesince its inauguration. A num- ber of the men were married, and he contended that employes who had obtained situations in response to advertisements ouglat at least to receive more than seven days' notice. When Thursday came round it meant that some men would be turned adrift amongst the unemployed as they had not had time to obtain other employment. (Hear,hear.) Councillor STorr said the Corporation were making an effort to find employment in other towns for some of the men.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>SOCIAL SERMONETTES FOR WORKING MEN,</head>
        <p>SOCIAL SERMONETTES FOR WORKING MEN,</p>
        <p>CALL RIGHTS RESERVED.]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>BY JOHN TAYLOR. Amtkor of `• Search Lights on. Gaga, Labottr, and Social Problems."</head>
        <p>BY JOHN TAYLOR. Amtkor of `• Search Lights on. Gaga, Labottr, and Social Problems."</p>
        <p>OUR PROTECTIONIST COLONIES.</p>
        <p>"One of the greatest obstacles tio the advance of I Free Trade has been that the working man has con- nected high wages with Protection. Experience has taught him, however, that the reverse is the truth, because the low prices of commodities in- crease the demand for them, and also for the labour which produces them. It is Protection which lessens the demand for laboue, and it is Free Trade that expands it."-LORD PLAYFAIR. The fallacies of Protection would deceive the very elect. They are about as elusive and deceptive as thimble-rigging, or the three card trick. The working man who knows nothing about economic law or political economy is easily persuaded that the more work other people do, the less there will be for him to do. And when he has swallowed this silly sophistry, he thinks it is a good thing to put a high price on the bit of work he does, and shut out every other man's work from his markets by prohibitive tariffs and heavy import duties. He believes this policy will give him high wages, so he shuts his eyes to the teaching of all experience, and to ' the arguments of tll the experts, and nothing but bitter experience will ever bring him to his senses. The only two Protectionist nations in the world that give higher wages to their -workmen than the British w orking man can earn are, the United States and our Protectionist colonies. And these higher wages are not due to Protection, but to monopolies and other excep- tional advantages. Our self-governing colonies are bitterly opposed to Free Trade. - They will not have our able-bodied work- ing men as settlers if they can help it. They putheavy tariffs on our engines and machinery to encourage home industries. They tax our calico, and woollen goods,and h ardvrare, and cuCery,to keep them out of the colonial markets. They build a wall of Protective barriers, not. only against foreign nations and the mother country,but against each other. In Australia, for instance, Queens- land and Victoria, and all the other provinces are cutting each other's throats, and carrying on an exhaus tire tariff against each other, as if they were foreign countries engaged in an industrial war. They cheerfully tax themselves to beggar their neighbours, and the artificially increase the cost of living in the belief that they will get higher wages in return. But does it pay? Can this foolish game last? I.—OUR PROTECTIONIST COLONIES CON- TROL THE LABOUR MARKET.</p>
        <p>This is one of the secrets of the high wages in the colonies. • Wages, like all other things that have any commercial value depend not upon the cost of produc- tion, but upon the law of supply and demand. The workman in Australia does not get seven shillings a day because he earns it, but because he is able to put a ring fence round every island and province,to keep all cheap labour out of the country, and then he can dictate his own terms at the ballot box. Let us see how icom- pletely labour s master of the situation by these methods.</p>
        <p>The Austmlian immigration law contains a clause de- claring that "Any person under a contract or agree- ment to perform manual labour within the Common- wealth shall be forbidden to land." They will welcome some rich aristocratic settler from England with open arms, but he must not bring his gardener, or his coach- man, or his English servants. He must leave them all behind, and he must employ highly paid colonials, with little ability or experience, because they happen to be on the spot, and entitled to all the plums and plunder. The avowed object of this law is to exclude destitute aliens and undesirables.lts real objectis to keep upcolonial wages by shutting out all foreign competition. This may be a good thing for the privileged monopolist colonial workman, but it is a very bad thing for the development of the resources of the country. It pre- vents the opening of new mines and the introduction of fresh industries and trades, and it cripples the colony in a thousand ways. A year or more ago there were no hat manufacturers in Australia, and all the felt and silk hats worn there had to be imported from this country. Some enterpris- ing individual built suitable premises, and set up machinery, and hired half-a-dozen practical hatters from the neighbourhood of Denton and Stockport to go out there and establish a local industry. When these Denton hatters arrived in Australia the trade unionists were up in arms, and clamouring for the expulsion of these new corners. They wanted the authorities to pre- vent them from landing, and to ship them back to England by the very next boat. They would not have skilled labourers imported to the colony to com- pete with them and to take the bread out of their mouths. The authorities asked the selfish dogs in the manger to find them any colonist who could make hats, and to prove that this new trade would take the bread out of anybody's mouth. There was a hot and angry controversy for ever so long, and the trade unionists had to climb down at the firush,but if this is the attitude of the colonial workman towards his brethren in this country, T can well understand that there will not be much love lost between them.</p>
        <p>In Cape Colony they have a law that every emigrant who comes to their shores, and desires to settle there, must pass a stiff medical examination, and be able to read and write, and he must have fifteen or twenty pounds in his possession. If he fails to pass all these tests to their satisfaction, the authorities put him on board the ship that brought him, and send him back to his own country. When this drastic law came into operation, hundreds of sober, intelligent, industrious working men were shipped back to England for the un- pardonable sin of poverty. They had not fifteen or twenty pounds left after paying their passage out, so they were returned as undesirables. In Canada the same exclusive monopolist spirit con- trols not merely the labour market, but it extends to the lawyers and the doctors and the learned profes- sions. It is all very well for the Canadians to boast of their freedom, but they impose some senseless and vexa- tious restrictions on those who desire to settle in their country. If men are willing to be hewers of wood and drawers of water, and drudges, and slaves,they are made welcome to their slavery. But if they have any brains or talents that would make them into formidable com- petitors with the men on the spot, they are circum- scribed and restricted in every way. You have a good example of this in the medical profession. No British doctor, however learned or well qualified, can practice there without first obtaining a local diploma. And if he wants to practice all over the Dominion he must have a separate diploma for each province. If he takes a degree in Ontario,•he must only visit patients in that, particular area. if he crosses the river, and sees any- body else on the other side,he is liable to prosecution. Now, I regard this as trades unionism gone mad. It is a system of tyranny and despotism that ought to be impossible in a free country. Canada is crying out for more emigrants, but she wants to pick and choose them for herself. She does not want educated men and women, so she, frames her trade union laws to exclude them. She wants men strong in the arm and weak in the head. The roughest pick-and-shovel hand can get six or seven shillings a day in the tovvrisi and in the outlying country places ke can earn a good deal more. But these rough labourers have their own unions to keep up prices, and to exclude foreign workmen, and to discourage emigration from other countries. lI.—OUR PROTECTIONIST COLONIES HAVE CAPTURED THE LEGISLATURES.</p>
        <p>Every working man has a vote, and he uses it, not for the good of his country, but to promote his own interests. He insists on working only eight hours a day and he will have six or seven shillings a day, whether he earns it or not. If private employers cannot afford to pay theseprices, then he becomes a glorified pauper, and he makes the State pay him his standard rate of wages for relief works and public improvements, and give him an old age pension into the bargain. There are thousands of these monopolist workmen in Sydney, and all the great centres of population, who are doing as little work as they can and starving on seven shillings a day. They have such a high old time at their coun- try's expense that they have to close their ports against our "Tired Tins" and "Weary Willies". who are eager to join them. But where does the money come from to pay all these big wages and old age pensions? It is every penny of It borrowed from the British investor, and spent with child-like simplicity or criminal recklessness. Thousands of miles of unprodn_tive railways have been built in Australia and New Zealand with this borrowed money and millions of unproductive capital sunk, and yielding no return. In 1902 the railways in Victoria were worked at a loss of £163,000. though the Govern- ment granted a subsidy of .t 86,000 in relief of the carriage on coal and corn. When the colonial railways were first built, nobody there could build railway engines or rolling stock, so these things had to be bought in England, or in the United States. Bat the Protectionists who could not make e igines decided to buildshops,and buy machinery, and teach their own people to do this work, if the engines cost ten times as much as we should charge for them. ' Dick S3ddon, the King of New Zealand, says he will give us no more orders for engines or rolling stock. He knows very well that his own workmen. will not be able forrainy years to build engines as serv!cTlble and cheap as those he has bought. So he is williag to tax his ' people, to subsidise the local engineers, and to give a bounty on every engine they build. In other words, he is willing to pay a fancy price loran inferior article in order to keep our goods out of his market. If this is the treatment we get\ from him, why should we wel- come him with open arms to our family, and provide him with an army and navy free gratis,and for nothing. By the time you have mastered these facts and figures it will begin to dawn on you that the high wages paid in our colonies are not due to the high price of produc- tion, or the effects of Protective tariffs. They are due to the monopoly of labour, and to the ability to carry on so many public works with borrowed money. The most alarming thing about these colonial debts</p>
        <p>liiiii</p>
        <p>is the rapidity of their growth. Thirty years ago th ' entire liabilities of our Australian colonies were only £28,300,000. In 1880 they had grown to £61,300,000. In 1890 they were £143,000,000, and at the beginning of 1900 they were above £215,000,000. South Australia with a population about the size of Dublin, has a debt of £26,156,180. This works out at £7O per head for every man, woman and child in the colony. If the mother country owed so much per head our national debt would be £2,800,000,000. The time is fast coming when everybody in Australia will be living on the State or plundering the British in- vestor. When the investor buttons up his pocket and refuses to lend any more money, there will soon be an end to this phenomenal and reckless prosperity. The colonies will have a high old time as long as they can borrow plenty of money, and never repay it. When they get to the end of their credit, and can borrow no more, they will have to turn over a new leaf, and shift for themselves or starve.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>ST. PAUL'S ANNUAL SALE OF WORK.</head>
        <p>ST. PAUL'S ANNUAL SALE OF WORK.</p>
        <p>A Satisfactory Result.</p>
        <p>As regularly as Spring comes round, the annual sale of work in connection with St. Paul's Church is held. This year an effort was made to raise £3OO for parochial institutions, and despite the many calls upon the parishioners and friends, the sum of £278 16s. 9d. was taken at the stalls. Alderman Smethurst has since given a donation of £25 towards the Yendowment fund, thus bringing up the total proceeds to £3o3—the high- est sum yet raised at this annual sale. A choice assortment of goods had been tastefully arranged on the stalls by a willing band of.workers. The stalls were in charge of the following ladies No. 1 Working Party Stall:—Mesdames Gibson, Smith, Lord, Robson, Chorley, Worrall and the Misses Reid and Pierpoint. No. 2 Stall :—The Misses White, Holly House, Sankey-street, in place of the Misses Geddes. No. 3 (Vicarage) Stall:—Mesdames Bracecamp, Barber, Lindley, J. Lawton and Locker. - No. 4 Stall :—Mesdames A. H. Burgess, Truslove, H. Collin, Hankinson, and Miss Edith Warburton. No. 5 Stall :—Misses Finney and Fowler. No. 6 Stall:—Mesdames Parsonage, J. Robinson and Albert Binks.</p>
        <p>No. 7 Stall (advertisement) :—Mesdames John Rigby, Turner, Cooper and Ayr. No. 8 Stall :—The Misses Dolly Braceeamp and Jolley. Refreshment Stall :—Mesdames Paul, Laughton, and the Misses Westwell, Houghton, Smethurst (2), Pleton, Rogers, and Ashurst. Flower Stall:—The Misses Wainwright, Greenough and F. Wood. • _ _</p>
        <p>Art Gallery :—Master Frank Bracecamp. The art gallery arranged by Master Frank Bracecamp was a curiosity in its way, and considerable ingenuity had been displayed by the young exhibitor in giving a mock title to his exhibits. Even a stately Churchwarden must have smiled blandly when he saw himself repre- sented by two long clay pipes, and "Mystery" had a fitting representative in a sausage. An excellent tea room had been provided and it was well patronised. At the opening ceremony on Monday afternoon, the Vicar (Rev. W. Bracecamp) presided and he was accom- panied on the platform by Mrs. John White, the Rev. T. W. Barber (curate), Mr. J. 11. Smethurst (vicar's warden), Mr. A. H. Burgess (people's warden), Alder- man Sutton, Alderman Smethurst, and Mr. Robson J. Chorley. The VICAR said as a congregation they tried to help themselves as would be seen when he stated that dur- ing the past 12 months they had raised £1,200, which was no little sum when it was remembered that the parishioners were all made up of the working classes, and that they had in the Bewsey district at least 3,00 J of the poorest of the poor. Money was required for the assistance of the clergy,the mission lady, and other organizations whichhad to be supported, and he hoped, I therefore, that all would do their best to help them. ' They appreciated Mrs. White's kindness in being present but were sorry her husband was not with them. (Hear, I hear.) The sympathy of Mr. and Mrs. White for every , good work was known in Warrington' especially in con- nection with the Church of England. (Hear, hear and applause.) They missed that afternoon two or three friends who had been in the habit of attending these sales for years, but, unfortunately, family bereavement', kept them away. He referred to the Misses Geddes,' who had lost a relative whose funeral was taking place that day. Mrs. JOHN WHITE,in declaring the sale of work open, said the Vicar had left very little for her to say, except to remind them that in a large and important parish like St. Paul's there were many organizations that must be maintained, and they could not be maintained with- out funds. The Vicar had to depend almost entirely on the annual sale of work for the necessary funds, which were becoming larger every year. She hoped the I sales would be larger this year than ever. Alderman SUTTON, in moving a vote of thanks to Mrs. White for her services' alluded to the time when Mr. John White's fnther and family worshipped at St. Paul's. That was more than 50 years ago, but it was within his (the speaker's). recollection, in fact, he believed Mr. Henry White was one of the first church- wardens there, if not the very first. Mr. and Mrs. John White had always been good friends to the churc'nes,and they also took a deep interest in all that concerned the welfare of the town. -</p>
        <p>Mr. J. H. SMETHURST seconded the motion, and alluded to the beautiful ladies who year after year rallied round them, and did what they could to make the sale a success. (Applause). The motion was heartily agreed to, and Mrs. WHITE briefly returned thanks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>DRAUGHTS.</head>
        <p>DRAUGHTS.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON v. WIDNES.</p>
        <p>A match between Warrington and Widnes was played at the Workmen's Club,Widnes, on Tuesday evening and the result was a somewhat easy victory for the visitors. This is the Widnes Club's first season and they have some very promising players among their ranks, which augers well for the club's future prospects of success, as they will doubtless improve by practice. There is some talk of forming a League composed of Warring- ton, Widnes, St. Helens, and Runcorn clubs., This would greatly increase the interest taken in the con- tests and also improve the standard of play all round. Score:—</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>WIDNES.</p>
        <p>. _ Wins. Draws. Wins. '2 F. Dunne 0 R. Plunington 0 2 J. Roberts 2 W. Tomkinson O P. Walsh 1 A Bennett 2 W. Rigby 0 E. Kirby 2 E. J. Powell 1 C. Powell 0 W. F Shaw 1 O W. Warburton 1 W. Hunt 2 J. F. Dixon 0 J. Clibborn 0 O J, Robinson. 1 W. Watson 1 G. Clare 2 J. Pomfrey 0 D. Clibborn 0</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>ENGLISH CHAMPIONSHIP TOURNEY.</head>
        <p>ENGLISH CHAMPIONSHIP TOURNEY.</p>
        <p>The following t-a-ble; give the complete results of the tourney for the draughts championship of England, played at the Coal Exchange, Manchester during Easter week.</p>
        <p>Herbert IVlorrall, of Liverpool. took first prize, £2O and a sterling silver medallion ; and will hold the Eng- lish Association Championship Cup for two years. William Blakeley, of Manchester, received second prize, £10; R. T. Ward, oi Birmingham, third £6; and R. Parry, of Manchester, fourth, £4. It will be observed that the two Warrington players Messrs. Dunne and Roberts were "knocked out" by the two chief prize winners, and that Mr. Dunne's score against the champion was a much better one than that made by the winner of the second prize in the final round.</p>
        <p>TABLE OF RESULTS. PRELIMINARY RO UND.</p>
