      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>GRAPPENHALL</head>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL</p>
        <p>YOUNG MEN'S CLUB AND GYMNASIUM.—A general meeting of the above was held on Friday the Bth inst. The chair v‘ as occupied by the Rector, the Rev. R. Greenall M.A. The chairman complimented the mem- bers on the work done in the gymnasium during the winter months and especially for their pat in connec- tion with the assault-at-arms recently given. He expressed his willingness to further any efforts next winter, which the members make, in again continuing the institution, and hoped for increased success. The accounts were then presented and passed. Votes of thanks were given to the treasurer and secretary. THE CRICKET CLUB.—As the cricket season is drawing nigh, practice games commence to-day, and, given fine weather, enthusiasts are likely .to put in an appearance on the cricket ground.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Rugby Football.</p>
        <p>By " Ovalist."</p>
        <p>The form of the Warrington men against Leeds on Saturday was closely watched in view of the semi-final with Bradford to-day. Nothing but re-assurance could be gained from the display of the homesters,who though minus Fish, defeated a strong fifteen by 9 points to 2 points. It was not what they did exactly but the way they did it that delighted Warrington's supporters. Forwards and backs alike were in grand trim and quite outclassed Leeds, who were in the running for the League championship. If there were any Bradford men on the look-out they must have been impressed with the difference in the displays given by the wirepullers against themselves on March 26th and against Leeds on Saturday.</p>
        <p>There was a strong wind blowing throughout the game, and when Leeds won the toss most people expected a very hard defensive forty minutes for Warrington. But the men inside the striped jerseys were not inclined to back down either for the wind or for Leeds. Instead they qiet about the visitors with surprising energy. The forwards got possession from a heavier pack far more frequently than usual, and with Davies and Hockenhall at the top of their form, Leeds were soon in danger. Their line had one or two escapes which could only be termed lucky, for the defence was pierced when a forward pass or some- thing of the kind robbed Warrington. There was plenty of enjoyable foltball, but no scoring until about 30 minutes had gone, when Dickenson snapped the ball up at centre, and after a strong run gave to Hockenhall for the latter to score a go3d try. Leeds came down again and kicked a penalty goal, but they were a point behind at the interval.</p>
        <p>The match seemed a certainty for Warrington when the teams crossed over. Leeds, however, kept the ball close and strove hard to work their way into the home quarter. Five minutes had only elapsed when Dickenson kicked a grand goal from near half-way, and though the visitors made one or two creditable efforts, Warrington were always masters of the situation. They utilised the wind better than their oppon nts had done, and by- and-by Harris followed up his own kick with such speed as to beat the Leeds full back and score far out. Dickenson kicked a goal from touch and the cheering was loud and long. It was clearly only a question of Warrington's majority, but the wind spoiled many a passing bout, and there was only one other score, Davies cleverly dodging over from a scrum ten yards from the line. The end came with Warrington winning by 2 goals 3 tries to 1 goal.</p>
        <p>Chief praise must be given to the work of the home half-backs. Davies and Hockenhall surprised even their admirers by the smartness, resource, and com- bination they displayed. Both "Philistine" and the " Impressionist " were really impressed by the play of the pair, Hockenhall in particular coming in for high praise. He has ousted Bate with a vengeance, and if Davies and he can perform as smartly against Bradford I have no fear as to the issue of the game. The Warrington back division was kept on the move last Saturday, and what that means most teams have reason to know. To score 13 points without Fish was a tribute to the effectiveness of the combination andin every departmentWarrington showed tip-top form. Kenyon was not given much opportunity but he filled Fish's plar9 with credit, while Thomas effected a welcome improvement in the work of the forwards.</p>
        <p>Leeds did not play badly,but they were outgeneralled and outpaced by the lighter Warrington men. Grace and Mosley worked hard behind the scrum while Llewellyn did excellent service at right centre. In fact his play stamped him as a class centre threequarter and he several times changed defence into attack with speedy running aid passing. The Headingley men were well-beaten, and the try to nil defeat earlier in the season was handsomely atoned for.</p>
        <p>A number of league matches have been played during the week, Bradford defeating Batley, and Salford winning at Runcorn. As the Reds lost at Hull on Satur day they now tie with Bradford for top place.</p>
        <p>Broughton Rangers sent a weak team to Hull on Thursday and were heavily defeated, the score read- ing: Hull Kingston Rovers, 3 goals 5 tries, (21 points); Rangers, 1 try, (3 points.)</p>
        <p>The League table up-to-date is as follows : For. Against. P. W. L. D. G. T. P. Gt. T. P. Pts Salford 32-23..,. 7... 2...51 73-321...15 23-99...48 Bradford Hunslet 31...22... 8... 1...47 49-241...17 32-130..45 Br'ghton R 31...19... 8... 4...30 76-288...28 27-137..42 Leeds 31..18. 8... 5...24 43-177...16 30-122..41 Oldham Hull Kl'n R 32...16...14... 2...27 41-178...19 39-123..34 Warrington 29...15...11... 3...26 43-181...21 23-111..33 Halifax 31...13...15... 3...22 22-110...27 25-129..29 Swinton 32-11-17_ 4...19 28-122-26 45-187..26 Hull Widnes 32 .10..17.. 5...19 24-110-28 59-233..25 Wigan Runcorn Batley 32...10..J9... 3...25 27-131...45 49-240..23 Leigh Keighlev 32... 7...20... 5...30 18-114_44 66-286..19 Hudderiflld3o,.. 8...22... 0...15 36-138-47 63-283..16</p>
        <p>In the South West Lancashire League, Leigh having beaten Runcorn by 4 points to 3, the table up-to- date is as follows :-- _ _</p>
        <p>_ P. W. L. D. For. Agst. Pts. Warrington 6 ... 5 ... 0 ... 1 61-17 ... 10 Wigan *St. Helens Runcorn 4.4eigh 9 ... 2 ... 4 ... 2 26-49 6 Widnes *St. Helens matches, not being home and away en. gagements, each count 4 points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>Semi-Final.</head>
        <p>The Semi-Final.</p>
        <p>„ • .</p>
        <p>What are Warrington's chances to-day? That is the question of the hour. Let us discuss them frankly. There is no doubt that Warrington are meeting one of the strongest teams in the union and he is a bold man who will back them to win the tie. Still I think War- rington have an excellent chance of success. They certainly possess the advantage of ground, and being the only Lancashire Club left in the Cup Competition they will doubtless receive most of the crowd's encour- agement, and this is not to be depised by any means. Then the team itself is performing in a very satisfac- tory manner just now, and a continuance of recent form must, I believe, spell success. They have only one thing to fear on the Bradford side, and that is weight—which counts for a lot: Still Warrington's deficiency in this respect may be less apparent than real, especially under certain conditions.</p>
        <p>The conditions we are all hoping for are fine weather and a dry ground. Granted this much and Warring- ton should be able to set a pace which their heavier opponents will have a difficulty in following. A light ball means a lot to Warrington, who are not power- ful kickers, and who rely largely on combination. I feel convinced that we can beat Bradford under such conditions, but I shall be very dubious should the weather be wet. Even then, Warrington will take a lot of beating. A fine day and a fair field and no favour is all Warrington want, however. Given this much, there should be a grand gate, and Warrington winners!</p>
        <p>The Warrington team should be quite at full strength with every man fit and well. Hockenhall, as expected,after his brilliant performances of late, has obtained the preference over Bate, and will partner Davies behind the scrummage. Much depends on ' these two men. They will need to keep a sharp eve— and very ofteA both hands—on Bradford's Inter- national half back, Marsden, and they will have to be smart in their movements if the Lancashire three- quarters are to be effective. Warrington should have a slight pull in the third line, but the Bradford quartette even without Eagers are not to be sneered at. At full back there will be two good men operating, for Gunn, the Bradford custodian is equal to Hallam. It will not be much (-rood to kick to him unless he gets a bad attack of stage fright, which s unlikely.</p>
        <p>A fine crowd of Warringtonians may be expected at Broughton. Four, and possibly five, specials will be in readiness to convey the locals to Manchester, and three or four thousands of them ought to be able to give a good rendering of the inspiring Wilderspool whisper.</p>
        <p>The Warrington fifteen, with the latest weights, is as follows :</p>
        <p>sts. lbs. Hallam, full back Fish Isherwood I 10 7 Dickenson rthree-quarters Harris Davies I half- lO 6 Hockenhall f backs Thomas \ Morrison ' Boardman Jolley 13 6 Forwards Naylor Cook Lunt Edmondson / Warrington have no less than five league matches to get through in the next fortnight. Leigh will be met at Leigh on Monday at 6 o'clock, and Wigan on Wednesday at 545, while the Runcorn match will be played on Friday or Monday, and Huddersfield away on Wednesday, three days before the final. Salford at Warrington will of course be played on Saturday next.</p>
        <p>BRADFORD VIEWS. [FROSI A BRADFORD CORRESPONDENT.]</p>
        <p>Critics in Yorkshire, or rather one critic, has prophesied unmistakable success for Bradford at Broughton on Saturday, but in reality, the club fully realise the toughness of the struggle and the uncer- tainty of the issue. It would be folly, in fact, for them to think otherwise, and it is no mean compliment to the cup-fighting prowess of Isherwood, Fish and Co., that they should be regarded as a stiffer obstacle than the Broughton Rangers. No; the Warrington men would do well not to rely upon any over- bearing confidence on the part of Brad- ford. They have learnt by experience, and even their success at Headingley last week has not turned their heads, surprising and complete as it was. The fact is, after the Rangers' defence was first pierced they gave up the ghost, and their ultimate break-up was due to want of heart, besides which they were thoroughly fagged. It is felt that such an advantage will not be with them against War- rington and that the defence will be surer.</p>
        <p>The Warrington forwards again, are calculated to make a better stand and will last better than the Broughton front rank, coupled with which is the well- known fact that the teani is splendidly compact and at a push can quickly find the weak spot in an oppon- ent's armour.</p>
        <p>Now, turning to Bradford's qualities,which will in- terest Warrington readers most,Gunn is an ideal back, and it will be useless to suppose that incessant kick- ing will tire him. He was one of the heroes of the match with the Rangers and showed himself a daring stopper. His diminutive stature is quite a contradic- tion to all his qualities. Then the Bradford third line is more efficient and resourceful than has been the case for many years, and here the bulk of the reliance will be placed, for it is a singular fact that all the scoring against Broughton was the result of amazing swift- ness and exactitude in three-quarter passing—the quick hand-to-hand passing especially. The ball changed hands quite half-a-dozen times before the first try was scored, and Warrington will have to collar like de- mons to keep their line intact. This transformation of tactics on the part of a Torkshire team, and especially of Bradford is quite noteworthy, for in past years Bradford have relied mainly upon headlong forward rushes, in which,truth'to tell, Broadley's men were reckless and most times fortunate. But all that is changed now. The forwards pay more atten- tic).* to the finesse of scrimmaging, heel with prompti- tude if they can hold their opponents, and set their back machinery a-foot as quickly as possible. This too is Warringtoa's forte, and it is possible that being older at the job they may prove the more adept. Given fine weather, and good turf there ought to be an eKhibition of football at Lower Broughton far superior to that seen in any of the three memorable encounters from which Bradford have just successfully emerged.</p>
        <p>The Bradford team will not be finally selected until Saturday, but, meanwhile, it will be of interest to see the material upon which the visitors are able to draw :</p>
        <p>Age. Weight. Back: St. lbs. Gunn Threequarters :</p>
        <p>Heseltine Dechan Sinton 24 ... 11 2 Dunbavin Mushy 2G ... 13 0</p>
        <p>Halves: _ Marsden (capt) 23 ... 11 8 Surman 2l ... 11 0 B rear Forwards: Broadley 32 ... 15 0 Hutt 24 ... 16 2 Laidlaw 26 ... 14 8 Feather Rees 23 ... 13 5 Upton 25 ... 12 8 Turner 25 ... 13 4 Barker 25 ... 12 7 Sharratt 24 ... 12 7 F. S males</p>
        <p>These are the men from whom the team will be chosen. "Why is the selection left so late?" i a prominent 'official was asked. "You see," he replied. "We don't know who will be fit." This may not be all the truth, but nevertheless, the three cup encounters with Broughton gave Bradbrd rather a hard shaking.</p>
        <p>A comparison between the tables and weights given above shows at once what an auv.antage in physique the Bradfordians possess. Leaving out Heseltine,and Brear from the Bradford list the remaining fifteen are ten lbs per man heavier than the yVarrington team. All the more crcdit to Warrington if they win !</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>Cricket.</head>
        <p>Cricket.</p>
        <p>While football enthusiasts are all excitement about the cup ties,our local wielders of the willow enter upon another season. To-day the Warrington eleven enter- tain Sefton at Arpley, and the contest with these old rivals is being looked flrward to with interest. Warring- ton will be represented by the following team :—E. A. Connor (captain), NV. H. Podmore. J. Callan, J. Thomp- son, NV. M. Silcock, P. B. Hawtin, Woods, Übs- dell, Hampson, Wright and Lynes. The appoint- ment of Mr. Connor to the captaincy will be hailed with satisfaction. In him the club has long possessed a hard-working and capable member, and he has always taken the greatest interest in its progress. There are one or two changes in the eleven. Messrs B. Silcock and Briggs have left the town, and a new-corner, who is expected to render valuable nervice as a batsman and bowler, is Mr. Übsdell. Last season he played for Garston, and he has also figured in the Sefton eleven. Two recruits from the second eleven— Messrs. Woods and Wright—will appear in the senior organization to-day, and another promising cricketer who will be given an opportunity of display- ing his prowess during the season is NV. Richardson. Given fine weather, a successful season is anticipated especially if Lynes, the popular professional, maintains, his splendid form of last year. The following is a list of the fixtures;—</p>
        <p>FIRST TEAM.</p>
        <p>SECOND TEAM.</p>
        <p>April April 16 Sefton H 18 Sefton A. 23 South Manchester...H 23 South Manchester A 30 Bolton H 30 Bolton I A May May 7 Bootle A 7 Bootle H 14 Wigan A 14 Wigan H 21 St. Helens H2l St. Helens ....... A 28 Preston A 23 Ashton- under - LyneA 23 Ashton-under-Lyne... H June June</p>
        <p>45t. Helens A 4 St. Helens ......... H 11 Sale H 11 Sale A. 13. Manchester A 18 Earlestown El 18 Earlestown A. 25 Bolton A 25 Bolton H • July July 2 Levenshulme 11 2 Levenshulme A 9 South Manchester A 9 SouthManchesterH 14 Wigan H 16 Sefton A 16 Sefton H 23 Earlestown A 23 Earlestown H 30 Manchester H August August 6 Bootle H 6 Bootle A 13 Ashton-under-Lyne II 13 Ashton-under-Lyne A 20 _ ... .</p>
        <p>2:7 Sale A 27 Sale</p>
        <p>September September 3 Preston H 3 Preston A It will be noticed that August 20th is a vacant date. In order to encourage local talent it has been suggested that a match with members of junior organizations be held on that afternoon.</p>
        <p>Cycling. "Cancelled" had to be written across the Warrington Cycling Club's fixture for Saturday, none of the mem- bers venturing to brave the unsettled weather. To-day's run is to Peover, and the outing is gsilerally a very popular one. Should the elements be propitious, a good turnout is anticipated.</p>
        <p>On Monday we had u touch of real Spring. Brilliant</p>
        <p>sunshine. and a gentle, cooling breeze made cycling a pleasure, and the roads, too, were in fine condition. Th6re is a charming freshness about the country side just now, when the trees are bursting into leaf, and some of the trim village gardens are already bright with flowers. A spin along the old Cheshire lanes is indeed refreshing, after spending dull winter in a grimy manu— facturing town like Warrington.</p>
        <p>Residents in Cheshire are lona in their complaints of the "terrible dust "caused by the motors. A corres- pondent writing to a Manchester contemporary on• Wednesday stated :—lt is really only a few hours since we had heavy rains, and yet the roads are at the present as dusty as they possibly can be, and there can be no doubt that the many motor-cars now on the high- ways are to a large extent responsible for this. This "motor-dust" is really becoming a, most serious nuisance in the many residential districts of Cheshire, and it is a matter which is causing some anxiety to owners of property situated near those roads which are now so much used by the motorist. It seems not unlikely that the local author- ities will have to try in the near future to deal with the dust nuisance in some way. Yesterday's fine, weather brought out large numbers of cars, and thee roads presented that whiteand dirty appearance which we see in sultry August. The hedge-rows, trees, and the foliage and plants in private gardens were simply covered, and in some places presented a curious sight. This at the beginning of the season. What will it be when the season is at its height ?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Confirmation at St. James's, Latchford, The Bishop of Chester administered the rite of con- firmation to 74 candidates at St. James's Church,Latch- ford, on Thursday evening. Addresses were delivered,. before the laying on of hands, by the Rev. the Hon. C. F. Cross, Vicar of Stretton, and afterwards by the Bishop. Candidates were presented as follows :—St. James's, 53; Ashton, 7; Padgate, 1; Partington, 9 Runcorn, 2; Stretton, 1; Chester, 1.</p>
        <p>St. Peter's Church. On Monday evening, a service of song, entitled "Mother's last Words," was given by the Young Women's Bible Class in the John-street Schools. There was a crowded audience, presided over by the Rev. D. R. James. Mrs. Percival, teacher of the class, read the difficult poetical parts in an admirable way, sustaining the closest attention of all present. Mrs. Adams very kindly acted as accompanist, while the solos, duets and quartets were taken by various members of the class who did their work in a very creditable manner. The views were exhibited by Captain Langton and made the ser- vice more real and lifelike. The proceeds will be devoted. towards the cost of prizes for the class and any other necessary object it connection with the same.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society. The usual weekly meeting was held on Sunday after- noon, when Mr. A. Healy presided, and spoke upo gratitude to God for His sparing mercies. Mr. J. Watson, of Liverpool was the speaker, and the subject of his address was our relationship to God, and God's. care and thoughtfulness, to the children of men. Our finite minds, he said, could not fully understand God's laws of nature, and His great spiritual mind. But He had revealed Himself Ps the sinner's Friend,by the gift of' His son Jesus,and the gift of the Holy Ghost. The Secre- tary.lannounced that three new members had joined the Society. He gave them the usual greeting. The Secretary then referred to the fatal accident that had happened at Dallam Iron Works to Mr. George Wood- ward, of 14, Wilson-street, Warrington, who was once a member of the brotherhood. He moved that their deepest sympathy and condolence be sent to Mrs. Wood-, ward and her family in their dark hour of sorrow and bereavement.—Mr. Payton, Mus. Bac., presided at the organ, and rendered an organ solo.</p>
        <p>Services for the People. The Parr Hall was again crowded on Sunday evening, when the Rev. H. Dunnieo gave an address on "The Home : It's influence on the character of the individual and on the life of the nation." There was no more important subject in the whole world, he said,than the subject of horue.The management of the home was more important than .the management of the nation or even the Church of God, for if the home were right, the nation and the Church would also be right. Locality, wealth and position did not constitute home, but love, sympathy and conference ; and only where these things. existed could there be a true home in the highest and best sense of the word. A larpe amount of the unhappi- ness in home life was due to the fact that people. reserved winsomeness and trivial acts of courtesy and kindness for the outside world only, instead of showing the best and most genial side of their nature to the members of the home circle. The preacher advised fathers and mothers ever to cultivate the friendship of their children, and also spoke words of counsel to the voting people concerning marriage and early married' life.—ln closing, Mr. Dunnico urged all to help to ?aake their lives happy and bright, remembering that they were a faint symbol of that Home which God had pre— pared for those who loved him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-16_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>SEAWEED AS A MEDICINE.</head>
        <p>SEAWEED AS A MEDICINE.</p>
        <p>ITS MARVELLOUS EFFECT UPON THE STOMACH, LIVER, KIDNEYS AND BLOOD. SEA-WEED possesses a natural strengthening, healing, and purifyin gpower, infinitely greater than ordinary medicines. This is now regarded as conclusive by eminent authorities, who recommend it daily since Veno's Sea-weed Tonic was first introduced. A pure palatable extract of Sea-weed is one of the' principle ingredients of Veno's Sea-weed Tonic. This remedy excels in the cure of INDIGESTION, WIND, HEADACHE, NERVOUS COMPLAINTS, KIDNEY TROUBLES, WEAK AND PALNYUL BACK, TORPID LIVER, PE3IALE• TROUBLES, POORNESS OF BLOOD and" HABITUAL CON- TIPATION." Its most brilliant effect is produced in Stomach, Liver and Kidney Complaints. The remarkable. cures Veno's Seaweed Tonic effect even in the worst cases is positive proof of its therapeutic value. Ask for VENO'S SEA-WEED TONfC, is. lid. and 2s. 9d. per bottle, at Chemists and Drug Stores.</p>
        <p>Enginemen, Cranemen, and Boller Firemen. In connection with the above Men's Union, a special meeting was held at. the Guildhall, Runcorn, on tion- day which was well attended. The chair was taken., by an old Trade Unionist. Mr. G. Sambrook,organiser, of Sheffield, who is visiting the branches in the dis- • trict, gave a splendid address on trade unionism and its principles. He urged upon those present the necessity of combination and said there was .never a time in the history of trade unionism when engineers and firemen needed combination so much. He urged. the, necessity of their being practical men and joining this society. They thus got educated, which was a great advantage to the employer. Questions were put and suitable answers given. Several members were enrolled and a vote of thanks to the chairman and/ speaker closed the me?ting.</p>
